{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Marissa bought a ball at the store for $20. If she had $80 on her and used the rest of the money to buy her brother Jimmy, candy bars sold at $5 each, how many candy bars did Marissa buy for Jimmy?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "12", "rationale": "To solve this problem, let me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, Marissa bought a ball for $20. She had $80 initially. To find out how much money she has left after buying the ball, I subtract the cost of the ball from her total money.\n\n$80 (initial money) - $20 (cost of the ball) = $60\n\nNow, Marissa uses the remaining $60 to buy candy bars for her brother Jimmy. Each candy bar costs $5. To determine how many candy bars she can buy with $60, I divide the remaining money by the cost per candy bar.\n\n$60 (money left) \u00f7 $5 (cost per candy bar) = 12\n\nTherefore, Marissa bought 12 candy bars for her brother Jimmy.\n</think>\n\n**Solution:**\n\n1. **Initial Money Marissa Has:**\n   \\[\n   \\$80\n   \\]\n\n2. **Cost of the Ball:**\n   \\[\n   \\$20\n   \\]\n\n3. **Money Left After Buying the Ball:**\n   \\[\n   \\$80 - \\$20 = \\$60\n   \\]\n\n4. **Cost Per Candy Bar:**\n   \\[\n   \\$5\n   \\]\n\n5. **Number of Candy Bars Marissa Can Buy:**\n   \\[\n   \\$60 \\div \\$5 = 12\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\\[\n\\boxed{12}\n\\]", "pred_ans": "12", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Marissa bought a ball at the store for $20. If she had $80 on her and used the rest of the money to buy her brother Jimmy, candy bars sold at $5 each, how many candy bars did Marissa buy for Jimmy?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Steve loves playing video games.  His parents get him a console along with 5 games for his birthday.  He saves up enough money to buy 1 game per month for a year, and then the following year he starts buying 2 games a month.  For the third year he buys 4 games a month as he has a new part-time job that makes him more money.  He also gets 5 games for Christmas every year.  How many games does Steve have after 3 years?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "104", "rationale": "Okay, so Steve loves playing video games, and his parents got him a console with 5 games for his birthday. That\u2019s a good start! Now, he saves up enough money to buy 1 game per month for a year. Hmm, so that's 12 games in the first year. Then, the following year, he starts buying 2 games a month. Let me calculate that: 2 games times 12 months would be 24 games. \n\nAlright, so after two years, he\u2019s bought 12 + 24 = 36 games. But he also gets 5 games for Christmas every year. So, over two years, that's 5 games each year times 2 years, which is 10 games. Adding that to his purchases, he now has 36 + 10 = 46 games after two years.\n\nNow, moving on to the third year. He starts buying 4 games a month because of his new part-time job. So, 4 games times 12 months is 48 games. But wait, he also gets another 5 games for Christmas in the third year. So, adding that, 48 + 5 = 53 games.\n\nHold on, let me double-check my calculations. First year: 1 game a month for 12 months is 12 games. Second year: 2 games a month for 12 months is 24 games. Third year: 4 games a month for 12 months is 48 games. Then, each year he gets 5 games for Christmas. That's 5 games times 3 years, which is 15 games. So, adding all his purchases: 12 + 24 + 48 = 84 games. Then, adding the 15 games from Christmas, 84 + 15 = 99 games total.\n\nHmm, so that seems a bit more accurate. Let me break it down step by step again:\