les as Cal:\n   \\[\n   S = \\frac{1}{2}K\n   \\]\n3. Sean has 56 marbles:\n   \\[\n   S = 56\n   \\]\n\nFirst, we find the number of marbles Cal has:\n\\[\nS = \\frac{1}{2}K \\implies 56 = \\frac{1}{2}K \\implies K = 56 \\times 2 = 112\n\\]\n\nNext, we find the number of marbles Carl has:\n\\[\nC = 4S \\implies C = 4 \\times 56 = 224\n\\]\n\nFinally, we calculate the combined total of Carl and Cal's marbles:\n\\[\nC + K = 224 + 112 = 336\n\\]\n\nThe combined number of marbles Carl and Cal have is \\(\\boxed{336}\\).", "pred_ans": "336", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Carl has four times as many marbles as Sean and Sean has half as many marbles as Cal. If Sean has 56 marbles, how many marbles do Carl and Cal have combined?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: A bakery has 40 less than seven times as many loaves of bread as Sam had last Friday. If Sam had seventy loaves of bread last Friday, how many loaves of bread does the bakery have?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "450", "rationale": "Okay, so I have this math problem here, and I need to figure it out step by step. Let me read it again to make sure I understand.\n\n\"A bakery has 40 less than seven times as many loaves of bread as Sam had last Friday. If Sam had seventy loaves of bread last Friday, how many loaves of bread does the bakery have?\"\n\nAlright, so Sam had 70 loaves last Friday. The bakery has 40 less than seven times that number. So, first, I need to find out what seven times 70 is, and then subtract 40 from that to get the number of loaves the bakery has.\n\nLet me break it down:\n\n1. **Find seven times Sam's loaves.**\n   - Sam had 70 loaves.\n   - So, 7 times 70 is... Hmm, 7 times 70. Let me calculate that.\n\n2. **Calculate 7 times 70.**\n   - 70 times 7. Well, 7 times 70 is 490. Because 70 times 7 is the same as 7 times 70, which is 490.\n\n3. **Subtract 40 from that number.**\n   - So, 490 minus 40 is... Let me do that. 490 minus 40 is 450.\n\n4. **So, the bakery has 450 loaves.**\n   - Wait, let me double-check to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nFirst, Sam had 70 loaves. Seven times 70 is indeed 490 because 70 times 7 is 490. Then, subtracting 40 from 490 gives 450. Yep, that seems right.\n\nI think that's all. So, the bakery has 450 loaves of bread.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe bakery has \\boxed{450} loaves of bread.\n</think>\n\nSam had 70 loaves of bread last Friday. The bakery has 40 less than seven times the number of loaves Sam had.\n\n1. Calculate seven times 70:\n   \\[\n   7 \\times 70 = 490\n   \\]\n\n2. Subtract 40 from 490:\n   \\[\n   490 - 40 = 450\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the bakery has \\boxed{450} loaves of bread.", "pr