ula for each leg and then adding/subtracting appropriately.\n\nYeah, so I think that's it. The distance is 374 miles.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe distance between the bird's northern and southern homes is \\boxed{374} miles.\n</think>\n\nThe bird starts by flying south for 10 hours at 30 miles per hour, covering a distance of:\n\\[ 30 \\, \\text{mph} \\times 10 \\, \\text{hours} = 300 \\, \\text{miles} \\]\n\nNext, the bird flies north for 2 hours at 18 miles per hour, covering a distance of:\n\\[ 18 \\, \\text{mph} \\times 2 \\, \\text{hours} = 36 \\, \\text{miles} \\]\n\nThen, the bird flies south again for 5 hours at 22 miles per hour, covering a distance of:\n\\[ 22 \\, \\text{mph} \\times 5 \\, \\text{hours} = 110 \\, \\text{miles} \\]\n\nCalculating the net displacement:\n- After the first leg, the bird is 300 miles south.\n- After the second leg, the bird is \\(300 - 36 = 264\\) miles south.\n- After the third leg, the bird is \\(264 + 110 = 374\\) miles south.\n\nThus, the distance between the bird's northern and southern homes is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{374}\n\\]", "pred_ans": "374", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: A bird decides to leave her summer home in the north and travel to her winter home in the south.  To make the journey, the bird leaves her home flying in a southerly direction for 10 hours at a speed of 30 miles per hour.  Then, the bird turns direction and flies towards the north for 2 hours are a speed of 18 miles per hour.  Finally, the bird changes direction and flies toward the south for 5 hours at a speed of 22 miles per hour before landing at her southern home.  What is the distance, in miles, between the bird's northern and the southern homes?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Billy is peeling and cutting potatoes in preparation for making potato salad for his big family reunion barbecue. It's a big event, so he has 60 potatoes to get through. The potatoes are roughly the same size, so it takes about the same amount of time to peel and cut each one. It takes Billy about a minute and a half to peel a potato, but only about 5 seconds to cut it up. How long will it take Billy to finish prepping the potatoes?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "95", "rationale": "Okay, so Billy has 60 potatoes to peel and cut for his family reunion barbecue. He wants to make potato salad, and he's got a bunch of potatoes, so he needs to get through all of them. They\u2019re all roughly the same size, which is good because it means the time it takes to peel and cut each one should be pretty consistent. \n\nLet me break this down. First, it takes Billy about a minute and a half to peel a potato. Hmm, a minute and a half is 1.5 minutes. But since we're dealing with time, it's often easier to convert everything into the same unit. So, 1.5 minutes is the same as 90 seconds because 1 minute is 60 seconds, and 30 seconds more makes 90 seconds.\n\nThen, cutting each potato only takes about 5 seconds. That seems really fast, but maybe if you're experienced, you can do that quickly. So, we have two tasks here: peeling and cutting. \n\nNow, I need to figure out how long it will take Billy to finish prepping all the potatoes. So, I guess I need to calculate the total time for all the peeling and the total time for all the cutting, and then add them together because these are sequential tasks\u2014he can\u2019t peel a potato while he's cutting another one, right? So, it's a series of actions.\n\nLet me think about how to calculate the total peeling time first. If it takes 90 seconds per potato to peel, and he has 60 potatoes, I can multiply 90 seconds by 60 potatoes. Let me do that math:\n\n90 seconds/potato * 60 potatoes = 5,400 seconds.\n\nThat's for peeling all the potatoes. But 5,400 seconds seems like a lot. Maybe I should convert that back into minutes to make it more understandable. Since 60 seconds is a minute, I can divide 5,400 seconds by 60 to get the total peeling time in minutes.\n\n5,400 seconds \u00f7 60 seconds/minute = 90 minutes.\n\nWhoa, so peeling all 60 potatoes will take 90 minutes? That seems like a long time. Is that right? Let me double-check. If one potato takes 1.5 minutes, then 60 potatoes would take 60 * 1.5, which is 90 minutes. Yeah, that's correct.\n\nOkay, so moving on to the cutting part. Each potato only takes 5 seconds to cut, and he has 60 potatoes. So, I can calculate the total cutting time as:\n\n5 seconds/potato * 60 potatoes = 300 seconds.\n\nAgain, converting that into minutes for clarity:\n\n300 seconds \u00f7 60 seconds/minute = 5 minutes.\n\nSo, cutting all the potatoes will take 5 minutes. That seems manageable.\n\nNow, to find the total time it will take Billy to finish prepping all the potatoes, I need to add the peeling time and the cutting time together because he can\u2019t do both at the same time. So:\n\nTotal time = peeling time + cutting time\nTotal time = 90 minutes + 5 minutes = 95 minutes.\n\nSo, according to this, Billy will take 95 minutes to peel and cut all 60 potatoes.\n\nWait, is there a more efficient way to do this? Like, maybe he can peel and cut a potato at the same time? But the problem says it takes about the same amount of time to peel and cut each one. So, does that mean that the peeling and cutting are separate tasks that can't be done simultaneously?\n\nHmm, the problem says, \"it takes about the same amount of time to peel and cut each one.\" So, maybe the peeling and cutting are two separate actions that take roughly the same time per potato, but he can't do both at the same time. So, he has to peel one potato, then cut it, then maybe peel another one, and so on.\n\nSo, that would mean that for each potato, the total time is peeling time plus cutting time. So, per potato, it's 90 seconds + 5 seconds = 95 seconds per potato.\n\nWait, that's different from what I thought before. Initially, I thought he can do peeling and cutting simultaneously, but now I'm not sure. Let me read the problem again: \"it takes about the same amount of time to peel and cut each one.\" So, it might mean that both tasks take the same amount of time per potato, implying that he has to do them one after another.\n\nWait, but 90 seconds to peel and 5 seconds to cut seems like a big difference. So, if it takes 90 seconds to peel and 5 seconds to cut, he can\u2019t do them at the same time. So, for each potato, he has to spend 95 seconds in total.\n\nSo, if each potato takes 95 seconds, and he has 60 potatoes, the total time would be 95 seconds * 60 = 5,700 seconds.\n\nConverting that to minutes: 5,700 seconds \u00f7 60 = 95 minutes, same as before.\n\nSo, either way, whether I add the total peeling time and total cutting time or calculate per potato and then multiply, I get the same total time of 95 minutes.\n\nTherefore, the total time it will take Billy to finish prepping the potatoes is 95 minutes.\n\nBut just to make sure, let me think if there's another way to interpret the problem. It says, \"it takes about the same amount of time to peel and cut each one.\" So, maybe he can do peeling and cutting at the same time for one potato. So, if it takes him 90 seconds to peel and 5 seconds to cut, he can't do both at the same time because they are sequential actions. So, actually, each potato takes 90 seconds to peel and then 5 seconds to cut, so 95 seconds per potato.\n\nTherefore, total time is 60 * 95 seconds, which is 5,700 seconds, which is 95 minutes. That seems right.\n\nAlternatively, if he can peel and cut simultaneously, but that seems unlikely because peeling and cutting are two different tasks. So, he can't peel and cut at the same time. So, either way, the total time is 95 minutes.\n\nWait, another thought: maybe the time to peel and cut is the same as cutting and peeling. So, perhaps he can alternate tasks? But I don't think that changes the total time because he still has to do all the peels and all the cuts.\n\nTherefore, I think 95 minutes is the correct answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nIt will take Billy a total of \\boxed{95} minutes to finish prepping the potatoes.\n</think>\n\nBilly has 60 potatoes to peel and cut for his family reunion barbecue. Each potato takes 1 minute and 30 seconds (90 seconds) to peel and 5 seconds to cut. \n\nFirst, calculate the total peeling time:\n- Peeling time per potato: 90 seconds\n- Total peeling time for 60 potatoes: \\( 90 \\text{ seconds/potato} \\times 60 \\text{ potatoes} = 5400 \\text{ seconds} \\)\n- Convert seconds to minutes: \\( 5400 \\text{ seconds} \\div 60 = 90 \\text{ minutes} \\)\n\nNext, calculate the total cutting time:\n- Cutting time per potato: 5 seconds\n- Total cutting time for 60 potatoes: \\( 5 \\text{ seconds/potato} \\times 60 \\text{ potatoes} = 300 \\text{ seconds} \\)\n- Convert seconds to minutes: \\( 300 \\text{ seconds} \\div 60 = 5 \\text{ minutes} \\)\n\nAdd the total peeling and cutting times together:\n- Total time: \\( 90 \\text{ minutes} + 5 \\text{ minutes} = 90 + 5 = 95 \\text{ minutes} \\)\n\nThus, it will take Billy a total of \\boxed{95} minutes to finish prepping the potatoes.", "pred_ans": "95", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Billy is peeling and cutting potatoes in preparation for making potato salad for his big family reunion barbecue. It's a big event, so he has 60 potatoes to get through. The potatoes are roughly the same size, so it takes about the same amount of time to peel and cut each one. It takes Billy about a minute and a half to peel a potato, but only about 5 seconds to cut it up. How long will it take Billy to finish prepping the potatoes?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
