{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Jerry is twice as old as he was 5 years ago. How old will Jerry be in 3 years?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "13", "rationale": "Okay, so I need to figure out how old Jerry will be in 3 years. The information given is that Jerry is twice as old as he was 5 years ago. Hmm, let me break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let me assign a variable to Jerry's current age. I'll call it J. So, J represents Jerry's current age. The problem says Jerry is twice as old as he was 5 years ago. That means if I take his age from 5 years ago and multiply it by two, I get his current age. \n\nSo, five years ago, Jerry was J - 5 years old. According to the problem, Jerry is twice that age now. So, mathematically, that would be 2 times (J - 5). So, I can write the equation:\n\nJ = 2 * (J - 5)\n\nAlright, let me solve this equation to find J. Expanding the right side first, I get:\n\nJ = 2J - 10\n\nHmm, now I have J on both sides. I need to get all the J terms on one side to solve for it. Let me subtract J from both sides:\n\nJ - J = 2J - 10 - J\n\nWhich simplifies to:\n\n0 = J - 10\n\nSo, J = 10.\n\nWait, that seems straightforward. So, Jerry is currently 10 years old. The question asks how old Jerry will be in 3 years. So, I need to add 3 to his current age.\n\nSo, 10 + 3 = 13.\n\nTherefore, Jerry will be 13 years old in 3 years.\n\nLet me double-check to make sure I did everything correctly. If Jerry is 10 now, five years ago he was 5. Is 10 twice that? Yes, because 2 times 5 is 10. That checks out. So, adding 3 years to 10 gives 13. That makes sense.\n\nI think that's it. So, I'm confident that Jerry will be 13 years old in 3 years.\n\n**Final Answer**\nJerry will be \\boxed{13} years old in 3 years.\n</think>\n\nLet \\( J \\) represent Jerry's current age. The problem states that Jerry is twice as old as he was 5 years ago. This can be written as:\n\n\\[ J = 2(J - 5) \\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying the equation:\n\n\\[ J = 2J - 10 \\]\n\\[ J - 2J = -10 \\]\n\\[ -J = -10 \\]\n\\[ J = 10 \\]\n\nJerry is currently 10 years old. To find out how old Jerry will be in 3 years, we add 3 to his current age:\n\n\\[ 10 + 3 = 13 \\]\n\nTherefore, Jerry will be \\boxed{13} years old in 3 years.", "pred_ans": "13", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Jerry is twice as old as he was 5 years ago. How old will Jerry be in 3 years?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
