o try the escape room. Now they have to be divided into groups of 8, each group taking 45 minutes. So, how many groups do they need? Let's see.\n\n40 divided by 8 is 5. So, 5 groups of 8 students each. Each group takes 45 minutes. So, would that mean 5 x 45 minutes? Let me compute that.\n\n5 x 45 is 225 minutes. But wait, that seems like a lot. Is there something else I need to consider here?\n\nWait, the problem says every group uses their full 45 minutes. So, each session is exactly 45 minutes, and each group can't be faster. So, they can't be done in less time. Therefore, to have all 40 students go through, we need 5 groups, each taking 45 minutes. So, the total time is 5 x 45 minutes.\n\nLet me compute 5 x 45. 45 + 45 is 90, plus another 45 is 135, plus another 45 is 180, plus another 45 is 225 minutes. So, 225 minutes is the total time needed.\n\nBut wait, the question is, how long will it take for everyone to try the escape room? So, that would be 225 minutes. But let me make sure I didn't miss anything.\n\nWait, does the escape room time include the setup and the time between groups? The problem says every group uses their full 45 minutes. So, I think each group's 45 minutes is the time they are actually trying to escape. So, if the next group comes in immediately after the previous one finishes, then the total time would be 45 minutes multiplied by the number of groups.\n\nSo, if 5 groups are needed, each taking 45 minutes, the total time is 225 minutes.\n\nBut maybe I should convert that into hours or days? The problem doesn't specify, so probably minutes is fine.\n\nWait, let me double-check: 5 groups of 8 students each, 8x5=40 students. Each group takes 45 minutes. So, 5x45=225 minutes.\n\nAlternatively, 225 minutes is equal to 3 hours and 45 minutes, because 60 minutes in an hour. So, 225 divided by 60 is 3.75 hours, which is 3 hours and 45 minutes.\n\nBut the problem doesn't specify the unit of time, so it's safer to use minutes unless specified otherwi