lenged to earn as many Accelerated Reader points as they can. The 10 students in each grade with the most points get to try an escape room set up by the teachers. Only 8 students can try the escape room at a time. They have 45 minutes to try and escape. If every group uses their full 45 minutes, how long will it take for everyone to try the escape room?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Tiffany is measuring how many surfers can ride a big wave without falling. She sees that when a wave over 30 feet arrives, only 25% of the riders can stay upright. Of these riders, 60% are women. If there are 100 riders, how many men can stay upright on the wave?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "10", "rationale": "Okay, let me try to figure out how to solve this problem. So, Tiffany is measuring how many surfers can ride a big wave without falling. When a wave over 30 feet arrives, only 25% of the riders can stay upright. Among these riders, 60% are women. There are 100 riders in total, and we need to find out how many men can stay upright on the wave.\n\nFirst, I'll break down the information given:\n\n1. Total riders: 100.\n2. When a wave over 30 feet arrives, only 25% can stay upright. So, the number of riders who can stay upright is 25% of 100.\n3. Among these 25% who can stay upright, 60% are women.\n\nWe need to find the number of men who can stay upright. Hmm, okay, so let's see. Since 60% of the upright riders are women, the rest must be men. So, maybe I can find the total number of upright riders first and then figure out how many of them are men.\n\nLet me write down the steps:\n\nStep 1: Calculate the number of riders who can stay upright.\n\nTotal riders = 100.\n\nOnly 25% can stay upright. So, I need to calculate 25% of 100.\n\n25% is the same as 0.25 in decimal. So, 0.25 * 100 = 25.\n\nSo, 25 riders can stay upright.\n\nStep 2: Among these 25 riders, 60% are women. Therefore, the remaining percentage must be men.\n\nSo, 60% of 25 are women, and 40% of 25 are men.\n\nWait, why 40%? Because 100% - 60% = 40%.\n\nSo, let me compute that.\n\n60% of 25: 0.60 * 25. Hmm, 0.60 * 25.\n\nWell, 0.6 * 25. 0.6 * 20 is 12, and 0.6 * 5 is 3, so total is 15. So, 15 women can stay upright.\n\nThen, 25 total upright riders minus 15 women gives 10 men.\n\nSo, 10 