{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: I am three years younger than my brother, and I am 2 years older than my sister. My mom's age is one less than three times my brother's age. When you add all our ages, you get 87. How old am I?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "13", "rationale": "Alright, so I have this math problem to solve, and I need to figure out how old I am. Let me read the problem again and try to break it down step by step.\n\nThe problem says:\n\"I am three years younger than my brother, and I am 2 years older than my sister. My mom's age is one less than three times my brother's age. When you add all our ages, you get 87. How old am I?\"\n\nHmm, okay. So, let's parse this information. It mentions several people: me, my brother, my sister, and my mom. So, four people in total. Let me assign variables to each of our ages to make it easier. Variables can help translate the words into equations.\n\nLet me denote:\n- Let my age be \\( x \\).\n- Let my brother's age be \\( b \\).\n- Let my sister's age be \\( s \\).\n- Let my mom's age be \\( m \\).\n\nNow, let's translate the given information into equations.\n\n1. \"I am three years younger than my brother.\" So, my age is the brother's age minus 3. So, \\( x = b - 3 \\). I can write that as Equation 1: \\( x = b - 3 \\).\n\n2. \"I am 2 years older than my sister.\" So, my age is the sister's age plus 2. That would be \\( x = s + 2 \\). That's Equation 2: \\( x = s + 2 \\).\n\n3. \"My mom's age is one less than three times my brother's age.\" So, my mom's age is 3 times my brother's age minus 1. So, \\( m = 3b - 1 \\). That's Equation 3: \\( m = 3b - 1 \\).\n\n4. \"When you add all our ages, you get 87.\" So, the sum of my age, my brother's age, my sister's age, and my mom's age is 87. So, \\( x + b + s + m = 87 \\). That's Equation 4: \\( x + b + s + m = 87 \\).\n\nOkay, so now I have four equations:\n\n1. \\( x = b - 3 \\)  [Equation 1]\n2. \\( x = s + 2 \\)  [Equation 2]\n3. \\( m = 3b - 1 \\) [Equation 3]\n4. \\( x + b + s + m = 87 \\) [Equation 4]\n\nNow, I need to solve these equations to find the value of \\( x \\), which is my age.\n\nLet me see how I can substitute these equations to find \\( x \\).\n\nFirst, I can use Equations 1 and 2 to relate my age to the sister's age.\n\nFrom Equation 1: \\( x = b - 3 \\)\n\nFrom Equation 2: \\( x = s + 2 \\)\n\nSince both equal \\( x \\), they equal each other:\n\n\\( b - 3 = s + 2 \\)\n\nLet me solve for \\( s \\) in terms of \\( b \\).\n\nSubtract 2 from both sides:\n\n\\( b - 3 - 2 = s \\)\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\( b - 5 = s \\)\n\nSo, \\( s = b - 5 \\). Let's call this Equation 5: \\( s = b - 5 \\).\n\nSo now, the sister's age is \\( b - 5 \\).\n\nNow, let's look at Equation 4: \\( x + b + s + m = 87 \\).\n\nWe can substitute \\( x \\) and \\( s \\) in terms of \\( b \\) and \\( m \\) in terms of \\( b \\) as well.\n\nFrom Equation 1: \\( x = b - 3 \\)\n\nFrom Equation 5: \\( s = b - 5 \\)\n\nFrom Equation 3: \\( m = 3b - 1 \\)\n\nSo substituting into Equation 4:\n\n\\( (b - 3) + b + (b - 5) + (3b - 1) = 87 \\)\n\nNow, let's simplify this equation step by step.\n\nFirst, let's write out all the terms:\n\n\\( (b - 3) + b + (b - 5) + (3b - 1) \\)\n\nCombine like terms. Let's first remove the parentheses:\n\n\\( b - 3 + b + b - 5 + 3b - 1 \\)\n\nNow, let's combine all the \\( b \\) terms:\n\n\\( b + b + b + 3b = 6b \\)\n\nNow, the constant terms:\n\n\\( -3 -5 -1 = -9 \\)\n\nSo, the equation simplifies to:\n\n\\( 6b - 9 = 87 \\)\n\nNow, solve for \\( b \\):\n\nAdd 9 to both sides:\n\n\\( 6b = 87 + 9 \\)\n\n\\( 6b = 96 \\)\n\nDivide both sides by 6:\n\n\\( b = 96 / 6 \\)\n\n\\( b = 16 \\)\n\nSo, my brother is 16 years old.\n\nNow, let's find my age \\( x \\).\n\nFrom Equation 1: \\( x = b - 3 = 16 - 3 = 13 \\). So, I am 13 years old.\n\nLet me double-check to make sure this is consistent with the other equations.\n\nFirst, my sister's age \\( s = b - 5 = 16 - 5 = 11 \\). So, sister is 11.\n\nMy mom's age \\( m = 3b - 1 = 3*16 -1 = 48 -1 = 47 \\). So, mom is 47.\n\nNow, let's add up all our ages:\n\nMy age: 13\n\nBrother: 16\n\nSister: 11\n\nMom: 47\n\nTotal: 13 + 16 + 11 + 47\n\nLet me compute that:\n\n13 + 16 = 29\n\n29 + 11 = 40\n\n40 + 47 = 87\n\nYes, that adds up to 87, which matches the given information.\n\nSo, all the conditions are satisfied.\n\nTherefore, my age is 13.\n\n**Final Answer**\nI am \\boxed{13} years old.\n</think>\n\nLet me denote:\n- My age as \\( x \\)\n- My brother's age as \\( b \\)\n- My sister's age as \\( s \\)\n- My mom's age as \\( m \\)\n\nFrom the problem, we have the following information:\n1. I am three years younger than my brother: \\( x = b - 3 \\)\n2. I am 2 years older than my sister: \\( x = s + 2 \\)\n3. My mom's age is one less than three times my brother's age: \\( m = 3b - 1 \\)\n4. The sum of all our ages is 87: \\( x + b + s + m = 87 \\)\n\nFirst, we relate my age to my brother's and sister's ages:\n- From \\( x = b - 3 \\) and \\( x = s + 2 \\), we get \\( b - 3 = s + 2 \\). Solving for \\( s \\), we find \\( s = b - 5 \\).\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for \\( x \\), \\( s \\), and \\( m \\) in terms of \\( b \\) into the equation \\( x + b + s + m = 87 \\):\n- \\( x = b - 3 \\)\n- \\( s = b - 5 \\)\n- \\( m = 3b - 1 \\)\n\nSubstituting these into the equation:\n\\[ (b - 3) + b + (b - 5) + (3b - 1) = 87 \\]\n\nSimplifying the equation:\n\\[ b - 3 + b + b - 5 + 3b - 1 = 87 \\]\n\\[ 6b - 9 = 87 \\]\n\nSolving for \\( b \\):\n\\[ 6b = 96 \\]\n\\[ b = 16 \\]\n\nUsing \\( b = 16 \\) to find my age:\n\\[ x = b - 3 = 16 - 3 = 13 \\]\n\nThus, I am 13 years old.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{13}\n\\]", "pred_ans": "13", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: I am three years younger than my brother, and I am 2 years older than my sister. My mom's age is one less than three times my brother's age. When you add all our ages, you get 87. How old am I?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
