{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: To kick off summer, Jolene is having a water balloon fight with her friends.  They fill up 10 packs of balloons that have 30 balloons per pack.  By the end of the afternoon, 12 balloons are left.  How many balloons did they throw?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "288", "rationale": "Okay, so I've got this problem about Jolene and her friends having a water balloon fight. They filled up 10 packs of balloons, each pack having 30 balloons. By the end of the afternoon, 12 balloons are left. I need to figure out how many balloons they threw. Hmm, let's break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, I think I should figure out the total number of balloons they had initially. They have 10 packs, and each pack has 30 balloons. So, to find the total number, I can multiply the number of packs by the number of balloons per pack. That makes sense because each pack contributes 30 balloons. So, 10 packs multiplied by 30 balloons per pack. Let me write that out:\n\nTotal balloons = Number of packs \u00d7 Balloons per pack\nTotal balloons = 10 \u00d7 30\n\nHmm, 10 times 30 is 300. So, they started with 300 balloons.\n\nNow, the problem says that by the end of the afternoon, 12 balloons are left. That means they threw out some balloons. I need to find out how many they threw. So, if they started with 300 and ended with 12, the number of balloons thrown would be the total minus the remaining. Let me write that:\n\nBalloons thrown = Total balloons \u2212 Balloons left\nBalloons thrown = 300 \u2212 12\n\nOkay, 300 minus 12 is 288. So, they threw out 288 balloons.\n\nWait, just to make sure I didn't make any mistakes. Let me double-check my calculations.\n\nFirst, 10 packs with 30 balloons each: 10 \u00d7 30. 10 times 30 is definitely 300. Then, subtract the remaining 12. 300 minus 12 is 288. Yeah, that seems right.\n\nIs there another way to think about this problem? Maybe, but I think this straightforward approach is enough. They filled 10 packs, so 300 balloons. They ended up with 12, so subtracting gives the thrown ones.\n\nAlternatively, I could think about how many balloons were left after each person threw some, but that might complicate things because we don't know how many people there were or how many each person threw. The problem doesn't specify that, so the total thrown is just the total minus the leftover.\n\nSo, yeah, I think 288 is the correct answer. I can't see any errors in my reasoning, so I feel confident about this.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of balloons thrown is \\boxed{288}.\n</think>\n\nJolene and her friends filled 10 packs of balloons, each containing 30 balloons. Therefore, the total number of balloons they had initially is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total balloons} = 10 \\times 30 = 300\n\\]\n\nBy the end of the afternoon, 12 balloons were left. The number of balloons thrown is the total number of balloons minus the remaining balloons:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Balloons thrown} = 300 - 12 = 288\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of balloons thrown is \\boxed{288}.", "pred_ans": "288", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: To kick off summer, Jolene is having a water balloon fight with her friends.  They fill up 10 packs of balloons that have 30 balloons per pack.  By the end of the afternoon, 12 balloons are left.  How many balloons did they throw?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
