ep by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Samantha has 12 fewer paintings than Shelley, and Shelley has 8 paintings more than Kim. If Samantha has 27 paintings, how many paintings does Kim have?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Oscar has 24 lollipops and eats 2 on his way to school.  He passes 14 out to his friends.  He buys twice as many lollipops on his way home as he gave to his friends.  He eats 3 more that night and 2 more in the morning.  How many lollipops does Oscar have?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "31", "rationale": "First, I need to determine how many lollipops Oscar has at the end of the day.\n\n1. **Starting Lollipops:** Oscar starts with 24 lollipops.\n\n2. **Eaten on the Way to School:** He eats 2 lollipops, leaving him with 24 - 2 = 22 lollipops.\n\n3. **Passing Out to Friends:** He gives 14 lollipops to his friends, so he has 22 - 14 = 8 lollipops left.\n\n4. **Buying More Lollipops:** On his way home, Oscar buys twice as many lollipops as he gave to his friends. Since he gave away 14, he buys 14 * 2 = 28 lollipops. Adding these to his remaining lollipops, he now has 8 + 28 = 36 lollipops.\n\n5. **Eaten at Night:** He eats 3 more lollipops that night, leaving him with 36 - 3 = 33 lollipops.\n\n6. **Eaten in the Morning:** Finally, he eats 2 more lollipops in the morning, resulting in 33 - 2 = 31 lollipops.\n\nTherefore, Oscar ends up with \\boxed{31} lollipops.\n</think>\n\nLet's solve the problem step by step.\n\n1. **Starting Lollipops:**\n   - Oscar starts with **24 lollipops**.\n\n2. **Eaten on the Way to School:**\n   - He eats **2 lollipops** on his way to school.\n   - Remaining lollipops: \\( 24 - 2 = 22 \\) lollipops.\n\n3. **Passing Out to Friends:**\n   - He gives **14 lollipops** to his friends.\n   - Remaining lollipops: \\( 22 - 14 = 8 \\) lollipops.\n\n4. **Buying More Lollipops:**\n   - He buys twice as many lollipops as he gave to his friends.\n   - He buys \\( 14 \\times 2 = 28 \\) lollipops.\n   - Total lollipops after buying: \\( 8 + 28 = 36 \\) lollipops.\n\n5. **Eaten at Night:**\n   - He eats **3 more lollipops** that night.\n   - Remaining lollipops: \\( 36 - 3 = 33 \\) lollipops.\n\n6. **Eaten in the Morning:**\n   - He eats **2 more lollipops** in the morning.\n   - Final number of lollipops: \\( 33 - 2 = 31 \\) lollipops.\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\\(\\boxed{31}\\) lollipops.", "pred_ans": "31", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Oscar has 24 lollipops and eats 2 on his way to school.  He passes 14 out to his friends.  He buys twice as many lollipops on his way home as he gave to his friends.  He eats 3 more that night and 2 more in the morning.  How many lollipops does Oscar have?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
