 problem says he wishes to spend an equal amount each day for one week. So, he's spending $10 each day, not necessarily buying one figure per day. So, perhaps he can buy multiple figures on a day if he wants, as long as he spends $10 each day.\n\nSo, from Sunday to Wednesday, he buys wooden figures costing $5 each. So, each day, he can spend $10, buying two wooden figures at $5 each. So, 2 per day, 4 days, so 8 total. Then, from Thursday to Saturday, he buys plastic figures at $2 each. So, each day he can spend $10, buying 5 plastic figures at $2 each. So, 5 per day, 3 days, so 15 total. So, 8 plus 15 is 23. Hmm, but that contradicts my initial thought.\n\nWait, so which is correct? Is it 4 wooden figures and 3 plastic figures, totaling 7, or 8 wooden and 15 plastic, totaling 23? Hmm.\n\nLooking back at the problem: \"Peter has $70 and wishes to spend an equal amount each day for one week.\" So, he is spending equal amount each day, so each day, he spends $10, as 70 divided by 7 is 10. So, each day he can spend $10 on whatever he wants.\n\n\"From Sunday through Wednesday, he spent his money on wooden action figures which cost $5 each.\" So, on each of those days, he spent $10 on wooden figures. So, each day, he buys two wooden figures, because each is $5. So, 2 per day times 4 days is 8 wooden figures.\n\n\"For the rest of the week, he will buy plastic action figures which cost $2 each.\" So, on each of the remaining 3 days, he will spend $10 on plastic figures. Each plastic figure is $2, so he buys 5 per day. 5 times 3 is 15. So, total action figures: 8 plus 15 is 23.\n\nBut wait, in my initial calculation, I thought he only bought 4 wooden and 3 plastic, but that was because I assumed he was only buying one figure per day, which is not the case. The problem doesn't specify he buys one each day, only that he spends equal amounts each day.\n\nSo, the correct way is: each day, he can buy as many as he wants, as long as the total spending for the day is equal. So, in 