he didn't buy the headphone set, he would have 48 left, which is 4*12, so he could buy 12 CDs in total. But since he already bought one, that's 11 more.\n\nWait, now I'm getting confused again. If he buys just one CD and one headphone set, as the problem states, then if he decides not to buy the headphone set, he can use the entire $48 for CDs. So, how many CDs is that? $48 divided by $4 is 12 CDs. So, he can buy 12 CDs, but he already bought one, so he can buy 11 more.\n\nYes, that seems consistent.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem is trying to trick us into thinking that he didn't buy the headphone set, so he can keep the $44 and buy 11 CDs, which is 11 more. Either way, whether we think of it as 12 total or 11 additional, the answer is 11.\n\nWait, but let me think about it another way. Suppose he didn't buy the headphone set, so he has $48. He already bought one CD for $4, so he has $44 left. Each CD is $4, so 44 divided by 4 is 11. So, he can buy 11 more CDs. Therefore, the answer is 11.\n\nYes, that seems solid.\n\nWait, just to be thorough, let me consider if he bought multiple headphone sets or multiple CDs. But the problem states he bought a CD and a headphone set, which is singular. So, he bought one CD and one headphone set. Therefore, his total expenditure is $4 + cost of one headphone set = $48, so the headphone set is $44.\n\nIf he didn't buy the headphone set, he would have the full $48, which he could use to buy more CDs. He already bought one, so how many more can he buy?\n\nTotal money without headphone set: $48.\n\nAlready spent $4 on CD, so remaining: $44.\n\nNumber of CDs he can buy with $44: $44 / $4 = 11.\n\nTherefore, he can buy 11 more CDs.\n\nSo, I think that's the correct answer. 11 more CDs.\n\nJust to make sure, let me write down all the steps clearly:\n\n1. Total amount paid: $48.\n2. Amount spent on CD: $4.\n3. Therefore, amount spent on headphone set: $48 - $4 = $44.\n4. If he had not bought the headphone set, total amount availa