 needs to buy, we subtract the number of bottles he already has from the total number needed:\n\\[ 140 - 48 = 92 \\]\n\nThus, Bill will need to buy an additional \\boxed{92} bottles of water.", "pred_ans": "92", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Bill is trying to figure out how many water bottles he needs to buy for his party.  He started out with 2 cases of water which have 24 bottles each. He has 70 guests that will be arriving, and he wants to have 2 bottles of water for each guest. How many additional bottles of water will he need to buy?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: George, a grade six teacher, ordered 600 burritos for the sixth-grade class picnic. If there were 50 students at the picnic, and each student was given ten burritos, with Mr. George eating 20 of them, calculate the total number of leftover burritos from the picnic?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "80", "rationale": "Okay, so George is a grade six teacher and he ordered 600 burritos for the sixth-grade class picnic. Hmm, that seems like a lot of burritos! Let me try to figure out how many burritos are leftover after the picnic. \n\nFirst, I know that there are 50 students at the picnic. Each student was given ten burritos. So, I guess I need to calculate how many burritos were given to the students. Let me write that down.\n\nNumber of students = 50\nBurritos per student = 10\n\nSo, total burritos for students = students * burritos per student\nTotal burritos for students = 50 * 10\n\nLet me do the multiplication. 50 times 10 is... hmm, 50 times 10 is 500. So, 500 burritos were given to the students.\n\nBut wait, Mr. George also ate 20 burritos. So, I need to account for those as well. So, the total number of burritos that were eaten is the 500 given to the students plus the 20 that Mr. George ate.\n\nTotal burritos eaten = burritos for students + Mr. George's burritos\nTotal burritos eaten = 500 + 20\n\nAdding those together, 500 plus 20 is 520. So, 520 burritos were eaten in total.\n\nNow, George ordered 600 burritos. So, to find out how many are leftover, I subtract the number of burritos eaten from the total number ordered.\n\nLeftover burritos = total burritos ordered - total burritos eaten\nLeftover burritos = 600 - 520\n\nHmm, 600 minus 520. Let me do that subtraction. 600 minus 500 is 100, and then minus 20 more is 80. So, 600 minus 520 equals 80.\n\nWait, let me double-check my calculations to make sure I didn't make a mistake. So, 50 students times 10 burritos each is 500. Plus 20 for Mr. George, so 520 eaten. 600 minus 520 is 80. Yeah, that seems right.\n\nSo, the total number of leftover burritos from the picnic is 80. Hmm, that's quite a few burritos left!\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe total number of leftover burritos is \\boxed{80}.\n</think>\n\nGeorge ordered 600 burritos for the sixth-grade class picnic. There were 50 students at the picnic, each given 10 burritos. Additionally, Mr. George ate 20 burritos. \n\nFirst, calculate the total number of burritos given to the students:\n\\[\n50 \\text{ students} \\times 10 \\text{ burritos/student} = 500 \\text{ burritos}\n\\]\n\nNext, add the 20 burritos that Mr. George ate:\n\\[\n500 \\text{ burritos} + 20 \\text{ burritos} = 520 \\text{ burritos}\n\\]\n\nSubtract the total number of burritos eaten from the total number ordered to find the leftover burritos:\n\\[\n600 \\text{ burritos} - 520 \\text{ burritos} = 80 \\text{ burritos}\n\\]\n\nThus, the total number of leftover burritos is \\boxed{80}.", "pred_ans": "80", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: George, a grade six teacher, ordered 600 burritos for the sixth-grade class picnic. If there were 50 students at the picnic, and each student was given ten burritos, with Mr. George eating 20 of them, calculate the total number of leftover burritos from the picnic?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Mark's car breaks down and he needs to get a new radiator.  The cost for a new radiator is $400 but he goes to get it at a junk shop and gets it for 80% off.  He then hires a mechanic to install it and it takes 3 hours at $50 an hour.  How much did he pay?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "230", "rationale": "Okay, so Mark's car broke down, and he needs a new radiator. Hmm, that sounds like a common problem. Let me try to figure out how much he ended up paying. I'll take it step by step because I don't want to rush and make mistakes.\n\nFirst, the cost of the new radiator is $400. But he goes to a junk shop and gets it for 80% off. Hmm, 80% off means he's paying 20% of the original price, right? Let me calculate 20% of $400. So, 20% is 0.20 in decimal, and 0.20 multiplied by 400 is... 80 dollars. So, the radiator costs him $80. That's a good deal!\n\nNow, he hires a mechanic to install it, and it takes 3 hours at $50 an hour. I need to calculate how much the mechanic charges him. So, 3 hours times $50 per hour. Let me do that multiplication: 3 \u00d7 50. That equals 150. So, the mechanic charges $150 for the installation.\n\nNow, to find out the total amount Mark paid, I should add the cost of the radiator and the labor cost. So, $80 for the radiator plus $150 for the installation. Let me add those together: 80 + 150. That equals 230. So, altogether, Mark paid $230.\n\nWait, let me double-check to make sure I didn't make any mistakes. The radiator was $400, and he got 80% off. 80% of 400 is 320, so the discount is $320. So, 400 minus 320 is indeed $80. That part seems right.\n\nFor the mechanic, 3 hours at $50 an hour. 50 times 3 is 150. That's straightforward. Adding 80 and 150 gives 230. Yeah, that seems correct.\n\nSo, putting it all together: radiator cost after discount is $80, labor cost is $150, total is $230. I don't think I missed anything here. The