ct.\n\nIf it's compound depreciation, using the formula (1 - rate)^t:\n\n2008: 20,000 \u00d7 0.79 = 15,800\n\n2009: 15,800 \u00d7 0.79 = 12,542\n\n2010: 12,542 \u00d7 0.79 \u2248 9,872.78\n\nSo, which one is it? The problem says \"depreciates at a constant rate of 21% per year.\" The term \"constant rate\" without mention of compounding suggests straight line depreciation. So, probably the answer is \\$7,400.\n\nBut let me think again. In some contexts, depreciation can be compound, especially if it's based on a percentage of the current value each year. So, maybe the problem is expecting compound depreciation.\n\nWait, the problem says, \"The price of the car depreciates at a constant rate of 21% per year.\" So, it doesn't say \"21% of the original price per year,\" which would imply straight line. It just says 21% per year, which could be interpreted as 21% each year, which is a compound rate.\n\nHmm, this is a bit confusing. Maybe I should go back to the original formula.\n\nIn the first approach, I used the formula:\n\nPrice after t years = Initial Price \u00d7 (1 - rate)^t\n\nWhich is the standard formula for compound depreciation. So, that's a bit more precise because it accounts for the decreasing value each year.\n\nSo, given that, let's stick with the first calculation where I used the compound formula, which gave me approximately \\$9,872.78.\n\nBut then again, the term \"constant rate\" might just mean that the rate is fixed each year, which is the case in both straight line and compound depreciation.\n\nWait, perhaps the problem is intended to use linear depreciation because it's simpler, and the term \"constant rate\" is meant to imply that the rate is the same each year.\n\nBut to be safe, maybe I should check both methods and see which one is more likely.\n\nWait, actually, in financial terms, depreciation is often done on a straight line basis unless specified otherwise. So, if it's just given as a constant rate, it's probably straight line.\n\nBut i