{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: A football team has 105 members.  There are twice as many players on the offense as there is on the defense. There is half the number of players on the special teams as there is on the defense.  How many players are on the defense?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "30", "rationale": "Alright, so I have this problem here about a football team with 105 members. It says there are twice as many players on the offense as there are on the defense. Then, it mentions that there are half the number of players on the special teams as there are on the defense. The question is asking how many players are on the defense.\n\nHmm, okay. Let me break this down. First, let me figure out what each part means. The total number of players is 105. That includes offense, defense, and special teams. So, if I denote the number of defense players as a variable, say D, then I can express the number of offense players and special teams players in terms of D.\n\nThe problem states that there are twice as many players on the offense as there are on the defense. So, if D is the number of defense players, then the number of offense players would be 2 times D, which is 2D.\n\nNext, it says there is half the number of players on the special teams as there is on the defense. So, if D is the number of defense players, then the number of special teams players would be half of D, which is (1/2)D or 0.5D.\n\nSo now I have offense as 2D, defense as D, and special teams as 0.5D. The total number of players is the sum of these, right? So, offense plus defense plus special teams equals 105.\n\nLet me write that out as an equation:\n\n2D (offense) + D (defense) + 0.5D (special teams) = 105.\n\nOkay, let me make sure I got that right. Offense is twice the defense, so 2D. Defense is D. Special teams are half the defense, so 0.5D. So, adding those together gives the total number of players, which is 105