imes that, which is 48 kg. No matter how I approach it, the answer remains consistent. I don't think I made any calculation errors here because all methods converge to the same number.\n\nSo, yeah, I think that's solid. 48 kg is the total amount of flour in three bags before they were divided into portions.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe total amount of flour in three bags before division was \\boxed{48} kilograms.\n</think>\n\nFirst, we determine the amount of flour in one bag by noting that it is divided into 8 portions, each of 2 kilograms. Therefore, one bag contains:\n\\[ 8 \\times 2 = 16 \\text{ kilograms} \\]\n\nNext, we calculate the total amount of flour in three bags. Since each bag is 16 kilograms, three bags will contain:\n\\[ 3 \\times 16 = 48 \\text{ kilograms} \\]\n\nThus, the total amount of flour in three bags before it was divided into portions was:\n\\[\n\\boxed{48}\n\\]", "pred_ans": "48", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: A bag of flour is divided into 8 portions of 2 kilograms each. How much flour (in kilograms) was in three bags in total, before it was divided into portions?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
