e flamingos.\n\nWait, is that right? Let me double-check. If you take one third of 18, that's 6, so you take 6 away, leaving 12 pink. Then, you paint those 6 white and put them back. So, total flamingos now are 12 pink and 6 white. That seems correct.\n\nThen, on Sunday morning, they added another 18 pink plastic flamingos. So, now, the total number of pink flamingos would be 12 plus 18, which is 30. And the white flamingos are still 6, right? Because they were painted on Saturday and not taken back or added to.\n\nSo, at noon on Sunday, the number of pink flamingos is 30, and the number of white flamingos is 6. The question is asking how many more pink plastic flamingos were out than white ones.\n\nSo, to find how many more pink than white, we subtract the number of white flamingos from the number of pink flamingos.\n\nThat would be 30 minus 6, which is 24.\n\nWait, hold on, let me make sure I didn't skip any steps. So, starting with 18 pink on Friday. Saturday morning, they take back one third, which is 6, so 6 pink are taken back. 6 become white. So now, we have 12 pink and 6 white. Then, on Sunday morning, they add another 18 pink, so 12 plus 18 is 30 pink. The white ones are still 6. So, 30 minus 6 is 24. Hmm, that seems straightforward.\n\nIs there another way to think about it? Maybe calculate the total number of flamingos and then see how they're distributed.\n\nOn Saturday morning, after taking back 6 pink and adding 6 white, the total number of flamingos is 12 pink + 6 white = 18 flamingos. Then, on Sunday morning, they add another 18 pink, so total flamingos are 18 + 18 = 36. So, 36 total, with 30 pink and 6 white.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using equations would help.\n\nLet me define:\n\nLet P = number of pink flamingos\n\nLet W = number of white flamingos\n\nOn Friday morning: P = 18\n\nOn Saturday morning: they take back one third of the pink flamingos, so they take back (1/3)*18 = 6. So, the number of pink flamingos becomes P - 6 = 12. Then, they paint 