ond, 865 more. So, total is 644 + 865 = 1509.\n\nI think that's solid. I can't think of another way to approach it, and all methods lead to the same answer.\n\nSo, in summary, each DVD can be played 1000 times before breaking. The first has been played 356 times, so it can be played 644 more times. The second has been played 135 times, so it can be played 865 more times. Adding those together gives the total number of additional plays before both break: 644 + 865 = 1509. Alternatively, considering the total capacity of both as 2000 plays, subtracting the 356 + 135 = 491 plays already done, gives 2000 - 491 = 1509.\n\nTherefore, the total number of times both DVDs can be played before they break is 1509.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe total number of times both DVDs can be played before they break is \\boxed{1509}.\n</think>\n\nTo determine how many total times both DVDs can be played before they break, we need to consider the remaining number of plays each DVD can handle.\n\n1. The first DVD has been played 356 times and can be played 1000 - 356 = 644 more times.\n2. The second DVD has been played 135 times and can be played 1000 - 135 = 865 more times.\n\nAdding the remaining plays for both DVDs:\n644 (remaining plays for the first DVD) + 865 (remaining plays for the second DVD) = 1509.\n\nThus, the total number of times both DVDs can be played before they break is \\boxed{1509}.", "pred_ans": "1509", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: A DVD can be played 1000 times before it breaks. There are two DVDs in the public library, one has been played 356 times and the other has been played 135 times. How many total times can both DVDs be played before they break?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
