{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Andrew bakes 200 mini cinnamon rolls and 300 mini blueberry muffins. A normal cinnamon roll has 600 calories and a normal blueberry muffin has 450 calories. If a mini pastry has 1/3rd of the calories of a normal version, how many calories do the pastries he baked have?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "85000", "rationale": "First, calculate the total number of pastries baked by Andrew. He bakes 200 mini cinnamon rolls and 300 mini blueberry muffins, which makes a total of 500 pastries.\n\nNext, determine the calories for each type of mini pastry. A normal cinnamon roll has 600 calories, so a mini cinnamon roll has 600 divided by 3, which equals 200 calories. Similarly, a normal blueberry muffin has 450 calories, so a mini blueberry muffin has 450 divided by 3, which equals 150 calories.\n\nFinally, calculate the total calories for all the pastries. Multiply the number of mini cinnamon rolls by the calories of each, and do the same for the mini blueberry muffins, then add the two results together to find the total calories.\n</think>\n\n**Solution:**\n\nAndrew bakes **200 mini cinnamon rolls** and **300 mini blueberry muffins**. Each mini pastry (whether a cinnamon roll or a blueberry muffin) has \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\) of the calories of their respective normal versions.\n\n1. **Calculate the total number of pastries:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total pastries} = \\text{Mini cinnamon rolls} + \\text{Mini blueberry muffins} = 200 + 300 = 500\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the calories for each type of mini pastry:**\n   - **Mini cinnamon roll:**\n     \\[\n     \\text{Calories per mini cinnamon roll} = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 600 = 200 \\text{ calories}\n     \\]\n   - **Mini blueberry muffin:**\n     \\[\n     \\text{Calories per mini blueberry muffin} = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 450 = 150 \\text{ calories}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the total calories for all pastries:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total calories} = (\\text{Number of mini cinnamon rolls} \\times \\text{Calories per mini cinnamon roll}) + (\\text{Number of mini blueberry muffins} \\times \\text{Calories per mini blueberry muffin})\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total calories} = (200 \\times 200) + (300 \\times 150) = 40,\\!000 + 45,\\!000 = 85,\\!000 \\text{ calories}\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\\[\n\\boxed{85,\\!000}\n\\]", "pred_ans": "85,000", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Andrew bakes 200 mini cinnamon rolls and 300 mini blueberry muffins. A normal cinnamon roll has 600 calories and a normal blueberry muffin has 450 calories. If a mini pastry has 1/3rd of the calories of a normal version, how many calories do the pastries he baked have?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
