correct.\n\nFourth child: 69 + 3 = 72 inches. So, 72 inches. Wait, that's the same as the first child. So, the fourth child is the same height as the first child. That seems a bit unusual, but according to the problem, that's how it's set up. So, I think that's right.\n\nJust to make sure, let me go through it again:\n\n1. First child: 6 feet = 72 inches.\n2. Second child: 72 + 2 = 74 inches.\n3. Third child: 74 - 5 = 69 inches.\n4. Fourth child: 69 + 3 = 72 inches.\n\nYes, that all adds up. So, the fourth child is 72 inches tall, which is 6 feet. So, even though the second and third children are taller and shorter respectively, the fourth child ends up being the same height as the first.\n\nI think that's the answer. It's interesting how it loops back around, but the math checks out.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe fourth child is \\boxed{72} inches tall.\n</think>\n\nFirst, convert the first child's height from feet to inches:\n\\[ 6 \\text{ feet} = 6 \\times 12 = 72 \\text{ inches} \\]\n\nNext, calculate the second child's height, who is 2 inches taller than the first child:\n\\[ 72 + 2 = 74 \\text{ inches} \\]\n\nThen, determine the third child's height, who is 5 inches shorter than the second child:\n\\[ 74 - 5 = 69 \\text{ inches} \\]\n\nFinally, find the fourth child's height, who is 3 inches taller than the third child:\n\\[ 69 + 3 = 72 \\text{ inches} \\]\n\nThe fourth child is \\boxed{72} inches tall.", "pred_ans": "72", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Caroline has 4 children.  The first child is 6 feet tall.  The second child is two inches taller than the first child.  The third child is 5 inches shorter than the second child.  And the fourth child is three inches taller than the third child.  How tall is the fourth child, in inches?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
