hat seems right.\n\nBut just to be sure, let me add up the people who got off and on to see if it makes sense. At the first stop, 47 got off and 13 got on. So, net change is -47 +13 = -34. So, 34 fewer people after the first stop. So, 172 -34 is 138. Then, at the next stop, another 38 get off, but no one gets on. So, that's a net change of -38. So, 138 -38 is 100. Yeah, that matches.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can think about it in terms of equations. Let me denote the initial number as N, which is 172. After the first stop, the number of people on the train is N - 47 + 13. Then, after the second stop, it's that number minus 38. So, in equation form:\n\nNumber after first stop: N - 47 + 13\n\nNumber after second stop: (N - 47 + 13) - 38\n\nSo, substituting N with 172:\n\nFirst stop: 172 - 47 +13 = 138\n\nSecond stop: 138 -38 = 100\n\nSo, yeah, that seems correct.\n\nI wonder if there's another way to look at this problem, maybe by combining the changes or something, but I think the step-by-step subtraction and addition is pretty straightforward.\n\nJust to visualize, imagine the train starting with 172 people. At the first stop, 47 people exit, which is like removing 47 passengers. Then, 13 new passengers get on, so it's like adding 13. So, the net effect is 172 -47 +13 = 138. Then, at the next stop, another 38 get off, so we subtract 38 from 138, getting 100.\n\nI think that's it. 100 people should be on the train after the second stop.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of people on the train is \\boxed{100}.\n</think>\n\nStarting with 172 people on the train:\n\n1. At the first stop, 47 people get off and 13 get on.\n   - Calculation: \\( 172 - 47 + 13 = 138 \\)\n2. At the next stop, 38 people get off.\n   - Calculation: \\( 138 - 38 = 100 \\)\n\nThus, the number of people on the train is \\(\\boxed{100}\\).", "pred_ans": "100", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: A train has 172 people traveling on it. At the first stop 47 people get off and 13 more people get on, and at the next stop another 38 people get off. How many people are on the train?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
