p to 1500, which checks out.\n\nNow, the problem also mentions that the remaining half of the students are under 16 years old. Since the total number of students is 3000, half of them would be 1500 as well. So, 1500 students are under 16 years old.\n\nIt says that half of the students under 16 are male. So, let me find out how many male students that is. Half of 1500 is 750. So, 750 male students are under 16.\n\nTherefore, the remaining half, which is also 750, must be female students under 16. That makes sense because if half are male, the other half must be female.\n\nSo, putting it all together, we have:\n\n- Students over 16: 1500\n  - Male: 375\n  - Female: 1125\n- Students under 16: 1500\n  - Male: 750\n  - Female: 750\n\nNow, the question is asking for the total number of female students enrolled at the school. So, I need to add the number of female students over 16 and the number of female students under 16.\n\nThat would be 1125 (female over 16) + 750 (female under 16). Let me do the addition: 1125 + 750.\n\n1125 + 750. Hmm, 1125 + 700 is 1825, and then +50 is 1875. So, 1875.\n\nWait, let me check that again. 1125 + 750. Alternatively, I can do 1125 + 750 by adding the hundreds: 1000 + 700 = 1700, then the tens: 125 + 50 = 175, so 1700 + 175 = 1875. Yes, that's correct.\n\nSo, the total number of female students is 1875. Let me just cross-verify all my calculations to make sure I didn't make a mistake somewhere.\n\nTotal students: 3000.\n\nHalf are over 16: 1500. So, 1500 over 16. One-fourth male: 375 male. So, 375 + 1125 female over 16. That adds to 1500, which is correct.\n\nRemaining half: 1500 under 16. Half are male: 750, so half must be female: 750. So, 750 + 750 = 1500. That's correct.\n\nTotal female students: 1125 + 750 = 1875. That seems right.\n\nLet me think if there's another way to approach this problem. Maybe by calculating the percentages or fractions and then applying them.\n\nAlternatively, since half the school is over 16 and half is und