ing the stamped letters to the already-stamped pile. So, the already-stamped pile was originally some number, and she added the one-third of 60 letters to it, making it 30.\n\nWait, that might not make sense. Let me think. If she puts stamps on one-third of the letters needing stamps, does that mean she's moving those letters from the needing stamps pile to the already-stamped pile? So, the already-stamped pile was smaller, and after adding, it becomes 30.\n\nSo, let me denote variables.\n\nLet me let x be the number of letters in the already-stamped pile when Jennie began.\n\nShe takes one-third of the letters needing stamps, which is (1/3)*60 = 20 letters, and adds these 20 letters to the already-stamped pile. So, the already-stamped pile becomes x + 20.\n\nBut the problem says that after this addition, there are now 30 letters in the already-stamped pile. So, x + 20 = 30.\n\nTherefore, solving for x: x = 30 - 20 = 10.\n\nWait, so the already-stamped pile had 10 letters when Jennie began.\n\nIs that correct? Let me check.\n\nShe had 60 letters needing stamps. She put stamps on one-third of them, which is 20 letters. Then, she added those 20 letters to the already-stamped pile, which had x letters. So, the new total is x + 20 = 30. So, yes, x is 10.\n\nSo, the initial number of already-stamped letters was 10.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I misinterpreted the problem. Maybe the 30 letters are in addition to the pile she moved. Let me read it again.\n\n\"She puts stamps on one-third of the letters needing stamps. If there are now 30 letters in the pile of already-stamped letters, how many were in that pile when Jennie began?\"\n\nSo, she adds 20 letters to the already-stamped pile, and the total becomes 30. So, x + 20 = 30, so x = 10. That seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, if she only moved the 20 letters, so the already-stamped pile is now 30, which would mean the already-stamped pile was 30 before? But that doesn't make sense because she adds letters to it. So, if she adds letters, the pile should increase. So, if it was x before she added, and then after adding 20, it's 30, so x is 10.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the already-stamped pile is 30, and she adds more to it? Wait, no, the problem says she puts stamps on one-third of the letters needing stamps. So, she's only moving letters from the needing stamps pile to the already-stamped pile. So, the already-stamped pile was 10, then she adds 20, making it 30.\n\nYes, that seems to make sense. So, the initial number was 10.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 10.\n\nBut let me try another approach to verify.\n\nLet me think about the total number of letters. She has 60 letters that need stamps. She uses 1/3 of them, which is 20, and stamps them. So, she's moving 20 letters from the needing stamps pile to the already-stamped pile.\n\nSo, if the already-stamped pile went from x to 30, that means x + 20 = 30, so x = 10. So, that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is saying that after stamping one-third of the needing stamps, the already-stamped pile is 30. If the already-stamped pile was 30, and she didn't add any letters, but just stamps, maybe that's different? Wait, no, because the problem says she puts stamps on one-third of the letters needing stamps, so she's adding those letters to the already-stamped pile. So, adding 20 letters to the already-stamped pile, which goes from x to 30. Therefore, x is 10.\n\nYes, so I think 10 is correct.\n\nBut just to explore, what if the already-stamped pile was 30, and she didn't add any letters, but just stamps? Then, the letters needing stamps would have 60 letters, one-third of which is 20, so 20 letters get stamps. But the already-stamped pile is still 30. So, if she didn't add any letters, just stamps, then it's 30.\n\nBut the problem says she puts stamps on one-third of the letters needing stamps. So, \"puts stamps on\" likely means adding the letters to the already-stamped pile. So, she moves 20 letters from the needing stamps pile to the already-stamped pile. Therefore, the already-stamped pile goes from x to x + 20 = 30. So, x = 10.\n\nI think that's solid.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the already-stamped pile is 30, and she's adding stamps to some letters in the needing stamps pile, not moving them. So, maybe the already-stamped pile remains 30, and the needing stamps pile now has 60 - 20 = 40.\n\nBut the problem says she puts stamps on one-third of the letters needing stamps. So, if she puts stamps on 20 letters, those 20 letters are now in the already-stamped pile? Or does putting stamps on them not move them.\n\nWait, no, putting stamps on letters in the needing stamps pile would mean that those letters are now stamped, so they can be added to the already-stamped pile. So, perhaps the already-stamped pile is being increased by 20 letters.\n\nSo, if it was x before, then x + 20 = 30. So, x is 10.\n\nYes, that makes sense.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the already-stamped pile is 30, and she adds 20 more letters to it, making it 50. But that contradicts the problem statement.\n\nWait, no. The problem says \"there are now 30 letters in the pile of already-stamped letters.\" So, it's 30 after she does her action.\n\nSo, the action is stamping one-third of the needing stamps, which is 20 letters, and putting them into the already-stamped pile.\n\nSo, the already-stamped pile was x, now it's x + 20 = 30. So, x = 10.\n\nYes, that seems correct.\n\nTherefore, the initial number of already-stamped letters was 10.\n\nSo, I think that's the answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of letters in the pile when Jennie began was \\boxed{10}.\n</think>\n\nJennie has a pile of 60 letters needing stamps and a pile of already-stamped letters. She puts stamps on one-third of the letters needing stamps, which is 20 letters. After this, the already-stamped pile has 30 letters. \n\nLet \\( x \\) be the number of letters in the already-stamped pile when Jennie began. After adding the 20 letters, the pile becomes \\( x + 20 \\