{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Two track teams are competing against each other in a 4 by 400 meter relay; a race where each competing team has four members that each run 400 meters, or one lap, around a standard track.  One of the two teams is very well-rounded and each of their members will run their 400 meter leg in precisely 55 seconds.  The other team is less well-rounded; their first runner will run their 400 meter leg in 60 seconds then each subsequent runner will be 3 seconds faster than the previous runner.  Using this information, how many seconds will the faster team win by?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "2", "rationale": "Alright, so I have this problem here about a 4 by 400 meter relay race between two teams. The track is standard, which I assume is 400 meters around, so each runner does one lap. \n\nFirst, let me make sure I understand the problem correctly. There are two teams competing. One team is very well-rounded, meaning each of their four runners is equally skilled and will complete their 400-meter leg in exactly 55 seconds each. The other team is less well-rounded. Their first runner will take 60 seconds to complete their 400 meters, but each subsequent runner will be 3 seconds faster than the previous one. So, the second runner will take 60 - 3 = 57 seconds, the third will take 57 - 3 = 54 seconds, and the fourth will take 54 - 3 = 51 seconds. \n\nThe question is asking how many seconds the faster team will win by. So, I need to figure out the total time each team takes to complete the relay and then find the difference between their total times.\n\nLet me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the total time for the well-rounded team. Since each of their runners takes 55 seconds per lap, and there are four runners, it's straightforward:\n\nTotal time for Team 1 = 4 runners * 55 seconds per runner = 4 * 55 = 220 seconds.\n\nNow, for the less well-r