wer.\n\nIs there any chance I misread the question? It says last year, 50 students. This year, 20% increase. So, maybe I should think about whether it's 20% of last year's or 20% relative to something else. But no, usually, percentage increases are always relative to the original value unless specified otherwise. So, in this case, it's 20% of last year's 50.\n\nAnother thing to consider: sometimes, people get confused whether to apply the percentage to the original or to something else, but in this case, I think 20% of 50 is the right approach.\n\nLet me also think about it in terms of ratios. If last year was 50, and this year it's 20% more, then the ratio of this year's students to last year's is 1.2:1. So, 1.2 corresponds to this year's number, which is 60, and 1 corresponds to last year's 50. So, 1.2 times 50 is 60. Yep, that's consistent.\n\nIs there any way this could be 50 times 1.2? Yes, that's another way to see it. 1.2 is the multiplier for the percentage increase. So, 50 times 1.2 is 60.\n\nLet me think about if I made any calculation errors. 20% of 50 is 10. 50 plus 10 is 60. 50 times 1.2 is 60. All of these methods confirm that the number is 60.\n\nWait, just to make sure, 1.2 times 50: 50 times 1 is 50, 50 times 0.2 is 10, so 50 + 10 is 60. Yep, that's correct.\n\nIs there a way to visualize this? Maybe with a bar diagram or something. If I draw a bar representing 50 students, and then another bar that's 20% longer. So, 20% of 50 is 10, so the second bar is 60. That makes sense.\n\nAlternatively, think of it as money. If I had $50 and I got 20% more, how much would I have? 20% of 50 is $10, so I'd have $60. That's a common way to think about it, and it helps reinforce that the answer is 60.\n\nWait, another thought: is 20% increase the same as tripling? No, because 100% increase would be double. So, 20% is only a fifth more. So, 50 plus 1/5 of 50 is 50 + 10 is 60. Yeah, that's another way to think about it.\n\nWait, just to make sure, 50 plus 20% is 60