les his money.\" Hmm, that's a bit ambiguous. It could mean tripling the total amount of money he has, or tripling the number of bills. I need to figure out which one makes sense in this context.\n\nGiven that he's dealing with bills, it's more likely that tripling his money refers to tripling the number of bills. Because if it were tripling the total amount, he would have to consider the denomination of the bills. So, I think it's reasonable to assume that tripling the number of bills is the correct interpretation here.\n\nSo, tripling 20 bills gives him 60 bills. So, he now has 60 twenty-dollar bills.\n\nThen, he converts all his bills to 5 dollar bills. So, he's going to take each of the 60 twenty-dollar bills and convert them into 5 dollar bills. Let me figure out how many 5 dollar bills that would be.\n\nEach 20 dollar bill can be converted into how many 5 dollar bills? Let me see:\n\n$20 / $5 = 4. So, each 20 dollar bill equals 4 five-dollar bills.\n\nTherefore, 60 twenty-dollar bills would convert into 60 * 4 = 240 five-dollar bills.\n\nWait, let me double-check that. If each 20 is 4 fives, then 60 times that would be 60 * 4. Let me calculate 60 * 4:\n\n60 * 4 = 240. Yeah, that's correct.\n\nSo, he ends up with 240 five-dollar bills.\n\nLet me just recap to make sure I didn't make a mistake:\n\n1. He withdraws $1000 in 20s: 50 bills.\n2. Loses 10 bills: 40 left.\n3. Uses half (20) to pay a bill: 20 left.\n4. Triples his money (bills): 20 * 3 = 60.\n5. Converts all to 5s: 60 * 4 = 240.\n\nYes, that seems to add up.\n\nBut wait, let me consider the alternative interpretation where tripling his money refers to tripling the total amount. Maybe that's what the problem intended. Let me explore that.\n\nIf tripling the total money, then he had $1000. Tripling that would be $3000. But he didn't have $3000 in cash, he had 40 bills left after losing 10. Wait, no, after losing 10, he had 40 bills, which is $800. Then he used half to pay a bill, so he had $400 left. Then