500, and 1500 * 0.2 is 300. So, adding them together: 1500 + 300 = 1800.\n\nSo, the friend bought the watch for $1800.\n\nNow, the question is asking for the percentage discount the friend obtained from the original price.\n\nSo, the original price is $2000, and the friend paid $1800. The discount is the amount he saved from the original price, which is 2000 - 1800. Let me compute that.\n\n2000 - 1800 = 200.\n\nSo, he saved $200.\n\nNow, to find the percentage discount, we need to compare this discount to the original price.\n\nSo, the formula for percentage discount is:\n\n(Discount / Original Price) * 100%\n\nPlugging in the numbers:\n\n(200 / 2000) * 100%\n\nLet me compute that.\n\nFirst, 200 divided by 2000. That's 0.1.\n\n0.1 * 100% = 10%.\n\nSo, the percentage discount is 10%.\n\nWait, let me double-check my calculations to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nOriginal price: $2000.\n\n75% of that is 1500. Correct.\n\nThen, 120% of 1500 is 1800. Correct.\n\nSo, from original price of 2000, he paid 1800, so discount is 200. 200 is 10% of 2000.\n\nYes, that makes sense.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can think of it another way.\n\nMr. Rogers bought the watch for 75% of original, so he paid 25% less than original. Then, he sold it at 120% of his price, which is 120% of 75% of original. So, 0.75 * 1.2 = 0.9, which is 90% of original. So, the friend paid 90% of the original price, which is a 10% discount.\n\nYes, that's another way to see it. 75% * 120% = 90%, so 10% discount.\n\nEither way, the discount is 10%.\n\nHmm, seems consistent.\n\nSo, the answer is 10% discount.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe percentage discount obtained by Mr. Roger's friend from the original price is \\boxed{10\\%}.\n</think>\n\nThe original price of the watch is $2000. Mr. Rogers bought it at 75% of the original price:\n\n\\[\n0.75 \\times 2000 = 1500\n\\]\n\nMr. Rogers then sold the watch to his friend at 120% of the price he bought it for:\n\n\\[\n1.20 \\times 1500 = 1800\n\\]\n\nThe discoun