{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Prince is a butcher. He sells 15kg of meat every hour he works, and he works 10 hours a day. His friend Bill gives him a bull that weighs 750kg. How many days will it take Prince to sell the meat from Bill's bull?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "5", "rationale": "Alright, so I need to figure out how many days it will take Prince to sell all the meat from Bill's bull. Let me break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, Prince sells meat every hour. He sells 15kg of meat each hour he works. Okay, so that means his selling rate is 15kg per hour. Now, he works 10 hours a day. So, to find out how much meat he sells in a day, I should probably multiply his hourly rate by the number of hours he works each day.\n\nLet me write that out: 15kg/hour * 10 hours/day. Hmm, 15 times 10 is 150. So, Prince sells 150kg of meat each day.\n\nNow, Bill gives Prince a bull that weighs 750kg. I need to find out how many days it will take Prince to sell all 750kg of meat. Since he sells 150kg each day, I can find the number of days by dividing the total weight of the bull by the amount he sells each day.\n\nSo, that would be 750kg divided by 150kg per day. Let me do that division: 750 \u00f7 150. Hmm, 150 times 5 is 750, so that means it will take 5 days.\n\nWait, let me double-check my calculations to make sure I didn't make a mistake. 15kg per hour times 10 hours is indeed 150kg per day. Then, 750 divided by 150 is 5. That seems right.\n\nIs there another way to think about this? Well, if Prince works 10 hours a day, and he sells 15kg each hour, then each hour he's selling 15kg, so in 10 hours, it's 15*10. Yeah, that's 150kg. So, the bull is 750kg, so 750 divided by 150 is 5 days. Okay, that makes sense.\n\nI guess another thing to consider is whether Prince can sell all the meat in whole days or if we need to account for partial days. But since the problem is asking for how many days it will take, I think it's safe to assume that he'll finish exactly on the 5th day without any leftover meat. So, I don't think we need to consider partial days here.\n\nLet me just summarize the steps:\n\n1. Prince's selling rate: 15kg/hour.\n2. Hours worked per day: 10 hours.\n3. Meat sold per day: 15kg/hour * 10 hours = 150kg/day.\n4. Total meat from bull: 750kg.\n5. Number of days needed: 750kg / 150kg/day = 5 days.\n\nYes, that all adds up. I don't see any errors in that reasoning.\n\n**Final Answer**\nIt will take Prince \\boxed{5} days to sell all the meat from Bill's bull.\n</think>\n\nPrince sells 15kg of meat every hour and works 10 hours a day. To find out how much meat he sells each day, we multiply his hourly rate by the number of hours he works:\n\n\\[\n15 \\, \\text{kg/hour} \\times 10 \\, \\text{hours/day} = 150 \\, \\text{kg/day}\n\\]\n\nBill's bull weighs 750kg. To determine how many days it will take Prince to sell all the meat, we divide the total weight of the bull by the amount he sells each day:\n\n\\[\n750 \\, \\text{kg} \\div 150 \\, \\text{kg/day} = 5 \\, \\text{days}\n\\]\n\nThus, it will take Prince \\boxed{5} days to sell all the meat from Bill's bull.", "pred_ans": "5", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Prince is a butcher. He sells 15kg of meat every hour he works, and he works 10 hours a day. His friend Bill gives him a bull that weighs 750kg. How many days will it take Prince to sell the meat from Bill's bull?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Jenny buys 1 bag of cookies a week.  The bag has 36 cookies and she puts 4 cookies in her son's lunch box 5 days a week.  Her husband eats 1 cookie a day for 7 days.  Jenny eats the rest of the cookies.  How many cookies does Jenny eat?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "9", "rationale": "Okay, so I need to figure out how many cookies Jenny eats. Let me try to break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, Jenny buys 1 bag of cookies each week. Each bag has 36 cookies. So, every week, she starts with 36 cookies.\n\nNext, she puts 4 cookies in her son's lunch box 5 days a week. Hmm, so that's 4 cookies each day for 5 days. Let me calculate how many cookies that is in total. If she does it 5 days, then 4 times 5 is 20. So, she's using 20 cookies each week for her son's lunch.\n\nThen, her husband eats 1 cookie a day for 7 days. That seems straightforward. 1 cookie per day times 7 days is 7 cookies. So, her husband eats 7 cookies each week.\n\nNow, let me add up how many cookies are being used each week. Her son is taking 20, her husband is taking 7, so that's 20 plus 7, which is 27 cookies in total per week.\n\nWait, but Jenny starts with 36 cookies. So, if 27 cookies are being used each week, how many does she have left? I subtract 27 from 36, which gives me 36 minus 27 equals 9 cookies. So, Jenny has 9 cookies left at the end of each week.\n\nThe problem says that Jenny eats the rest of the cookies. So, those 9 cookies are what she eats. Therefore, Jenny eats 9 cookies each week.\n\nLet me just double-check my calculations to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Starting with 36 cookies. She uses 4 cookies a day for 5 days, which is 20. Her husband eats 1 cookie a day for 7 days, which is 7. So, total used is 20 + 7 = 27. Subtract that from 36, and 36 - 27 is indeed 9. So, Jenny eats 9 cookies. That seems right.\n\nI don't think I missed anything. Each part of the problem is addressed: the number of cookies in the bag, how many she uses for her son, how many her husband eats, and what she has left to eat herself. Yeah, I think 9 is the correct number.\n\n**Final Answer**\nJenny eats \\boxed{9} cookies.\n</think>\n\nJenny buys 1 bag of cookies each week, containing 36 cookies. She puts 4 cookies in her son's lunch box 5 days a week, which totals 4 \u00d7 5 = 20 cookies. Her husband eats 1 cookie a day for 7 days, which totals 1 \u00d7 7 = 7 cookies. \n\nThe total number of cookies used each week is 20 (for her son) + 7 (for her husband) = 27 cookies. Subtracting this from the total number of cookies Jenny has, we get 36 - 27 = 9 cookies. Therefore, Jenny eats 9 cookies each week.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]", "pred_ans": "9", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Jenny buys 1 bag of cookies a week.  The bag has 36 cookies and she puts 4 cookies in her son's lunch box 5 days a week.  Her husband eats 1 cookie a day for 7 days.  Jenny eats the rest of the cookies.  How many cookies does Jenny eat?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
