ed the problem? Let me read it again. \"Roy has saved 40% more in money earned by chores than his brother Anthony.\" So, that does mean Roy has 140% of Anthony's amount. \"Anthony has saved $10.00 more than their sister Eva.\" So, Anthony is $10 more than Eva. \"Eva has saved $20.00.\" So, yes, that seems correct.\n\nWait, another way to look at it is: 40% more than Anthony. So, if Anthony has saved X, Roy has saved 1.4X. So, if X is $30, then 1.4 times 30 is 42.\n\nAlternatively, if I were to represent this with variables, let me try that.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nEva's savings = E\nAnthony's savings = A\nRoy's savings = R\n\nGiven that:\n\nEva has saved $20.00, so E = $20.00.\n\nAnthony has saved $10 more than Eva, so A = E + $10.00 = $20 + $10 = $30.00.\n\nRoy has saved 40% more than Anthony. So, R = A + 40% of A = A * (1 + 0.40) = A * 1.40.\n\nSo, plugging in A = $30.00, we have R = 30 * 1.40 = $42.00.\n\nYep, same result.\n\nI think that's solid. So, Roy has saved $42.00.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{42}\n</think>\n\nEva has saved $20.00. Anthony has saved $10.00 more than Eva, so Anthony's savings are:\n\\[ 20.00 + 10.00 = 30.00 \\]\n\nRoy has saved 40% more than Anthony. To find 40% of Anthony's savings:\n\\[ 0.40 \\times 30.00 = 12.00 \\]\n\nAdding this to Anthony's savings gives Roy's total savings:\n\\[ 30.00 + 12.00 = 42.00 \\]\n\nThus, Roy has saved:\n\\[\n\\boxed{42}\n\\]", "pred_ans": "42", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Roy has saved 40% more in money earned by chores than his brother Anthony.  Anthony has saved $10.00 more than their sister Eva.   Eva has saved $20.00.  How much money does Roy have?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: On Mondays, Wednesdays, and Fridays, college student Kimo has three 1-hour  classes each day.  On Tuesdays and Thursdays, he has two 2-hour classes each day.  In one semester, there are 16 weeks of school.  In one semester, how many hours does Kimo spend attending classes?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "272", "rationale": "Okay, so I need to figure out how many hours Kimo spends attending classes in one semester. Let me break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let's understand the problem. Kimo is a college student, and his schedule varies depending on the day of the week. On Mondays, Wednesdays, and Fridays, he has three 1-hour classes each day. That means each of these three days, he's in class for 3 hours. On the other hand, on Tuesdays and Thursdays, he has two 2-hour classes each day. So, each of these two days, he's in class for 4 hours because 2 times 2 is 4.\n\nNow, the semester has 16 weeks. So, I need to calculate how many hours he spends in classes each week and then multiply that by 16 to get the total for the semester.\n\nLet me write down the details:\n\n- Days with three 1-hour classes: Monday, Wednesday, Friday. So, that's three days a week.\n- Days with two 2-hour classes: Tuesday, Thursday. That's two days a week.\n\nFirst, let me calculate the total hours he spends in class per week.\n\nFor the days with three 1-hour classes:\n- Each of these three days: 3 hours.\n- So, per week, that's 3 days * 3 hours/day = 9 hours.\n\nFor the days with two 2-hour classes:\n- Each of these two days: 2 hours * 2 = 4 hours.\n- So, per week, that's 2 days * 4 hours/day = 8 hours.\n\nNow, adding both parts together to get the total hours per week:\n- 9 hours (from M, W, F) + 8 hours (from T, Th) = 17 hours/week.\n\nWait, that seems a bit high, but let me double-check. Three days with 3 hours each: 3*3=9. Two days with 4 hours each: 2*4=8. Yes, 9+8=17 hours per week.\n\nNow, since there are 16 weeks in a semester, I need to multiply the weekly hours by 16 to get the total hours for the semester.\n\nSo, total hours = 17 hours/week * 16 weeks.\n\nLet me compute that: 17 * 16.\n\nHmm, 17 times 16. I can think of 16 as 10 + 6, so 17*10=170 and 17*6=102. Then, 170+102=272.\n\nSo, 17*16=272.\n\nWait, is that correct? Let me verify with another method. 17*16: 17 multiplied by 10 is 170, and 17 multiplied by 6 is 102. Adding 170 and 102 gives 272. Yes, that seems right.\n\nTherefore, Kimo spends 272 hours in classes in one semester.\n\nBut just to make sure I didn't make a mistake in the initial calculations, let me recap:\n\n- 3 days a week with 3 hours each: 3*3=9.\n- 2 days a week with 4 hours each: 2*4=8.\n- Total per week: 9+8=17.\n- 17 hours/week *16 weeks=272 hours.\n\nYes, that all adds up correctly. I don't see any errors in the reasoning or calculations.\n\n**Final Answer**\nKimo spends \\boxed{272} hours attending classes in one semester.\n</think>\n\nKimo is a college student with a varying schedule of classes. On Mondays, Wednesdays, and Fridays, he has three 1-hour classes each day. On Tuesdays and Thursdays, he has two 2-hour classes each day. \n\nTo determine the total number of hours he spends attending classes in one semester, we first calculate the hours per week:\n\n- For three days (M, W, F) with 3 hours each:\n  \\[\n  3 \\text{ days} \\times 3 \\text{ hours/day} = 9 \\text{ hours}\n  \\]\n\n- For two days (T, Th) with 2 hours each:\n  \\[\n  2 \\text{ days} \\times 2 \\times 2 \\text{ hours/day} = 8 \\text{ hours}\n  \\]\n\nAdding these together gives the total hours per week:\n\\[\n9 \\text{ hours} + 8 \\text{ hours} = 17 \\text{ hours/week}\n\\]\n\nGiven that the semester has 16 weeks, we multiply the weekly hours by 16:\n\\[\n17 \\text{ hours/week} \\times 16 \\text{ weeks} = 272 \\text{ hours}\n\\]\n\nThus, Kimo spends \\boxed{272} hours attending classes in one semester.", "pred_ans": "272", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: On Mondays, Wednesdays, and Fridays, college student Kimo has three 1-hour  classes each day.  On Tuesdays and Thursdays, he has two 2-hour classes each day.  In one semester, there are 16 weeks of school.  In one semester, how many hours does Kimo spend attending classes?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Jon runs a triathlon.  It takes him 40 minutes for the swim, an hour and 20 minutes for the bike ride and 50 minutes for the run.  Compared to Jon, James finishes the swim 10% faster but takes 5 minutes longer on the bike.  If Jon won by 10 minutes, how long did it take James to do the run?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "59", "rationale": "Alright, so I need to figure out how long it took James to do the run in this triathlon problem. Let's see, the problem is about Jon and James competing in a triathlon, which includes swimming, biking, and running. I need to compare their times and find out the time James took for the run.\n\nFirst, let's list down the information given. For Jon, the times are: swimming is 40 minutes, biking is an hour and 20 minutes, and running is 50 minutes. Then, for James, compared to Jon, he finishes the swim 10% faster but takes 5 minutes longer on the bike. Also, Jon won by 10 minutes. So, I need to find out how long James took for the run.\n\nHmm, okay. Let me try to break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, maybe I should figure out how long James took for each part of the triathlon and then see how his total time compares to Jon's.\n\nStarting with the swim. Jon took 40 minutes. James finishes the swim 10% faster. So, 10% faster than Jon's time. Let me calculate that. 10% of 40 minutes is 4 minutes. So, James's time for the swim is 40 minus 4, which is 36 minutes. So, James takes 36 minutes for the swim.\n\nNext, the bike ride. Jon took an hour and 20 minutes. Let me convert that to minutes for easier calculation. An hour is 60 minutes, so 60 + 20 = 80 minutes. So, Jon took 80 minutes on the bike ride. Now, James takes 5 minutes longer on the bike compared to Jon. So, James's time for the bike ride is 80 + 5 = 85 minutes.\n\nSo far, we have:\n- Jon: Swim - 40 minutes, Bike - 80 minutes, Run - 50 minutes.\n- James: Swim - 36 minutes, Bike - 85 minutes.\n\nNow, we need to figure out James's run time. For that, we might need to find out the total time each took and then see the difference, which is given as Jon won by 10 minutes. So, if I can find the total time for both and set the difference to 10 minutes, I can solve for James's run time.\n\nLet's denote James's run time as R. So, James's total time is 36 (swim) + 85 (bike) + R (run). Let me compute this: 36 + 85 is 121, so his total time is 121 + R minutes.\n\nJon's total time is 40 (swim) + 80 (bike) + 50 (run). Let me add that: 40 + 80 is 120, plus 50 is 170 minutes. So, Jon's total time is 170 minutes.\n\nIt's given that Jon won by 10 minutes. So, James's total time is 10 minutes longer than Jon's? Wait, hold on. If Jon won by 10 minutes, that would mean Jon finished 10 minutes before James. So, James's total time is 10 minutes longer than Jon's. So, James's total time is 170 + 10 = 180 minutes.\n\nWait, let me double-check that. If Jon won by 10 minutes, then yes, James took longer. So, James's total time is 180 minutes.\n\nSo, we have James's total time as 36 + 85 + R = 121 + R = 180. Therefore, 121 + R = 180. So, R = 180 - 121 = 59 minutes.\n\nSo, James took 59 minutes for the run.\n\nWait, let me quickly verify to make sure I didn't make any mistakes.\n\nSo, James's swim is 36 minutes, bike is 85 minutes, run is 59 minutes. Total is 36 + 85 = 121, plus 59 is 180 minutes. Jon's total is 170 minutes. So, James took 10 minutes longer, which makes sense. So, that's correct.\n\nSo, the answer is 59 minutes.\n\nBut just to make sure, let me re-examine the problem.\n\n\"Jon runs a triathlon. It takes him 40 minutes for the swim, an hour and 20 minutes for the bike ride, and 50 minutes for the run. Compared to Jon, James finishes the swim 10% faster but takes 5 minutes longer on the bike. If Jon won by 10 minutes, how long did it take James to do the run?\"\n\nSo, swim time for James is 10% faster. So, 10% of 40 is 4, so 40 - 4 = 36. Correct.\n\nBike time: 5 minutes longer, so 80 + 50? Wait, no, no. Wait, no, 80 minutes plus 5 minutes is 85. Right.\n\nThen, total time for James: 36 + 85 + R = 121 + R.\n\nTotal time for Jon: 40 + 80 + 50 = 170.\n\nGiven that James's total time is 10 minutes longer, so 170 + 10 = 180. Therefore, 121 + R = 180. So, R = 59. So, 59 minutes.\n\nWait, but let me think about another way. Maybe the difference in bike time is 5 minutes, but is that 5 minutes longer, so yes, 85.\n\nWait, is it possible that the 10% faster is 10% of the time, not 10% of the distance? Hmm, but since it's time, it's 10% of the time.\n\nSo, 10% of 40 minutes is indeed 4 minutes, so 36 minutes.\n\nSo, I think that's correct.\n\nSo, after verifying, yes, James took 59 minutes for the run.\n\n**Final Answer**\nIt took James \\boxed{59} minutes to do the run.\n</think>\n\nFirst, we determine the time James took for each segment of the triathlon compared to Jon:\n\n1. **Swim**: Jon took 40 minutes. James finished the swim 10% faster.\n   - 10% of 40 minutes is 4 minutes.\n   - Therefore, James's swim time is \\(40 - 4 = 36\\) minutes.\n\n2. **Bike Ride**: Jon took 80 minutes. James took 5 minutes longer.\n   - Therefore, James's bike time is \\(80 + 5 = 85\\) minutes.\n\nNext, we calculate the total time for both Jon and James:\n\n- **Jon's total time**:\n  - Swim: 40 minutes\n  - Bike: 80 minutes\n  - Run: 50 minutes\n  - Total: \\(40 + 80 + 50 = 170\\) minutes\n\n- **James's total time**:\n  - Swim: 36 minutes\n  - Bike: 85 minutes\n  - Run: \\(R\\) minutes (unknown)\n  - Total: \\(36 + 85 + R = 121 + R\\) minutes\n\nGiven that Jon won by 10 minutes, James's total time is 10 minutes longer than Jon's:\n  - James's total time: \\(170 + 10 = 180\\) minutes\n\nSetting up the equation for James's total time:\n  - \\(121 + R = 180\\)\n  - Solving for \\(R\\): \\(R = 180 - 121 = 59\\) minutes\n\nThus, it took James \\(\\boxed{59}\\) minutes to do the run.", "pred_ans": "59", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Jon runs a triathlon.  It takes him 40 minutes for the swim, an hour and 20 minutes for the bike ride and 50 minutes for the run.  Compared to Jon, James finishes the swim 10% faster but takes 5 minutes longer on the bike.  If Jon won by 10 minutes, how long did it take James to do the run?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: In the city of Soda, there are exactly 23786 inhabitants. They include 8417 men and 9092 women. The rest of the population is made up of children. How many kids are there in Soda?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "6277", "rationale": "Okay, so I need to figure out how many kids are in Soda. Let me try to understand the problem step by step. \n\nFirst, the total population of Soda is 23,786 inhabitants. Out of these, there are 8,417 men and 9,092 women. The rest of the population is made up of children. So, the kids are what's left after subtracting the number of men and women from the total population. \n\nHmm, that makes sense. So, if I add the number of men and women together, that should give me the total number of adults in Soda. Then, subtracting that from the total population will give me the number of children. \n\nLet me write that down:\n\nTotal population = Men + Women + Children\n\nSo, Children = Total population - Men - Women\n\nAlright, so plugging in the numbers:\n\nTotal population = 23,786\n\nMen = 8,417\n\nWomen = 9,092\n\nSo, let me add Men and Women first. Let me do that step by step to avoid mistakes.\n\n8,417 (men) + 9,092 (women)\n\nLet me add the thousands first: 8,000 + 9,000 = 17,000\n\nThen the hundreds: 400 + 90 = 490\n\nThen the tens: 10 + 2 = 12\n\nWait, that doesn't seem right. Wait, 8,417 is 8,000 + 400 + 10 + 7, and 9,092 is 9,000 + 90 + 2. So, when adding them:\n\nStart with 8,000 + 9,000 = 17,000\n\nThen 400 + 90 = 490\n\nThen 10 + 2 = 12\n\nThen 7 + 0 = 7\n\nWait, no, actually, that's not the correct way. Wait, no, I think I'm overcomplicating it. Maybe I should just add them digit by digit.\n\nLet me write them one under the other:\n\n8,417\n\n+9,092\n\n------\n\nLet me add the units place: 7 + 2 = 9\n\nTens place: 1 + 9 = 10, so I put down 0 and carry over 1.\n\nHundreds place: 4 + 0 = 4, plus the carried 1 makes 5.\n\nThousands place: 8 + 9 = 17.\n\nSo, combining that: 17,000 + 500 + 10 + 9? Wait, no, wait, the way we add:\n\nWait, 8,417 + 9,092:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n7 + 2 = 9\n\n1 + 9 = 10, so write 0, carryover 1\n\n4 + 0 = 4, plus 1 carried over is 5\n\n8 + 9 = 17\n\nSo, putting it all together: 17,509. \n\nWait, that seems a bit off. Let me check again:\n\n8,417\n\n+9,092\n\nAdding units: 7 + 2 = 9\n\nTens: 1 + 9 = 10, so carryover 1\n\nHundreds: 4 + 0 + 1 = 5\n\nThousands: 8 + 9 = 17\n\nSo, the total is 17,509. Hmm, okay, that seems correct.\n\nSo, total adults are 17,509.\n\nTherefore, the number of children is total population minus adults.\n\nTotal population is 23,786.\n\nSo, 23,786 - 17,509 = ?\n\nLet me compute that.\n\n23,786 - 17,509\n\nSubtracting 17,000 from 23,786 gives 6,786\n\nThen subtract the remaining 509: 6,786 - 509\n\nWait, 6,786 - 500 = 6,286\n\nThen subtract 9 more: 6,286 - 9 = 6,277\n\nWait, so 23,786 - 17,509 is 6,277.\n\nSo, the number of children is 6,277.\n\nWait, let me verify that subtraction again.\n\n23,786 minus 17,509:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n6 - 9: Can't do that, so borrow 1 from the tens place.\n\nTens place is 8, so it becomes 7, and the units place becomes 16.\n\n16 - 9 = 7\n\nNow, tens place: 7 - 0 = 7\n\nHundreds place: 7 - 5 = 2\n\nThousands place: 3 - 7: Can't do that, so borrow 1 from the ten-thousands place.\n\nTen-thousands place: 2 becomes 1, and the thousands place becomes 13.\n\n13 - 7 = 6\n\nSo, the result is 6,277.\n\nYes, that seems correct.\n\nSo, the number of children in Soda is 6,277.\n\nI think that's the solution. But let me double-check my steps.\n\n1. Total population: 23,786\n\n2. Number of men: 8,417\n\n3. Number of women: 9,092\n\n4. Total adults = men + women = 8,417 + 9,092 = 17,509\n\n5. Children = total population - adults = 23,786 - 17,509 = 6,277\n\nYes, that makes sense.\n\nI don't see any mistakes in the calculations, so I think 6,277 is the correct number of children in Soda.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of kids in Soda is \\boxed{6277}.\n</think>\n\nTo determine the number of children in Soda, we start with the total population and subtract the number of men and women.\n\n1. Total population: 23,786\n2. Number of men: 8,417\n3. Number of women: 9,092\n\nFirst, we add the number of men and women:\n\n\\[ 8,417 + 9,092 = 17,509 \\]\n\nNext, we subtract the total number of adults (men and women) from the total population to find the number of children:\n\n\\[ 23,786 - 17,509 = 6,277 \\]\n\nThus, the number of children in Soda is \\(\\boxed{6277}\\).", "pred_ans": "6277", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: In the city of Soda, there are exactly 23786 inhabitants. They include 8417 men and 9092 women. The rest of the population is made up of children. How many kids are there in Soda?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
