is 90. Correct.\n\nYes, seems solid.\n\nWait, maybe I confused lollipops bought and sold? Let me just make sure.\n\nThey bought the lollipops for $0.5 each and sold them for $0.8 each. So, per lollipop, the profit is 0.8 - 0.5 = $0.3. That's correct.\n\nEach student sold 10 lollipops, so profit per student is 10 * 0.3 = $3. 30 students, so 30 * 3 = $90. Correct.\n\nYes, that's the same way as before.\n\nAlternatively, thinking in terms of total lollipops, the total profit is the profit per lollipop times the number sold.\n\nTotal profit = (0.8 - 0.5) * 300 = 0.3 * 300 = 90. So, same answer.\n\nTherefore, I think I can confidently say that the class raised $90 from the profit of selling lollipops.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe class was able to raise a total of \\boxed{90} dollars from the profit of selling lollipops.\n</think>\n\nTo determine how much money the class was able to raise from the profit of selling lollipops, we need to calculate both the total revenue and the total cost, and then find the profit by subtracting the total cost from the total revenue.\n\n1. **Calculate total revenue:**\n   - Each student sold 10 lollipops.\n   - There are 30 students, so the total number of lollipops sold is:\n     \\[\n     30 \\text{ students} \\times 10 \\text{ lollipops/student} = 300 \\text{ lollipops}\n     \\]\n   - The revenue from selling each lollipop is $0.8, so the total revenue is:\n     \\[\n     300 \\text{ lollipops} \\times \\$0.8/\\text{lollypop} = \\$240\n     \\]\n\n2. **Calculate total cost:**\n   - Each student bought 10 lollipops at $0.5 each.\n   - The cost per student is:\n     \\[\n     10 \\text{ lollipops} \\times \\$0.5/\\text{lollypop} = \\$5\n     \\]\n   - The total cost for all the lollipops is:\n     \\[\n     30 \\text{ students} \\times \\$5/\\text{student} = \\$150\n     \\]\n\n3. **Calculate profit:**\n   - The profit is the total revenue minus the total cost:\n     \\[\n     \\$240 - \\$150 = \\$90\n     \\]\n\nThus, the class was able to raise a total of \\boxed{90} dollars from the profit of selling lollipops.", "pred_ans": "90", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: To raise money for their class fund, each of the 30 students from one class sold lollipops that cost $0.8 per lollypop. On average, each student sold 10 lollipops. If they bought the lollipops for $0.5 each, how much money was the class able to raise from the profit of selling lollipops?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
