{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Bob has a certain number of marbles. If he receives 2 dozen more marbles, he will have 60 marbles. If he loses 10 of the marbles he has, how many marbles will Bob have?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "26", "rationale": "Okay, so Bob has some marbles, and there are two scenarios given here. First, if he receives 2 dozen more marbles, he will have 60 marbles. Second, if he loses 10 marbles, we need to find out how many marbles he will have. Hmm, let me think about how to approach this.\n\nFirst, I know that a dozen is 12, so 2 dozen is 24 marbles. So when Bob gets 24 more marbles, his total becomes 60. That means the number of marbles he originally had before getting the 24 is 60 minus 24. Let me write that down.\n\nLet \\( x \\) be the number of marbles Bob originally has. Then, according to the first scenario:\n\\( x + 24 = 60 \\)\n\nSo, to find \\( x \\), I can subtract 24 from both sides:\n\\( x = 60 - 24 \\)\n\\( x = 36 \\)\n\nAlright, so Bob originally has 36 marbles. Now, the second part of the problem says if he loses 10 marbles, how many will he have? So, we take the original number of marbles and subtract 10.\n\nSo, that would be:\n\\( 36 - 10 = 26 \\)\n\nTherefore, Bob will have 26 marbles after losing 10.\n\nWait, let me double-check to make sure I didn't make a mistake. If he starts with 36 marbles and gets 24 more, that's 60. Then, if he loses 10, he goes back down to 26. That seems right.\n\nIs there another way to think about this? Maybe, but I think this straightforward method is correct. I don't see any errors in the calculations. So, yeah, the answer should be 26 marbles.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of marbles Bob will have is \\boxed{26}.\n</think>\n\nLet \\( x \\) be the number of marbles Bob originally has. \n\nAccording to the first scenario, if Bob receives 2 dozen (which is 24) marbles, he will have 60 marbles. This can be written as:\n\\[ x + 24 = 60 \\]\n\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = 60 - 24 \\]\n\\[ x = 36 \\]\n\nSo, Bob originally has 36 marbles. \n\nIn the second scenario, if Bob loses 10 marbles, the number of marbles he will have is:\n\\[ 36 - 10 = 26 \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of marbles Bob will have is \\boxed{26}.", "pred_ans": "26", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Bob has a certain number of marbles. If he receives 2 dozen more marbles, he will have 60 marbles. If he loses 10 of the marbles he has, how many marbles will Bob have?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
