{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: It's strawberry-picking time on Grandma Concetta's farm.  Tony can pick 6 quarts of strawberries per hour, while Bobby picks one less quart of strawberries per hour than Tony.  Kathy can pick twice as many strawberries per hour as Bobby, and Ricky picks two fewer quarts of strawberries per hour than does Kathy.  In total, how many quarts of strawberries can Tony, Bobby, Ricky, and Kathy pick per hour on Grandma Concetta's farm?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "29", "rationale": "Alright, let me try to figure out how many quarts of strawberries all four of them can pick per hour. Hmm, so the problem is about calculating the total strawberry picking rate for Tony, Bobby, Kathy, and Ricky. \n\nFirst, let me break down the information given. \n\nIt says Tony can pick 6 quarts per hour. Okay, that's straightforward. So, Tony's rate is 6 quarts/hour.\n\nNext, Bobby picks one less quart per hour than Tony. Hmm, so if Tony is picking 6 quarts, Bobby must pick 6 minus 1 quarts each hour. Let me write that down. \n\nBobby's rate = Tony's rate - 1 quart/hour\nBobby's rate = 6 - 1\nBobby's rate = 5 quarts/hour.\n\nOkay, so Bobby can pick 5 quarts per hour.\n\nMoving on, Kathy can pick twice as many strawberries per hour as Bobby. So, I need to find Bobby's rate first, which we already have as 5 quarts/hour. So, twice as many as Bobby would be 2 multiplied by Bobby's rate. \n\nKathy's rate = 2 * Bobby's rate\nKathy's rate = 2 * 5\nKathy's rate = 10 quarts/hour.\n\nGot it. Kathy can pick 10 quarts per hour.\n\nThen, it says Ricky picks two fewer quarts per hour than Kathy. So, similar to how Bobby was one less than Tony, Ricky is two less than Kathy. Since Kathy can pick 10 quarts, Ricky can pick 10 minus 2 quarts per hour.\n\nRicky's rate = Kathy's rate - 2 quarts/hour\nRicky's rate = 10 - 2\nRicky's rate = 8 quarts/hour.\n\nAlright, so Ricky can pick 8 quarts per hour.