\u00d7 4 = 96.\n\nThen, 20 not working, so 96 - 20 = 76.\n\nSame result. So that seems consistent.\n\nAlternatively, could I have messed up the number of streetlights per pole? Let me make sure.\n\nThe problem says each pole has 4 streetlights. So, per pole, 4 streetlights. So, that's correct.\n\nEach intersection has 6 poles. So, 6 poles \u00d7 4 streetlights each = 24 streetlights per intersection.\n\n4 intersections \u00d7 24 streetlights = 96. Correct.\n\n20 not working, so 76 working. So, I think that's right.\n\nWait, is there any possibility that the 20 non-working streetlights are per intersection or something? The problem says \"20 streetlights from the total number are not working.\" So, that means 20 in total across all intersections.\n\nSo, yes, we just subtract 20 from the total of 96, giving 76.\n\nI think I've double-checked this thoroughly. So, the total number of functioning streetlights is 76.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe total number of functioning street lights is \\boxed{76}.\n</think>\n\nTo calculate the total number of functioning street lights, we start by determining the total number of street lights. Each pole has 4 street lights, each intersection has 6 poles, and there are 4 intersections.\n\n1. Calculate the number of street lights per intersection:\n   \\[\n   4 \\text{ streetlights per pole} \\times 6 \\text{ poles per intersection} = 24 \\text{ streetlights per intersection}\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate the total number of street lights across all intersections:\n   \\[\n   24 \\text{ streetlights per intersection} \\times 4 \\text{ intersections} = 96 \\text{ total streetlights}\n   \\]\n\n3. Subtract the number of non-working street lights from the total:\n   \\[\n   96 \\text{ total streetlights} - 20 \\text{ non-working streetlights} = 76 \\text{ functioning streetlights}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the total number of functioning street lights is \\boxed{76}.", "pred_ans": "76", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Each pole on a road intersection has 4 street lights. If the number of poles at each intersection is 6, and the road has 4 intersections, calculate the total number of functioning street lights if 20 streetlights from the total number are not working.\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
