{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Sarah\u2019s basketball games are 4 quarters that are each 12 minutes long. In the last quarter, there was a tie so the game was extended for five minutes. How long did the entire game last?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "53", "rationale": "Okay, so Sarah has a basketball game that's divided into four quarters, each lasting 12 minutes. That part seems straightforward. But then, in the last quarter, there was a tie, which means the game went into overtime. They extended the game for five extra minutes. Hmm, I need to figure out how long the entire game lasted. Let me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, let's think about the regular parts of the game. There are four quarters, each 12 minutes. So, normally, without any overtime, the game would last 4 times 12 minutes. Let me calculate that. 4 multiplied by 12 is 48 minutes. So, under normal circumstances, the game is 48 minutes long.\n\nBut wait, in the last quarter, there was a tie. That's why they had to extend the game. So, that means the game didn't end after the usual 12 minutes for the fourth quarter. Instead, they played for an additional five minutes. So, for the last quarter, instead of just 12 minutes, it was 12 plus 5 minutes. Let me add those together. 12 plus 5 is 17 minutes. So, the last quarter took 17 minutes in total.\n\nNow, to find the total duration of the entire game, I need to add up all the minutes from the first three quarters and then add the extended time from the fourth quarter. The first three quarters are each 12 minutes, so that's 3 times 12. Let me compute that. 3 times 12 is 36 minutes. Then, as we established, the fourth quarter took 17 minutes. So, I need to add 36 and 17 together to get the total game time.\n\nAdding 36 and 17... Hmm, 36 plus 10 is 46, and then plus 7 is 53. So, the total game lasted 53 minutes.\n\nJust to make sure I didn't make a mistake, let me double-check my calculations. Four quarters at 12 minutes each: 4 x 12 is 48. Then, the last quarter was extended by 5 minutes, making it 17 minutes. So, 48 plus 5 is 53. Yeah, that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, I can think about it as each quarter is 12 minutes, but the last quarter was 17 minutes because of the tiebreaker. So, adding all four quarters: 12 + 12 + 12 + 17. Let's add them step by step. 12 + 12 is 24, plus another 12 is 36, and then plus 17 is 53. Yep, same result.\n\nI don't think I made any errors here. The key was recognizing that only the last quarter was extended and adding those five minutes to the usual 12-minute duration. Then, just adding all the quarters together, keeping track of the extended time. So, the entire game lasted 53 minutes.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe entire game lasted \\boxed{53} minutes.\n</think>\n\nSarah\u2019s basketball game is divided into four quarters, each lasting 12 minutes. In the last quarter, the game was extended for five minutes due to a tie.\n\nFirst, calculate the duration of the first three quarters:\n\\[ 3 \\times 12 = 36 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nNext, calculate the duration of the fourth quarter, which was extended:\n\\[ 12 + 5 = 17 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nAdd the durations of all four quarters together:\n\\[ 36 + 17 = 53 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nThus, the entire game lasted \\(\\boxed{53}\\) minutes.", "pred_ans": "53", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Sarah\u2019s basketball games are 4 quarters that are each 12 minutes long. In the last quarter, there was a tie so the game was extended for five minutes. How long did the entire game last?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
