{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Maria was charging her power bank before going to the park when she disconnected it and noticed the power bank wasn\u2019t fully charged yet. Once at the park, her friends asked her if they could charge their phones. While charging her friends\u2019 phones, she noticed that her power bank was losing 9% of the total capacity each hour. 5 hours later the battery started to lose instead 7% of the total capacity each hour for about 3 hours. In the end, the charge remaining was 28%. What was the charge of the power bank when Maria went out to the park?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "94", "rationale": "Okay, so I need to figure out how much charge Maria's power bank had when she went out to the park. Let's break down the problem step by step.\n\nFirst, when Maria left her house, she noticed that the power bank wasn't fully charged. Then, while she was at the park, she started charging her friends' phones. During this time, she observed that the power bank was losing charge. Initially, it was losing 9% of its total capacity each hour. But after 5 hours, the rate of charge loss changed. For the next 3 hours, the battery was losing 7% of the total capacity each hour. In the end, the remaining charge was 28%. I need to find out what the initial charge was when she left her house.\n\nLet me denote the total capacity of the power bank as \\( C \\). The initial charge when she left was less than full, so let's call the initial charge \\( x \\). That means \\( x \\) is what we're trying to find.\n\nNow, Maria went to the park. While there, she started charging her friends' phones, but during this process, the power bank started to lose charge. So, the power bank was both charging others and losing charge itself? Hmm, that might complicate things. Wait, actually, the problem says she noticed that her power bank wasn't fully charged yet when she disconnected it. Then, while charging her friends' phones, she noticed that her power bank was losing 9% of the total capacity each hour. So, does that mean that while she was charging others, her power bank was being used to charge others, and in the process, it was losing charge? Or does it mean that she was using her power bank to charge others, which caused her own power bank to lose charge? I think it's the latter. So, when she started charging others, she was using her power bank to charge their phones, which in turn caused her own power bank to lose charge.\n\nTherefore, the power bank was being used to charge others, which means that the charge was flowing out of her power bank while it was being used. So, during the time she was charging her friends, her power bank was losing charge both because she was using it to charge others and also because of the natural discharge. Wait, no, maybe it's just that she noticed that while she was charging her friends, her own power bank was losing 9% each hour. So, perhaps the act of charging others caused her power bank to lose charge at 9% per hour.\n\nSo, for the first 5 hours, her power bank was losing 9% per hour. Then, after 5 hours, the rate changed to 7% per hour, and this lasted for 3 hours. After that, the remaining charge was 28%.\n\nSo, let's model this.\n\nLet me denote the initial charge as \\( x \\). So, she starts with \\( x \\) charge.\n\nFirst, for 5 hours, the power bank loses 9% per hour. So, each hour, it loses 9% of the current charge. So, this is an exponential decay, right? Because each hour, it's losing a percentage of the remaining charge.\n\nSo, the formula for exponential decay is:\n\n\\( C(t) = C_0 \\times (1 - r)^t \\)\n\nWhere \\( C(t) \\) is the remaining charge after \\( t \\) hours, \\( C_0 \\) is the initial charge, and \\( r \\) is the rate of decay per hour.\n\nSo, in the first 5 hours, the charge is losing 9% each hour. So, \\( r = 0.09 \\).\n\nTherefore, after the first 5 hours, the remaining charge would be