\\text{ legs per animal} = 20 \\text{ legs}\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the total number of legs from the 4-legged animals:\n\\[\n15 \\text{ animals} \\times 4 \\text{ legs per animal} = 60 \\text{ legs}\n\\]\n\nWe then add the legs from both types of animals to find the total number of legs:\n\\[\n20 \\text{ legs} + 60 \\text{ legs} = 80 \\text{ legs}\n\\]\n\nTo find the number of pairs of legs, we divide the total number of legs by 2:\n\\[\n80 \\text{ legs} \\div 2 = 40 \\text{ pairs}\n\\]\n\nThe total number of pairs of animal legs on the farm is \\boxed{40}.", "pred_ans": "40", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: A farm has 10 2-legged animals and 15 4-legged animals. If all animals are healthy, how many pairs of animal legs in total are on the farm?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
