{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Janet gets a job as a waitress.  She makes $10 an hour from wages and another $15 an hour from tips.  She wants to save up 20% of the cost of a $10000 car for a downpayment.  If she works 40 hours a week how many weeks will she need to work to save the down payment?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "2", "rationale": "Okay, so Janet got a new job as a waitress, and she's trying to save up for a car. Hmm, I need to figure out how many weeks she has to work to save the down payment. Let me break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, the problem says she makes $10 an hour from her wages and another $15 an hour from tips. So, I guess her total earnings each hour are the sum of her wages and tips. Let me calculate that.\n\nTotal earnings per hour = Wages + Tips = $10 + $15 = $25 per hour.\n\nAlright, so she earns $25 every hour. Now, she wants to save 20% of the cost of a $10,000 car for a down payment. I need to find out how much that down payment is going to be.\n\nDown payment = 20% of $10,000.\n\nHmm, 20% is the same as 0.20 in decimal. So, let me compute that.\n\nDown payment = 0.20 * $10,000 = $2,000.\n\nSo, she needs to save $2,000 for the down payment. Got it.\n\nNow, she works 40 hours a week. I need to figure out how many weeks she has to work to earn $2,000. For that, I should first find her total earnings per week and then divide the total amount she needs by her weekly earnings.\n\nTotal earnings per week = Hours per week * Earnings per hour = 40 hours * $25 per hour.\n\nLet me calculate that.\n\n40 * 25 = 1,000.\n\nSo, she makes $1,000 each week. Hmm, that's pretty good!\n\nNow, she needs $2,000. So, the number of weeks she needs to work is the total amount divided by her weekly earnings.\n\nNumber of weeks = Total down payment needed / Weekly earnings = $2,000 / $1,000 per week.\n\n2,000 divided by 1,000 is 2. So, she needs to work 2 weeks.\n\nWait, let me double-check that. If she works 40 hours a week at $25 an hour, that's $1,000 a week. So, in two weeks, she'd make $2,000. Yep, that makes sense.\n\nBut just to make sure I didn't miss anything, let me go through the steps again.\n\n1. Total hourly wage: $10 + $15 = $25.\n2. She wants to save 20% of $10,000, which is 0.20 * 10,000 = $2,000.\n3. She works 40 hours a week, so her weekly income is 40 * 25 = $1,000.\n4. Number of weeks needed: 2,000 / 1,000 = 2 weeks.\n\nEverything seems to add up. I don't see any mistakes here. So, yeah, she needs to work for 2 weeks to save the down payment.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{2}\n</think>\n\nJanet's total earnings per hour are the sum of her wages and tips:\n\\[\n\\text{Total earnings per hour} = \\$10 + \\$15 = \\$25 \\text{ per hour}\n\\]\n\nShe wants to save 20% of the cost of a \\$10,000 car for a down payment:\n\\[\n\\text{Down payment} = 0.20 \\times \\$10,000 = \\$2,000\n\\]\n\nJanet works 40 hours a week, so her total earnings per week are:\n\\[\n\\text{Total earnings per week} = 40 \\text{ hours} \\times \\$25 \\text{ per hour} = \\$1,000 \\text{ per week}\n\\]\n\nThe number of weeks she needs to work to save the down payment is:\n\\[\n\\text{Number of weeks} = \\frac{\\$2,000}{\\$1,000 \\text{ per week}} = 2\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]", "pred_ans": "2", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Janet gets a job as a waitress.  She makes $10 an hour from wages and another $15 an hour from tips.  She wants to save up 20% of the cost of a $10000 car for a downpayment.  If she works 40 hours a week how many weeks will she need to work to save the down payment?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
