40.\n\nNumber of sessions per week: 3.\n\nWeekly earnings: 40 * 3 = $120.\n\nNumber of weeks in a month: 4.\n\nMonthly earnings: 120 * 4 = $480.\n\nLooks correct. So, the dance studio earns $480 in a month.\n\nBut just to make sure I didn't misinterpret anything, let me re-examine the problem.\n\nIt says it costs $25 per session to rent the studio plus $1.50 per student per session. So, yes, fixed plus variable.\n\nThey have 10 students, so per session, the variable cost is 10 * 1.50 = 15.\n\nRented 3 days a week, so 3 sessions.\n\nSo, per week: 25 + 15 * 3. Wait, hold on, is it 15 per session or is it 15 per week?\n\nNo, wait, the $1.50 is per student per session, so per session, it's 10 * 1.50, which is $15 per session.\n\nSo, per session, total cost is $25 + $15 = $40.\n\nTherefore, per week, 3 sessions, so 3 * $40 = $120.\n\nAnd a month is 4 weeks, so 4 * $120 = $480.\n\nYes, that seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, I can think in terms of total earnings for the month.\n\nEach session, they make 10 students * $1.50 = $15 per student, plus $25 for the studio.\n\nSo, per session, they earn $15 + $25 = $40.\n\nThey have 3 sessions a week, so 3 * $40 = $120 per week.\n\nIn a month, which is 4 weeks, 4 * $120 = $480.\n\nSo, both ways, same result.\n\nI don't think I made any mistakes here. It seems straightforward.\n\nSo, the dance studio's expected earnings in a month are $480.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe dance studio's expected monthly earnings are \\boxed{480} dollars.\n</think>\n\nThe dance studio charges $25 per session for renting the studio and $1.50 per student per session. They have 10 students and rent the studio 3 days a week.\n\nFirst, calculate the variable cost per session:\n\\[ 10 \\text{ students} \\times 1.50 \\text{ dollars/student/session} = 15 \\text{ dollars/session} \\]\n\nAdd the fixed cost for renting the studio:\n\\[ 25 \\text{ dollars/session} + 15 \\text{ dollars/session} = 40 \\text{ dollars/session} \\]\n\nNext, calculate the weekly earnings:\n\\[ 3 \\text{ sessions/week} \\times 40 \\text{ dollars/session} = 120 \\text{ dollars/week} \\]\n\nAssuming a month is 4 weeks, calculate the monthly earnings:\n\\[ 4 \\text{ weeks/month} \\times 120 \\text{ dollars/week} = 480 \\text{ dollars/month} \\]\n\nThe dance studio's expected monthly earnings are \\boxed{480} dollars.", "pred_ans": "480", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: A dance studio offers lessons to students. It costs $25 per session to rent the studio plus $1.50 per student per session. At present, the dance studio has 10 students and is rented 3 days a week. How much are the expected earnings of the dance studio in a month?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Nick is choosing between two jobs. Job A pays $15 an hour for 2000 hours a year, and is in a state with a 20% total tax rate. Job B pays $42,000 a year and is in a state that charges $6,000 in property tax and a 10% tax rate on net income after property tax. How much more money will Nick make at the job with a higher net pay rate, compared to the other job?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "8400", "rationale": "Alright, so Nick has to choose between two jobs, and he needs to figure out which one pays him more after taxes. Hmm, okay, let me try to break this down step by step. I think the first thing to do is to calculate the gross pay for each job and then figure out how much taxes he'll have to pay on each. Once I have the net pay for both, I can compare them and see which one is better for Nick.\n\nStarting with Job A: It pays $15 an hour for 2000 hours a year. Let me calculate the annual gross pay first. So, $15 per hour times 2000 hours. Hmm, 15 times 2000... let me do that math. 15 times 2000 is 30,000. So, Job A gives him a gross income of $30,000 a year.\n\nNow, the state where Job A is located has a 20% total tax rate. I assume this is a combined tax rate, maybe income tax and something else like sales tax or property tax. But the problem doesn't specify, so I'll just go with 20% total tax rate. So, to find out how much tax he has to pay, I need to calculate 20% of $30,000.\n\n20% of 30,000 is... well, 10% is 3,000, so 20% is double that, which is $6,000. So, his total tax for Job A is $6,000. Therefore, his net pay after taxes would be the gross pay minus the tax. That's $30,000 minus $6,000, which is $24,000. Okay, so Nick would make $24,000 a year with Job A after taxes.\n\nMoving on to Job B: It pays $42,000 a year. But there's more to consider here. The state has a property tax of $6,000 and a 10% tax rate on net income after property tax. Hmm, 