 by 1/2 each time, but the last step is subtracting 270. So, it's not a continuous 1/2, but two multiplications by 1/2 and then a subtraction.\n\nSo, 270 is equal to the leftover after two halvings, which is (1/2 * 1/2) X = 1/4 X. So, 1/4 X = 270, so X = 1080.\n\nTherefore, I think 1080 is correct. So, the triceratops had 1080 kilograms of meat before the T-Rex ate it.\n\nWait, but just to test it, let me do the forward calculations:\n\nStart with 1080 kg.\n\nT-Rex eats half: 1080 / 2 = 540 eaten. So, 1080 - 540 = 540 left.\n\nThen, velociraptors eat half of 540: 540 / 2 = 270 eaten. So, 540 - 270 = 270 left.\n\nThen, Allosaurus eats the last 270. So, 270 - 270 = 0.\n\nPerfect, that matches the problem statement. So, 1080 kg is the original amount.\n\nTherefore, I think my initial answer is correct. I got confused for a moment when I considered whether it was three multiplications by 1/2, but in reality, the last step is an absolute subtraction, not a multiplication. So, that's why it's only two multiplications by 1/2 and then subtracting 270.\n\nSo, to summarize:\n\n1. Let X = original amount of meat.\n\n2. After T-Rex: X / 2 remains.\n\n3. After raptors: (X / 2) / 2 = X / 4 remains.\n\n4. After Allosaurus: (X / 4) - 270 = 0.\n\nThus, X / 4 = 270, so X = 1080.\n\nYes, that makes sense.\n\nAlternatively, if I wanted to model it as a mathematical equation:\n\nLet X be the original amount.\n\nAmount after T-Rex: X - (X / 2) = X / 2.\n\nAmount after raptors: (X / 2) - ( (X / 2) / 2 ) = X / 2 - X / 4 = X / 4.\n\nAmount after Allosaurus: X / 4 - 270 = 0.\n\nSo, X / 4 = 270 => X = 270 * 4 = 1080.\n\nEither way, same result.\n\nAnother way to think about it is to reverse the operations. Since the Allosaurus ate the last 270, which was 1/4 of the meat after the raptors ate. So, 270 is 1/4 of what was there after raptors. So, to find out how much was there after raptors, it's 270 * 4 = 1080. But that 1080 is half of what was there after the T-Rex ate. So, half of 1080 is 540