 found out there were 8 more, so total was 12 dogs. Then, he went back and saw that the number of dogs is twice as many as when he left, so 2 times 12 is 24 dogs. So, now, he realizes he needs more toys because the number of dogs has doubled.\n\nWait, so initially, he had 4 toys for 4 dogs. Then, he added 8 toys for the 8 dogs, making it 12 toys total. But then, after that, the number of dogs doubled to 24. Therefore, he needs 24 toys in total for all the dogs. But hold on, he already had 12 toys. So, he needs 24 minus 12, which is 12 more toys.\n\nBut wait, the problem says he had to buy some more toys after the second time. So, maybe he had to buy 12 more toys. So, now he has 12 + 12 = 24 toys in total.\n\nBut then, when James came back yet again, 3 dogs were gone. So, he no longer needed those toys. Hmm. So, 3 dogs left, meaning he needs one less toy for each of those 3 dogs. So, he can remove 3 toys from his total.\n\nTherefore, the total number of toys he needs is 24 - 3 = 21 toys.\n\nWait, let me double-check that.\n\nStarting point: 4 dogs, 4 toys.\n\nThen, 8 more dogs, so total 12 dogs, needs 12 toys. So, he buys 8 more, bringing total toys to 12.\n\nThen, he comes back and sees that the number of dogs is twice as many as when he left. When he left, he had 12 dogs, so twice as many is 24 dogs. So, he needs 24 toys. Since he already had 12, he needs to buy 12 more, making it 24 toys.\n\nThen, 3 dogs are gone, so he no longer needs 3 toys. So, 24 - 3 = 21 toys.\n\nYes, that seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I misinterpreted the \"twice as many more dogs than when he left.\" Let me consider another interpretation.\n\nIf he went back and saw that there are twice as many more dogs than when he left, that could mean that the increase is double. So, when he left, he had 12 dogs, then 8 more were added, making it 20? Wait, that doesn't fit.\n\nNo, I think my first interpretation was correct. He had 4 dogs, then found 8 more, making 12. Then, when he went back, the number of dogs had doubled to 24, so he needed 24 toys. So, he had to buy 12 more, making it 24. Then, 3 dogs left, so 21 toys.\n\nAlternatively, maybe he bought 8 toys initially, then when he saw the number doubled, he bought 16 more, making it 24. Then, 3 dogs left, so 21.\n\nYes, I think that's the correct approach.\n\nSo, in summary:\n\n1. Initially, 4 dogs, 4 toys.\n\n2. Found 8 more dogs, needing 8 toys. Total toys: 4 + 8 = 12.\n\n3. Then, when he came back, the number of dogs had doubled, so 24 dogs. Therefore, he needs 24 toys. So, buys 12 more, total toys 24.\n\n4. Then, 3 dogs were gone, so he no longer needs 3 toys. Total toys needed: 24 - 3 = 21.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 21 toys.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe total number of toys James needs is \\boxed{21}.\n</think>\n\nJames needs to get more toys for his dog shelter. Each dog needs one toy. Initially, he has 4 toys for 4 dogs. He then finds out there are 8 more dogs, making the total number of dogs 12. Therefore, he needs 12 toys in total.\n\nAfter buying these toys, he returns and finds that the number of dogs has doubled. This means the number of dogs is now 24, so he needs 24 toys. Since he already has 12 toys, he needs to buy 12 more, making the total number of toys 24.\n\nHowever, 3 dogs are gone when he returns again, so he no longer needs 3 toys. Therefore, the total number of toys he needs is 24 - 3 = 21.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{21}\n\\]", "pred_ans": "21", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: James needs to get more toys for his doggie shelter. Each dog needs one toy. James currently has 4 toys on hand for 4 dogs, but there are 8 more dogs in the shelter now. After buying the toys, he went back to see that there are twice as many more dogs than when he left so he had to buy some more toys. When James came back yet again, 3 dogs were gone so he no longer needed those toys. How many toys in total does James need?\n Solution:\n<think>\