hirts without collars and 30 floral shirts without buttons. So, I subtract the number of white shirts without collars from the number of floral shirts without buttons.\n\nSo, 30 minus 20 is 10. Therefore, there are 10 more floral shirts with no buttons than white shirts with no collars.\n\nWait, let me just double-check my calculations to make sure I didn't make any mistakes. For the white shirts: half of 40 is 20, so 20 with collars and 20 without. For the floral shirts: 50 total minus 20 with buttons is 30 without buttons. Then, 30 minus 20 is 10. Yeah, that seems right.\n\nSo, I think the answer is 10. But let me make sure I interpreted the question correctly. It says, \"how many more floral shirts with no buttons are there than white shirts with no collars.\" So, it's the difference between the two quantities. So, if I have 30 floral shirts without buttons and 20 white shirts without collars, then 30 minus 20 is indeed 10. So, 10 more floral shirts without buttons.\n\nI don't see any mistakes in my reasoning, so I think this is the correct answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of extra floral shirts with no buttons compared to white shirts with no collars is \\boxed{10}.\n</think>\n\nThe clothing store has 40 white shirts and 50 floral shirts. \n\nFirst, we determine the number of white shirts with collars and without collars:\n- Half of the white shirts have collars: \n  \\[\n  \\frac{40}{2} = 20 \\text{ white shirts with collars}\n  \\]\n- The remaining white shirts do not have collars:\n  \\[\n  40 - 20 = 20 \\text{ white shirts without collars}\n  \\]\n\nNext, we determine the number of floral shirts with no buttons:\n- There are 20 floral shirts with buttons:\n  \\[\n  50 - 20 = 30 \\text{ floral shirts without buttons}\n  \\]\n\nTo find how many more floral shirts with no buttons there are than white shirts with no collars, we subtract the number of white shirts without collars from the number of floral shirts without buttons:\n  \\[\n  30 - 20 = 10\n  \\]\n\nThus, the number of extra floral shirts with no buttons compared to white shirts with no collars is \\boxed{10}.", "pred_ans": "10", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: A clothing store has 40 white shirts and 50 floral shirts. Half of the white shirts have collars, and 20 of the floral shirts have buttons. How many more floral shirts with no buttons are there than white shirts with no collars?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: The farm has 30 cows and the zoo has 20 sheep.  The zoo has twice as many cows as the farm does.  The farm has half as many sheep as the zoo does.  How many animals do the farm and zoo have combined?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "120", "rationale": "Alright, let's try to figure out this math problem step by step. It's about cows and sheep on a farm and in a zoo. Hmm, okay. Let me read the problem again to make sure I understand it.\n\n\"The farm has 30 cows and the zoo has 20 sheep. The zoo has twice as many cows as the farm does. The farm has half as many sheep as the zoo does. How many animals do the farm and zoo have combined?\"\n\nWait, hold on. Let me parse this carefully. It says the farm has 30 cows and the zoo has 20 sheep. So, initially, I know the number of cows and sheep separately. But then it adds some relationships: the zoo has twice as many cows as the farm, and the farm has half as many sheep as the zoo does. Hmm, okay.\n\nLet me write down what I know:\n\n- Farm cows: 30\n- Zoo cows: ?\n- Zoo sheep: 20\n- Farm sheep: ?\n\nThe problem gives me that the zoo has twice as many cows as the farm. So, if the farm has 30 cows, the zoo must have twice that number. Let me calculate that.\n\nZoo cows = 2 * Farm cows\nZoo cows = 2 * 30\nZoo cows = 60\n\nGot it. So, the zoo has 60 cows.\n\nNext, it says the farm has half as many sheep as the zoo does. The zoo has 20 sheep, so the farm must have half of that. Let me figure that out.\n\nFarm sheep = 1/2 * Zoo sheep\nFarm sheep = 1/2 * 20\nFarm sheep = 10\n\nOkay, so the farm has 10 sheep.\n\nNow, the question is asking how many animals the farm and zoo have combined. So, I need to add up all the cows and sheep on both the farm and the zoo.\n\nLet me list all the numbers again to make sure I have them straight:\n\n- Farm cows: 30\n- Zoo cows: 60\n- Zoo sheep: 20\n- Farm sheep: 10\n\nSo, adding them all 