per acre gives 50 tons. Then, multiplying by 2 barrels per ton, we get 100 barrels. The units: acres cancel with acres, tons cancel with tons, leaving us with barrels. Perfect.\n\nI think I've considered multiple methods to reach the same answer. It's consistent across different approaches, so I'm confident that 100 barrels is correct.\n\nJust to recap: 10 acres * 5 tons/acre = 50 tons. 50 tons * 2 barrels/ton = 100 barrels. Yep, that's the process.\n\nI don't think I've made any calculation errors here. 5 times 10 is 50, and 50 times 2 is 100. Simple multiplication, straightforward.\n\nWait a second, just to be absolutely sure, let me compute it again:\n\n10 acres * 5 tons/acre = 50 tons.\n\n50 tons * 2 barrels/ton = 100 barrels.\n\nYes, that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, 10 * 5 is 50. 50 * 2 is 100. So, 50 tons is 100 barrels.\n\nYep, same answer.\n\nI think I've beaten this to death, but it's better to be thorough, especially when making sure the answer is correct.\n\nSo, putting it all together, each step is clear:\n\n1. Calculate total grape production: 10 acres * 5 tons/acre = 50 tons.\n2. Convert grape tons to wine barrels: 50 tons * 2 barrels/ton = 100 barrels.\n\nSo, Josie's farm produces 100 barrels of wine per year.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe farm produces \\boxed{100} barrels of wine per year.\n</think>\n\nJosie grows grapes on her 10-acre farm. Each acre produces 5 tons of grapes per year, and each ton of grapes makes 2 barrels of wine. To find the total number of barrels of wine produced per year, we follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the total grape production for all 10 acres:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total grape tons} = 10 \\text{ acres} \\times 5 \\text{ tons/acre} = 50 \\text{ tons}\n   \\]\n\n2. Convert the total grape tons into barrels of wine:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total wine barrels} = 50 \\text{ tons} \\times 2 \\text{ barrels/ton} = 100 \\text{ barrels}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the farm produces \\boxed{100} barrels of wine per year.", "pred_ans": "100", "wrap