efore, in 1 mile, it's 362.5. Then, 400 miles would be 362.5 * 400. Yep, 362.5 * 400 is indeed 145,000. Okay, that's correct.\n\nSo, Jeremy's car tires rotate 145,000 times every month. Now, each tire can only handle 10,440,000 rotations before needing replacement. So, we need to find out how many months it takes for the tires to reach that 10,440,000 rotation limit.\n\nTo find that, we can divide the total number of allowed rotations by the number of rotations per month. Let's do that:\n\nNumber of months = 10,440,000 rotations / 145,000 rotations/month.\n\nHmm, let me compute 10,440,000 divided by 145,000. Maybe it's easier if I write them both in thousands.\n\n10,440,000 is 10.44 million, and 145,000 is 0.145 million. So, 10.44 / 0.145 is approximately, let me compute that.\n\n10.44 divided by 0.145. Let's see, 0.145 times 70 is 10.15, because 0.145*70=10.15. Then, 10.44 - 10.15 is 0.29. 0.29 / 0.145 is about 2. So, total is 70 + 2 = 72 months.\n\nWait, so that's 72 months. Hmm, but let me check this calculation again because that seems a bit high.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's better to write the division as 10,440,000 / 145,000.\n\nLet me write both numbers in numbers: 10,440,000 and 145,000.\n\nDividing 10,440,000 by 145,000:\n\nFirst, I can divide numerator and denominator by 1000 to make it easier: 10,440 / 145.\n\nSo, 10,440 divided by 145. Let me do that division.\n\n145 goes into 10,440 how many times?\n\n145 * 70 is 10,150 (since 145*70=10,150). Subtract that from 10,440: 10,440 - 10,150 = 290.\n\nNow, 145 goes into 290 twice. So, 2 times 145 is 290.\n\nSo, total is 70 + 2 = 72. Therefore, 72 months.\n\nSo, 72 months. To convert that into years, since there are 12 months in a year, divide 72 by 12.\n\n72 / 12 = 6. So, 6 years.\n\nWait, so the tires will need to be replaced after 6 years.\n\nLet me just go through the steps again to make sure I didn't make any mistakes.\n\n1. Every 2 miles, 725 rotations. So, per mile, 725 / 2 = 362.5 rotations.\n\n2. 400 miles per month. So, 362.5 * 400 = 145,000 rotations per month.\n\n3. Each tire can handle 10,440,000 rotations. So, number of months needed is 10,440,000 / 145,000.\n\n4. 10,440,000 divided by 145,000 is 72 months.\n\n5. 72 months divided by 12 is 6 years.\n\nYep, that seems correct.\n\nBut just to be thorough, maybe I can check my calculations another way.\n\nAlternatively, instead of computing per mile, maybe compute total rotations per month another way.\n\nGiven that every 2 miles, it's 725 rotations, so for 400 miles, how many sets of 2 miles are there? 400 / 2 = 200 sets. Each set is 725 rotations, so 200 * 725 = ?\n\n200 * 725: 200 * 700 = 140,000; 200 * 25 = 5,000. So, 140,000 + 5,000 = 145,000. Same as before. So, that's 145,000 rotations per month. So, that's correct.\n\nSo, 145,000 per month. So, 10,440,000 / 145,000 = 72 months, which is 6 years. So, yes, that seems consistent.\n\nAnother way to think about it is, how many years is Jeremy driving before the tires wear out.\n\nBut maybe think of it in terms of rotations per year.\n\nSo, 145,000 rotations per month. So, per year, it's 145,000 * 12 = 1,740,000 rotations per year.\n\nSo, each tire can handle 10,440,000 rotations. So, 10,440,000 / 1,740,000 = ?\n\n10,440,000 divided by 1,740,000.\n\nAgain, divide numerator and denominator by 1,000: 10,440 / 1,740.\n\nNow, 1,740 goes into 10,440 how many times? Let's see.\n\n1,740 * 6 = 10,440. Because 1,740 * 5 = 8,700. 1,740 * 6 = 8,700 + 1,740 = 10,440.\n\nSo, 6 years.\n\nSame result.\n\nSo, that's consistent. So, the tires will need to be replaced after 6 years.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can think of it as tire life in terms of rotations, and how much is consumed per month.\n\nBut that seems similar to what I did before.\n\nWait, perhaps another approach: Instead of computing total rotations per month, compute how many months per rotation.\n\nBut that might be more complicated. Let me see.\n\n10,440,000 rotations needed.\n\nIf each month, 145,000 rotations occur, then the number of months is 10,440,000 / 145,000, which is 72, as before.\n\nSo, same result.\n\nSo, in any case, 72 months, which is 6 years.\n\nTherefore, Jeremy will need to replace his tires every 6 years.\n\nSo, I think I'm confident in that answer now.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe tires will need to be replaced after \\boxed{6} years.\n</think>\n\nJeremy drives 400 miles a month, and each 2 miles results in 725 tire rotations. We need to determine how many years it will take for the tires to reach 10,440,000 rotations before needing replacement.\n\n1. Calculate the number of rotations per mile:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Rotations per mile} = \\frac{725 \\text{ rotations}}{2 \\text{ miles}} = 362.5 \\text{ rotations per mile}\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate the total number of rotations per month:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total rotations per month} = 362.5 \\text{ rotations/mile} \\times 400 \\text{ miles} = 145,000 \\text{ rotations/month}\n   \\]\n\n3. Determine the number of months required to reach 10,440,000 rotations:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of months} = \\frac{10,440,000 \\text{ rotations}}{145,000 \\text{ rotations/month}} = 72 \\text{ months}\n   \\]\n\n4. Convert the number of months into years:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of years} = \\frac{72 \\text{ months}}{12 \\text{ months/year}} = 6 \\text{ years}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the tires will need to be replaced after \\boxed{6} years.", "pred_ans": "6", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Every 2 miles a car drives the tires rotate 725 times.  Jeremy drives 400 miles a month.  If each tire can undergo 10,440,000 rotations how many years before the tire needs to be replaced?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
