{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Tim has a box with 7 blue shoe boxes and 9 red shoe boxes. If he uses 3 blue shoeboxes and 1/3 red of his shoeboxes to go fishing, how many red and blue shoe boxes are left in Tim's box?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "10", "rationale": "Alright, let me try to figure out this problem step by step. So, Tim has some shoeboxes, right? Specifically, he has 7 blue ones and 9 red ones. He uses some of them to go fishing. The problem says he uses 3 blue shoeboxes and 1/3 of his red shoeboxes. Hmm, I need to find out how many red and blue shoeboxes are left after he uses some.\n\nFirst, let me write down what I know. Tim starts with:\n\n- Blue shoeboxes: 7\n- Red shoeboxes: 9\n\nHe uses 3 blue shoeboxes. That part seems straightforward. So, the number of blue shoeboxes used is 3. To find out how many are left, I can subtract the used ones from the total he had.\n\nSo, blue shoeboxes left = total blue shoeboxes - blue shoeboxes used\n= 7 - 3\n= 4\n\nOkay, so he has 4 blue shoeboxes left. That part was simple. Now, the tricky part is figuring out how many red shoeboxes he uses. The problem says he uses 1/3 of his shoeboxes. Wait, does that mean 1/3 of his red shoeboxes or 1/3 of all his shoeboxes? Hmm, the wording says \"1/3 red of his shoeboxes,\" which is a bit confusing. But I think it's referring to red shoeboxes. So, he uses 1/3 of his red shoeboxes.\n\nSo, red shoeboxes used = 1/3 of 9\n= (1/3) * 9\n= 3\n\nWait, so he uses 3 red shoeboxes. That makes sense. So, to find out how many red shoeboxes are left, I subtract the used ones from the total he had.\n\nRed shoeboxes left = total red shoeboxes - red shoeboxes used\n= 9 - 3\n= 6\n\nSo, he has 6 red shoeboxes left. To recap:\n\n- Blue shoeboxes used: 3\n- Red shoeboxes used: 3\n- Blue shoeboxes left: 4\n- Red shoeboxes left: 6\n\nWait a minute, let me double-check. If he uses 1/3 of his red shoeboxes, that's i