ny Dragons has Prince Bruce slain?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Toby is reading a book that is 45 pages long. It averages 200 words a page. Toby can read at a rate of 300 words per minute. He has to be at the airport in 60 minutes and plans to leave as soon as he finishes the book. It takes 10 minutes to get to the airport. How many minutes early will Toby be?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "20", "rationale": "Okay, so I have this problem here about Toby reading a book and needing to figure out how early he'll be to the airport. Let me try to break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let me read the problem again and note down all the important details:\n\n- The book is 45 pages long.\n- It averages 200 words per page.\n- Toby reads at a rate of 300 words per minute.\n- He has to be at the airport in 60 minutes.\n- He plans to leave as soon as he finishes the book.\n- It takes 10 minutes to get to the airport.\n- We need to find out how many minutes early Toby will be.\n\nAlright, so I need to figure out how long it will take Toby to read the entire book, then add the 10 minutes it takes to get to the airport, and see how that compares to the 60 minutes he has before he needs to leave. The difference will tell me how early he is.\n\nLet me start by calculating the total number of words in the book. Since it's 45 pages and each page has 200 words, I can multiply these two numbers to get the total words.\n\nTotal words = number of pages \u00d7 words per page\nTotal words = 45 \u00d7 200\n\nHmm, what's 45 times 200? Let me compute that. 45 times 200 is the same as 45 times 2 times 100, right? So 45 times 2 is 90, and then times 100 is 9000. So, the book has 9000 words.\n\nNow, I need to figure out how long it will take Toby to read all 9000 words. He reads at 300 words per minute. So, time is equal to the number of words divided by the reading rate.\n\nTime to read = total words \u00f7 reading rate\nTime to read = 9000 \u00f7 300\n\nLet me calculate that. 9000 divided by 300. Well, 300 goes into 9000 how many times? 300 times 30 is 9000, so that means it will take Toby 30 minutes to read the entire book.\n\nOkay, so Toby finishes the book in 30 minutes. Then, it takes him 10 minutes to get to the airport. So, the total time from when he starts reading until he gets to the airport is 30 + 10 = 40 minutes.\n\nBut Toby has until 60 minutes to get to the airport. So, how much earlier will he be? That should be the difference between 60 minutes and 40 minutes.\n\nMinutes early = total allowed time - actual time\nMinutes early = 60 - 40\nMinutes early = 20\n\nSo, Toby will be 20 minutes early at the airport.\n\nWait, let me double-check my calculations to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nTotal words: 45 pages \u00d7 200 words/page = 9000 words. That seems right.\n\nReading time: 9000 words \u00f7 300 words per minute = 30 minutes. Yeah, that makes sense.\n\nTravel time: 10 minutes. So, total time from starting to reading to arriving at the airport is 30 + 10 = 40 minutes.\n\nAllowed time: 60 minutes. So, he has 60 minutes, but only needs 40 to get there. Therefore, he'll be 20 minutes early. That seems correct.\n\nIs there another way to approach this problem? Maybe by calculating his reading time and then subtracting the travel time from his total available time?\n\nWait, that's essentially what I did. But let me think:\n\nTotal time from starting to finish the book and travel: 30 + 10 = 40 minutes.\n\nHe has 60 minutes, so 60 - 40 = 20 minutes early.\n\nAlternatively, if I think about how much extra time he has, it's 60 minutes minus the time he actually uses, which is 40 minutes. So, 20 minutes extra, which is how early he is.\n\nI think that's solid. But just to make sure, let me consider the rates and see if I can represent this with equations.\n\nLet me denote:\n\n- Total words in book: W = 45 \u00d7 200 = 9000\n- Toby's reading rate: R = 300 words per minute\n- Time to read the book: T = W / R = 9000 / 300 = 30 minutes\n- Time to get to the airport: G = 10 minutes\n- Total time he has: H = 60 minutes\n\nSo, the total time from when he starts reading until he gets to the airport is T + G = 30 + 10 = 40 minutes.\n\nHe arrives 40 minutes after starting to read, but he has 60 minutes. So, the time difference is H - (T + G) = 60 - 40 = 20 minutes.\n\nTherefore, he is 20 minutes early.\n\nAnother way to think about it is in terms of how much time he has left after finishing the book. Once he finishes the book in 30 minutes, he still has 60 - 30 = 30 minutes before he needs to leave. But he only needs 10 minutes to get to the airport, so he'll have 30 - 10 = 20 minutes to spare. So, that's another way to see it.\n\nWait, that method also gives 20 minutes. So, that's consistent. So, that reinforces that 20 minutes is the correct answer.\n\nIs there a chance that I might have confused the total time he has? Let me see. The problem says he has to be at the airport in 60 minutes. So, does that mean he has 60 minutes from now, or that he needs to leave in 60 minutes and it takes 10 minutes to get there? It seems like he has to be there in 60 minutes, so he can leave as soon as he finishes the book, which takes 30 minutes, and then it takes 10 minutes to get there, so total time from now is 40 minutes.\n\nSo, he has 60 minutes, which is the latest he can be there. So, he's 20 minutes early.\n\nAlternatively, if he had to leave in 60 minutes, meaning he has to be at the airport in 60 minutes, so he can start his journey at any time, but he can't take more than 60 minutes from now to get there. He finishes the book in 30 minutes, then takes 10 minutes to get there, so he arrives at 40 minutes. So, he arrives 20 minutes before the 60-minute mark.\n\nEither way, I think 20 minutes is correct.\n\nLet me try to visualize the timeline:\n\n- Time 0: Toby starts reading the book.\n- Time 30: Toby finishes the book.\n- Time 30 + 10 = 40: Toby arrives at the airport.\n- He needs to be there by Time 60, so he arrives 20 minutes early.\n\nYes, that makes sense. So, I think I've considered it from multiple angles, and all lead to the same conclusion.\n\nJust to recap:\n\n1. Calculate total words: 45 pages \u00d7 200 words/page = 9000 words.\n2. Calculate reading time: 9000 \u00f7 300 = 30 minutes.\n3. Calculate total time to get to the airport: 30 + 10 = 40 minutes.\n4. Subtract from total allowed time: 60 - 40 = 20 minutes.\n\nTherefore, Toby will be 20 minutes early.\n\nI think that's thorough. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations or reasoning. So, I feel confident that 20 minutes is the correct answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nToby will be \\boxed{20} minutes early.\n</think>\n\nFirst, we calculate the total number of words in the book:\n\\[\n\\text{Total words} = 45 \\text{ pages} \\times 200 \\text{ words per page} = 9000 \\text{ words}\n\\]\n\nNext, we determine how long it will take Toby to read the entire book at his reading rate of 300 words per minute:\n\\[\n\\text{Time to read} = \\frac{9000 \\text{ words}}{300 \\text{ words per minute}} = 30 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nThen, we add the travel time to the airport, which is 10 minutes:\n\\[\n\\text{Total time from start to arrival} = 30 \\text{ minutes} + 10 \\text{ minutes} = 40 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nWe compare this total time to the 60 minutes Toby has before he needs to leave:\n\\[\n\\text{Minutes early} = 60 \\text{ minutes} - 40 \\text{ minutes} = 20 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, Toby will be \\boxed{20} minutes early.", "pred_ans": "20", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Toby is reading a book that is 45 pages long. It averages 200 words a page. Toby can read at a rate of 300 words per minute. He has to be at the airport in 60 minutes and plans to leave as soon as he finishes the book. It takes 10 minutes to get to the airport. How many minutes early will Toby be?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
