e 2 \u00d7 9 cookies. Let me compute that. 2 \u00d7 9 is 18. So, Kelsie made 18 cookies.\n\nWait, let me make sure I did that correctly. Sometimes with these word problems, it's easy to mix up the relationships. So, let me double-check each step.\n\nFirst, Suzanne made 36 cookies. That part is clear. Then, Josh made one-fourth of that. So, 36 divided by 4 is 9. That seems right. So, Josh made 9 cookies.\n\nThen, Kelsie made two times more than Josh. So, that should be 2 \u00d7 9, which is 18. Hmm, that seems straightforward. So, yeah, Kelsie made 18 cookies.\n\nBut let me think again about the wording. When it says \"two times more,\" does that mean 2J or 3J? Because sometimes people get confused. Let me see.\n\nIf Josh made 9, then two times more would mean 9 + 2\u00d79 = 27. But that seems like three times as much, right? Because 9 is the original, and 2\u00d79 is 18, so 9 + 18 is 27, which is three times as much. So, is \"two times more\" equal to three times as much or two times as much?\n\nHmm, actually, in standard math terms, \"two times more\" is ambiguous. But in common usage, I think \"two times more\" usually means double, so two times as much. So, Kelsie made two times as much as Josh. So, 2J. So, in that case, if Josh made 9, Kelsie made 18.\n\nBut just to be thorough, let me think about both interpretations.\n\nIf \"two times more\" is three times as much, then Kelsie made 3J. So, 3 \u00d7 9 is 27. But 27 is three times as much as 9, which is technically \"three times more.\" So, maybe that's where the confusion is.\n\nI think in most cases, when someone says \"two times more,\" they mean two times as much, which is 2J. So, in this context, Kelsie made 18 cookies. So, I think that's the correct answer.\n\nLet me just reconstruct the problem:\n\n- Suzanne made 36 cookies.\n\n- Josh made one-fourth of that, so 36 / 4 = 9.\n\n- Kelsie made two times more than Josh, which is 2 \u00d7 9 = 18.\n\nTherefore, Kelsie made 18 cookies.\n\nSo, yeah, I think that's right. I can't see any mistakes in that logic. So, I think the answer is 18.\n\n**Final Answer**\nKelsie made \\boxed{18} cookies.\n</think>\n\nFirst, we know that Suzanne made 36 cookies. \n\nNext, Josh made one-fourth of the number of cookies that Suzanne made. Therefore, we calculate Josh's cookies as follows:\n\\[\n\\text{Josh's cookies} = \\frac{1}{4} \\times 36 = 9\n\\]\n\nKelsie made two times more cookies than Josh. This means Kelsie made twice the number of cookies that Josh made:\n\\[\n\\text{Kelsie's cookies} = 2 \\times 9 = 18\n\\]\n\nThus, Kelsie made \\boxed{18} cookies.", "pred_ans": "18", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: The eighth-grade class held a bake-off. Kelsie made two times more cookies than Josh. Josh made one-fourth the number of cookies that Suzanne made. If Suzanne made 36 cookies, how many did Kelsie make?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
