50 - 374. Hmm, 650 - 300 is 350. Then, I have to subtract 74 more. So, 350 - 74. Let me do that step by step. 350 - 70 is 280, and then subtract 4 more, which is 276. So, 650 - 374 is 276. Therefore, there will be 276 wheels left.\n\nWait, let me just verify this calculation another way to make sure I didn't make a mistake. 374 + 276 should equal 650? Let's check: 374 + 200 is 574, then +76 is 650. Yes, that adds up correctly.\n\nSo, putting it all together:\n\n- Wheels needed for cars: 57 * 4 = 228\n- Wheels needed for motorcycles: 73 * 2 = 146\n- Total wheels needed: 228 + 146 = 374\n- Wheels available: 650\n- Wheels left: 650 - 374 = 276\n\nTherefore, Henry will have 276 wheels left after assembling all the toys.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of wheels left will be \\boxed{276}.\n</think>\n\nHenry needs to assemble 57 cars and 73 motorcycles. Each car requires 4 wheels and each motorcycle requires 2 wheels. He has a box with 650 wheels.\n\nFirst, calculate the number of wheels needed for the cars:\n\\[ 57 \\text{ cars} \\times 4 \\text{ wheels/car} = 228 \\text{ wheels} \\]\n\nNext, calculate the number of wheels needed for the motorcycles:\n\\[ 73 \\text{ motorcycles} \\times 2 \\text{ wheels/motorcycle} = 146 \\text{ wheels} \\]\n\nAdd the wheels needed for cars and motorcycles:\n\\[ 228 \\text{ wheels} + 146 \\text{ wheels} = 374 \\text{ wheels} \\]\n\nSubtract the total number of wheels used from the total number of wheels available:\n\\[ 650 \\text{ wheels} - 374 \\text{ wheels} = 276 \\text{ wheels} \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of wheels left will be \\(\\boxed{276}\\).", "pred_ans": "276", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Henry needs to assemble some toys, specifically 57 cars and 73 motorcycles. Henry knows that to assemble all the toys he will need 4 wheels for each car and 2 wheels for each motorcycle. How many wheels will be left if he has a box with 650 wheels in it?\n Solution: