{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Samwell owns an appliances store. For this week, one-fourth of their sales are smart TVs, one-eighth are analog TVs, and the rest are OLED TVs. If they were able to sell a total of 40 TVs, how many OLED TVs were they able to sell?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "25", "rationale": "Okay, so I have this problem where Samwell owns an appliance store, and I need to figure out how many OLED TVs they sold this week. Let's break down the information step by step.\n\nFirst, the problem says that one-fourth of their sales are smart TVs. Then, one-eighth are analog TVs. The rest are OLED TVs. They sold a total of 40 TVs. I need to find out how many of those are OLED TVs.\n\nHmm, okay. So, let me think about how to represent this mathematically. Let's denote the total number of TVs sold as T, which is given as 40. Then, the number sold as smart TVs is one-fourth of T, which would be (1/4)T. Similarly, analog TVs are one-eighth of T, so that's (1/8)T. The rest are OLED TVs, so that must be whatever is left after subtracting the smart and analog TVs from the total.\n\nSo, if I write that as an equation, it would be:\n\nOLED TVs = Total TVs - Smart TVs - Analog TVs\n\nWhich translates to:\n\nOLED TVs = T - (1/4)T - (1/8)T\n\nSince T is 40, I can plug that in:\n\nOLED TVs = 40 - (1/4)*40 - (1/8)*40\n\nLet me compute each part step by step.\n\nFirst, (1/4)*40. Well, 40 divided by 4 is 10. So, smart TVs are 10 units.\n\nNext, (1/8)*40. Hmm, 40 divided by 8 is 5. So, analog TVs are 5 units.\n\nNow, subtracting those from the total:\n\n40 - 10 - 5.\n\nWait, that's 40 minus 10 is 30, then 30 minus 5 is 25.\n\nSo, does that mean they sold 25 OLED TVs?\n\nLet me double-check my calculations to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nTotal TVs: 40.\n\nSmart TVs: 1/4 of 40 is 10. That makes sense because 10 times 4 is 40.\n\nAnalog TVs: 1/8 of 40 is 5. That also makes sense because