\n"}
{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Tom gets 4 car washes a month.  If each car wash costs $15 how much does he pay in a year?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "720", "rationale": "First, determine the cost of one car wash by multiplying $15 by 1.\n\nNext, calculate the total cost for one month by multiplying the cost of one car wash by 4.\n\nThen, find the total cost for one year by multiplying the total monthly cost by 12 months.\n\nFinally, present the final answer clearly.\n</think>\n\n**Solution:**\n\nTo determine how much Tom pays in a year for car washes, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Cost of One Car Wash:**\n   \n   \\[\n   \\$15 \\text{ per wash} \\times 1 = \\$15\n   \\]\n\n2. **Total Cost for One Month:**\n   \n   \\[\n   \\$15 \\times 4 \\text{ washes/month} = \\$60 \\text{ per month}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Total Cost for One Year:**\n   \n   \\[\n   \\$60 \\text{ per month} \\times 12 \\text{ months} = \\$720\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\nTom pays \\boxed{720} dollars in a year.", "pred_ans": "720", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Tom gets 4 car washes a month.  If each car wash costs $15 how much does he pay in a year?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Nissa hires 60 seasonal workers to play elves in her department store's Santa village. A third of the elves quit after children vomit on them, then 10 of the remaining elves quit after kids kick their shins. How many elves are left?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "30", "rationale": "Okay, so Nissa hires 60 seasonal workers to play elves in her Santa village. Hmm, that's a lot of elves! First, a third of the elves quit after some kids vomit on them. Then, 10 more elves quit after kids kick their shins. I need to figure out how many elves are left after these two rounds of quitting.\n\nLet me take it step by step. First, let's find out how many elves quit after the first incident. A third of 60 is... well, 60 divided by 3 is 20. So, 20 elves quit. That means the number of elves remaining after this first quitting is 60 minus 20, which is 40 elves left.\n\nNow, moving on to the second part. After the kids kick their shins, 10 more elves quit. So, I need to subtract these 10 elves from the remaining 40. Let me do that: 40 minus 10 equals 30.\n\nWait, so does that mean 30 elves are left? That seems straightforward. Let me double-check my calculations to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nFirst, starting with 60 elves. A third of them is 20, so 60 - 20 is indeed 40. Then, 10 elves quit, so 40 - 10 is 30. Yep, that adds up.\n\nJust to make sure I didn't misinterpret the problem, let me read it again. A third of the elves quit after the first incident, then 10 of the remaining elves quit after the second incident. So, the order is first a third leave, then 10 leave from the new total. So, yes, subtracting 20 from 60 gives 40, then subtracting another 10 gives 30. So, 30 elves are left.\n\nI don't think I made any errors here. The key was to handle each quitting event one after the other and correctly calculate the remaining number each time. It's important to do each step carefully to avoid mistakes. So, I think 30 is the correct number of elves left.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of elves left is \\boxed{30}.\n</think>\n\nNissa hires 60 seasonal workers to play elves. \n\n1. A third of the elves quit after the first incident:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{3} \\times 60 = 20\n   \\]\n   Remaining elves:\n   \\[\n   60 - 20 = 40\n   \\]\n\n2. 10 more elves quit after the second incident:\n   \\[\n   40 - 10 = 30\n   \\]\n\nThe number of elves left is \\boxed{30}.", "pred_ans": "30", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Nissa hires 60 seasonal workers to play elves in her department store's Santa village. A third of the elves quit after children vomit on them, then 10 of the remaining elves quit after kids kick their shins. How many elves are left?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
