{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Mrs. Jameson's bamboo in the backyard grows up to 30 inches a day. Today, its height is 20 feet. In how many days will its height be 600 inches?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "12", "rationale": "Okay, so I need to solve this math problem. Let me read it again to make sure I understand.\n\nMrs. Jameson's bamboo in the backyard grows up to 30 inches a day. Today, its height is 20 feet. In how many days will its height be 600 inches?\n\nAlright, so let's break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, I need to figure out how many inches the bamboo is today and then determine how many more days it'll take to reach 600 inches. The bamboo grows 30 inches each day, so I can use that information to create an equation.\n\nBut wait, today's height is given in feet, and the growth rate and the target height are given in inches. I need to make sure all the units match so I can work with consistent measurements. Probably, I should convert feet to inches because all the other numbers are in inches.\n\nI remember that 1 foot is equal to 12 inches. So, if today's height is 20 feet, I can convert that to inches by multiplying 20 by 12.\n\nLet me do that: 20 feet * 12 inches/foot. Hmm, 20 times 12 is 240. So, the bamboo is currently 240 inches tall.\n\nGot that. So today it's 240 inches, and it grows 30 inches each day. We need to find out how many days it'll take to reach 600 inches.\n\nSo, the current height is 240 inches, and we want it to be 600 inches. The difference between these two heights will give me the total growth needed. Let me subtract 240 from 600.\n\n600 inches - 240 inches = 360 inches.\n\nSo, the bamboo needs to grow an additional 360 inches. Since it grows 30 inches each day, I can divide the total growth needed by the daily growth rate to find the number of days.\n\nLet me write that as a formula:\n\nNumber of days = Total growth needed / Growth per day\n\nSo, plugging in the numbers:\n\nNumber of days = 360 inches / 30 inches/day\n\nCalculating that, 360 divided by 30 is 12. So, that would be 12 days.\n\nWait, let me double-check to make sure I didn't make an error in my calculations.\n\nStarting height: 20 feet. Convert that to inches: 20 * 12 = 240 inches. That seems right.\n\nTotal desired height: 600 inches.\n\nDifference: 600 - 240 = 360 inches. That makes sense.\n\nGrowth per day: 30 inches. So, 360 / 30 = 12 days. Yep, that's correct.\n\nI don't see any mistakes here. So, the bamboo will reach 600 inches in 12 days.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe bamboo will reach 600 inches in \\boxed{12} days.\n</think>\n\nMrs. Jameson's bamboo currently has a height of 20 feet, which converts to inches as follows:\n\\[ 20 \\text{ feet} \\times 12 \\text{ inches/foot} = 240 \\text{ inches} \\]\n\nThe bamboo needs to grow to 600 inches. The total growth needed is:\n\\[ 600 \\text{ inches} - 240 \\text{ inches} = 360 \\text{ inches} \\]\n\nThe bamboo grows 30 inches each day. The number of days required to achieve the total growth is:\n\\[ \\frac{360 \\text{ inches}}{30 \\text{ inches/day}} = 12 \\text{ days} \\]\n\nThus, the bamboo will reach 600 inches in \\boxed{12} days.", "pred_ans": "12", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Mrs. Jameson's bamboo in the backyard grows up to 30 inches a day. Today, its height is 20 feet. In how many days will its height be 600 inches?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
