r finishes the race?\" So, if Steve is faster, he will get home first, and Tim will have to wait until Steve is done. But the question is about the winner waiting for the loser. So, actually, if Steve is the winner, he will wait for Tim to finish. So, the waiting time is the difference between their times.\n\nSo, since Steve takes 36 minutes, Tim takes 40 minutes, the difference is 40 - 36 = 4 minutes. So, the winner, which is Steve, will wait for 4 minutes for Tim to finish. So, the answer is 4 minutes.\n\nWait, that seems straightforward, but let me just make sure I didn't flip them.\n\nWait, another way to think about it is, if Steve is the winner, meaning he gets home first, then he will have to wait until Tim finishes. So, the time between Steve arriving home and Tim arriving home is the waiting time. So, that's Tim's time minus Steve's time, which is 40 - 36 = 4 minutes.\n\nAlternatively, if Tim was the winner, the waiting time would be Steve's time minus Tim's time, but since Tim is slower, he can't be the winner.\n\nSo yeah, the waiting time is 4 minutes. Hmm, seems correct.\n\nWait, but just to be thorough, let me convert everything into minutes per mile or miles per minute to double-check.\n\nWait, Steve's speed is 440 feet per minute, which is 440/5280 miles per minute.\n\nSimilarly, Tim's speed is 264 feet per minute, which is 264/5280 miles per minute.\n\nSo, let's compute their speeds in miles per minute.\n\nFor Steve: 440 / 5280 = 1/12 miles per minute. Because 440 is a third of 1320, which is 5280 divided by 4. Wait, 440*12=5280, so yes, 440 is 1/12 of 5280.\n\nSimilarly, Tim's speed: 264 / 5280. Let's compute that.\n\n264 divided by 5280: 264 is half of 528, so 264 / 5280 is 1/20 miles per minute. Because 264*20=5280.\n\nSo, Steve's speed is 1/12 miles per minute, Tim's speed is 1/20 miles per minute.\n\nTherefore, Steve's time is 1 mile divided by (1/12) miles per minute, so 12 minutes per mile. Wait, no, that's not per mile. Wait, sorry, no.\n\nWait, speed is 1/12 miles per minute, so time is 1 / (1/12) = 12 minutes per mile.\n\nWait, that doesn't make sense because 12 minutes per mile for 3 miles would be 36 minutes. Which matches our previous calculation. So, yeah, 12 minutes per mile.\n\nFor Tim, speed is 1/20 miles per minute, so time is 1 / (1/20) = 20 minutes per mile.\n\nSo, for 2 miles, it's 20*2=40 minutes, which also matches.\n\nSo, that confirms that Steve takes 36 minutes and Tim takes 40 minutes.\n\nSo, the difference is 4 minutes. So, the winner, which is Steve, will have to wait 4 minutes for Tim to finish.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 4 minutes.\n\nWait, but hold on a second, the problem says, \"the winner will be waiting at their house before the loser finishes the race.\" So, is it the winner waiting at their house? Hmm, so when does the waiting start?\n\nWhen does the race start? They both leave school at the same time, I assume.\n\nSo, Steve leaves school at time 0, bikes home, arrives at 36 minutes. Tim leaves school at time 0, skates home, arrives at 40 minutes.\n\nSo, when does the waiting begin? For the winner, which is Steve, he gets home at 36 minutes. So, he has to wait until Tim finishes at 40 minutes. So, he will be waiting for 4 minutes.\n\nSimilarly, Tim arrives at 40 minutes, but Steve is already home at 36 minutes, so he has been waiting since 36 minutes.\n\nBut the problem says, \"how long will the winner be waiting at their house before the loser finishes the race?\" So, the winner is at their house waiting, and the loser is still racing.\n\nSo, in this case, Steve is the winner because he arrives first. So, he is waiting at his house, and Tim is still on the race, trying to finish. The time from when Steve arrives until Tim finishes is the waiting time.\n\nSo, that is 40 minutes minus 36 minutes, which is 4 minutes.\n\nAlternatively, if Tim was the winner, he would have to wait for Steve, but in this case, since Steve is faster, he is the winner, so he waits for Tim.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 4 minutes.\n\nSo, I think that's solid. I don't see any errors in the calculations. So, I feel confident that 4 minutes is the correct answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe winner will be waiting for \\boxed{4} minutes.\n</think>\n\nSteve and Tim are racing home from school. Steve lives 3 miles away and bikes at 440 feet per minute, while Tim lives 2 miles away and skates at 264 feet per minute. We need to determine how long the winner will wait for the loser to finish.\n\nFirst, convert the distances from miles to feet:\n- Steve's distance: \\(3 \\text{ miles} \\times 5280 \\text{ feet/mile} = 15840 \\text{ feet}\\)\n- Tim's distance: \\(2 \\text{ miles} \\times 5280 \\text{ feet/mile} = 10560 \\text{ feet}\\)\n\nNext, calculate their times using the formula \\( \\text{time} = \\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{speed}} \\):\n- Steve's time: \\(\\frac{15840 \\text{ feet}}{440 \\text{ feet/minute}} = 36 \\text{ minutes}\\)\n- Tim's time: \\(\\frac{10560 \\text{ feet}}{264 \\text{ feet/minute}} = 40 \\text{ minutes}\\)\n\nThe difference in their times is:\n- \\(40 \\text{ minutes} - 36 \\text{ minutes} = 4 \\text{ minutes}\\)\n\nSince Steve is the winner, he will wait for Tim to finish. Therefore, the winner will be waiting for \\(\\boxed{4}\\) minutes.", "pred_ans": "4", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Steve and Tim decide to see who can get home from school the fastest. Steve lives further away than Tim, so he is allowed to ride his bike. Steve lives 3 miles from the school and can bike at 440 feet per minute. Tim lives 2 miles away from the school. If Tim can ride his skateboard at 264 feet per minute, how long will the winner be waiting at their house before the loser finishes the race?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: 20% of 50 people think horse #2 will win the big race.  60% of the remaining people think horse #7 will win.  The rest of the people think horse #12 will win the big race.  How many people think that horse #12 will win?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "16", "rationale": "To determine how many people think horse #12 will win, let's break down the problem step by step.\n\nFirst, we know that 20% of 50 people believe horse #2 will win. So, we can calculate the number of people who think horse #2 will win by multiplying 20% by 50.\n\n20% of 50 is 0.20 * 50 = 10 people.\n\nNext, we need to find out how many people are left after those 10 people. Subtracting the 10 people from the original 50 gives us 50 - 10 = 40 people remaining.\n\nNow, 60% of these remaining 40 people believe horse #7 will win. Calculating 60% of 40:\n\n0.60 * 40 = 24 people.\n\nSubtracting these 24 people from the remaining 40 gives us 40 - 24 = 16 people left who do not think horse #2 or horse #7 will win.\n\nFinally, these 16 people are the ones who think horse #12 will win.\n\nSo, the number of people who believe horse #12 will win is 16.\n</think>\n\nLet's solve the problem step by step.\n\n**Given:**\n- Total number of people = 50\n- 20% of 50 people think horse #2 will win.\n- 60% of the remaining people think horse #7 will win.\n- The rest think horse #12 will win.\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the number of people who think horse #2 will win.**\n\\[\n20\\% \\text{ of } 50 = 0.20 \\times 50 = 10 \\text{ people}\n\\]\n\n**Step 2: Determine the number of people left after those who think horse #2 will win.**\n\\[\n50 \\text{ people} - 10 \\text{ people} = 40 \\text{ people}\n\\]\n\n**Step 3: Calculate the number of people who think horse #7 will win.**\n\\[\n60\\% \\text{ of } 40 = 0.60 \\times 40 = 24 \\text{ people}\n\\]\n\n**Step 4: Determine the number of people left after those who think horse #7 will win.**\n\\[\n40 \\text{ people} - 24 \\text{ people} = 16 \\text{ people}\n\\]\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThe remaining **16** people think horse #12 will win.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{16}\n\\]", "pred_ans": "16", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: 20% of 50 people think horse #2 will win the big race.  60% of the remaining people think horse #7 will win.  The rest of the people think horse #12 will win the big race.  How many people think that horse #12 will win?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Jenna and her mother picked some apples from their apple farm. Jenna picked half as many apples as her mom. If her mom got 20 apples, how many apples did they both pick?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "30", "rationale": "First, determine the number of apples Jenna's mother picked by considering that Jenna picked half as many apples as her mother. If her mother picked 20 apples, Jenna would have picked 10 apples.\n\nNext, add the apples picked by Jenna and her mother together to find the total number of apples they both picked. Adding 10 apples and 20 apples gives a total of 30 apples.\n</think>\n\nLet's solve the problem step by step.\n\n**Step 1: Determine the number of apples Jenna picked.**\n\nJenna picked half as many apples as her mother. If her mother picked 20 apples, then Jenna picked:\n\\[\n\\frac{20}{2} = 10 \\text{ apples}\n\\]\n\n**Step 2: Calculate the total number of apples they picked together.**\n\nAdd the apples picked by Jenna and her mother:\n\\[\n10 \\text{ (Jenna)} + 20 \\text{ (Mother)} = 30 \\text{ apples}\n\\]\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\\[\n\\boxed{30}\n\\]", "pred_ans": "30", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Jenna and her mother picked some apples from their apple farm. Jenna picked half as many apples as her mom. If her mom got 20 apples, how many apples did they both pick?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
