{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: There are some oranges in a basket. Ana spends 3 minutes peeling an orange and Jane spends 4 minutes doing the same. If Ana and Jane start picking oranges from this basket to peel at the same time, how many more oranges will Ana have peeled than Jane after an hour?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "5", "rationale": "Okay, so I have this math problem here, and I want to figure it out step by step. Let me read it again: There are some oranges in a basket. Ana spends 3 minutes peeling an orange, and Jane spends 4 minutes doing the same. If Ana and Jane start picking oranges from this basket to peel at the same time, how many more oranges will Ana have peeled than Jane after an hour?\n\nHmm, okay. So, let's break this down. First, Ana and Jane are both peeling oranges, but they take different amounts of time per orange. Ana takes 3 minutes per orange, and Jane takes 4 minutes per orange. They start at the same time, and we need to find out how many more oranges Ana has peeled compared to Jane after one hour.\n\nAlright, so maybe I should figure out how many oranges each of them can peel in one hour. Then, subtract the number Jane peeled from the number Ana peeled to find the difference.\n\nLet me think about how to compute how many oranges each person can peel in an hour. Since time is in minutes, I should convert an hour into minutes. One hour is 60 minutes, right? So, each of them is going to be peeling oranges for 60 minutes.\n\nLet's start with Ana. She takes 3 minutes per orange. So, in 60 minutes, how many oranges can she peel? I can calculate this by dividing the total time by the time per orange. So, that would be 60 minutes divided by 3 minutes per orange. Let me write that down:\n\nNumber of oranges Ana can peel = 60 / 3 = 20.\n\nAlright, so Ana can peel 20 oranges in an hour. Makes sense because 3 times 20 is 60.\n\nNow, let's do the same for Jane. Sh