ich is 5.\n\nI don't see any other factors that could influence this, like if they were working together or peeling different oranges, but the problem says they start picking oranges from the basket to peel at the same time. So, it's just each of them individually peeling their own set of oranges.\n\nSo, yeah, I think I'm all set. The answer should be 5.\n\n**Final Answer**\nAna will have peeled \\boxed{5} more oranges than Jane after an hour.\n</think>\n\nAna spends 3 minutes peeling one orange, and Jane spends 4 minutes peeling one orange. We need to determine how many more oranges Ana will have peeled than Jane after one hour (60 minutes).\n\nFirst, we calculate how many oranges each can peel in 60 minutes:\n\n- Ana's rate: \\( \\frac{60 \\text{ minutes}}{3 \\text{ minutes per orange}} = 20 \\text{ oranges} \\)\n- Jane's rate: \\( \\frac{60 \\text{ minutes}}{4 \\text{ minutes per orange}} = 15 \\text{ oranges} \\)\n\nNext, we find the difference in the number of oranges peeled by Ana and Jane:\n\n- Number of oranges Ana peeled - Number of oranges Jane peeled = \\( 20 - 15 = 5 \\)\n\nThus, Ana will have peeled \\(\\boxed{5}\\) more oranges than Jane after an hour.", "pred_ans": "5", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: There are some oranges in a basket. Ana spends 3 minutes peeling an orange and Jane spends 4 minutes doing the same. If Ana and Jane start picking oranges from this basket to peel at the same time, how many more oranges will Ana have peeled than Jane after an hour?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
