3 + 209. Let me add them step by step. 198 + 193: 198 + 190 is 388, plus 3 is 391. 391 + 209: 391 + 200 is 591, plus 9 is 600. Oh, okay, that adds up to 600. So, my initial confusion was because of a miscalculation, but after redoing it, it works out. So, 193 is correct.\n\nTherefore, the number of newspapers James delivers to District B is 193.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of newspapers James delivers to District B is \\boxed{193}.\n</think>\n\nJames delivers a total of 600 newspapers in a day. He delivers 198 newspapers to District A and 209 newspapers to District C. To find out how many newspapers he delivers to District B, we first add the newspapers delivered to Districts A and C together:\n\n\\[\n198 + 209 = 407\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract this sum from the total number of newspapers delivered:\n\n\\[\n600 - 407 = 193\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we add the newspapers delivered to District B to those delivered to Districts A and C:\n\n\\[\n198 + 193 + 209 = 600\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of newspapers James delivers to District B is \\boxed{193}.", "pred_ans": "193", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: James delivers 600 newspapers in a day. He delivers 198 newspapers to District A, some to District B and 209 newspapers to District C. How many newspapers does he deliver to District B?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
