{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Although Soledad works in a windowless office, she loves the outdoors. She will be on vacation for the entire month of June and cannot wait to hike 9,300 miles within that month. She is thinking of walking twice a day, covering 125 miles each time. How many more miles per day must Soledad hike to complete her journey on time?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "60", "rationale": "Okay, so I have this problem where Soledad wants to hike 9,300 miles in the entire month of June. She currently walks twice a day, covering 125 miles each time. The question is asking how many more miles per day she needs to hike to complete her journey on time.\n\nHmm, let me break this down. First, I need to figure out how many days she has in June. June is a month with 30 days, right? So that's 30 days total.\n\nShe's currently walking 125 miles twice a day. So each day, she walks 125 miles times 2. Let me write that down: 125 * 2 = 250 miles per day. So currently, she's hiking 250 miles every day.\n\nShe wants to cover a total of 9,300 miles. So, to find out how many days she has left, or rather, how many days she needs to hike, I can divide the total miles by her current daily mileage. So that would be 9,300 miles divided by 250 miles per day. Let me calculate that: 9,300 / 250.\n\nHmm, 250 times 37 is 9,250 because 250*30 is 7,500, and 250*7 is 1,750, so together that's 9,250. Then, 9,300 minus 9,250 is 50. So 50 miles left. So 50 miles divided by 250 miles per day is 0.2 days. So, she needs 37.2 days to complete 9,300 miles.\n\nBut June only has 30 days. So, she's short by 37.2 - 30 = 7.2 days. Wait, that doesn't make sense. Wait, no. Wait, no, that's not right. She needs 37.2 days, but June only has 30 days. So, she needs to cover 9,300 miles in 30 days, but her current pace would take 37.2 days, which is longer than June. So, she needs to increase her daily mileage.\n\nSo, she needs to cover 9,300 miles in 30 days. So, her required daily mileage is 9,300 divided by 30. Let me calculate that: 9,300 / 30 = 310 miles per day. Okay, so she needs to hike 310 miles each day.\n\nShe's currently hiking 250 miles each day. So, the difference is 310 - 250 = 60 miles. So, she needs to hike 60 miles more each day to complete her journey on time.\n\nWait, let me verify that again. If she walks 250 miles each day, in 30 days, she'd hike 250 * 30 = 7,500 miles. But she needs 9,300 miles. So, the deficit is 9,300 - 7,500 = 1,800 miles.\n\nShe's currently doing 250 miles per day. So, to cover the deficit of 1,800 miles, how much more does she need each day?\n\nSo, 1,800 / 30 days = 60 miles per day. So, that's the same as before. She needs to add 60 miles to her daily hike.\n\nWait, that doesn't seem right. Wait, 250 times 30 is 7,500. 7,500 plus 1,800 is 9,300. So, 1,800 is the deficit. So, 1,800 divided by 30 is 60. So, she needs to cover 60 miles more each day. That seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, if she needs 310 miles per day, and she's currently doing 250, so 310 minus 250 is 60. Yeah, that's the same answer.\n\nSo, Soledad needs to hike 60 more miles per day to finish her 9,300-mile journey by the end of June.\n\n**Final Answer**\nSoledad must hike an additional \\boxed{60} miles per day.\n</think>\n\nSoledad wants to hike 9,300 miles in the month of June, which has 30 days. She currently hikes 125 miles twice a day, which totals 250 miles per day.\n\nFirst, we calculate how many days she needs to complete 9,300 miles at her current pace:\n\\[\n\\frac{9,300 \\text{ miles}}{250 \\text{ miles/day}} = 37.2 \\text{ days}\n\\]\nSince June only has 30 days, she needs to increase her daily mileage to complete the journey on time.\n\nNext, we determine the required daily mileage to cover 9,300 miles in 30 days:\n\\[\n\\frac{9,300 \\text{ miles}}{30 \\text{ days}} = 310 \\text{ miles/day}\n\\]\nShe currently hikes 250 miles per day, so the additional miles she needs to hike each day are:\n\\[\n310 \\text{ miles/day} - 250 \\text{ miles/day} = 60 \\text{ miles/day}\n\\]\n\nThus, Soledad must hike an additional \\boxed{60} miles per day.", "pred_ans": "60", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Although Soledad works in a windowless office, she loves the outdoors. She will be on vacation for the entire month of June and cannot wait to hike 9,300 miles within that month. She is thinking of walking twice a day, covering 125 miles each time. How many more miles per day must Soledad hike to complete her journey on time?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
