{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: John had a son James when he was 19.  James is now twice as old as his sister Dora, who will turn 12 in 3 years.  How old will John's youngest son, who was born when John was 32, be in 3 years?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "8", "rationale": "Okay, let me try to figure out this problem. It's a bit complicated, but I'll take it step by step.\n\nSo, the problem goes like this: John had a son named James when he was 19. James is now twice as old as his sister Dora, who will turn 12 in 3 years. How old will John's youngest son, who was born when John was 32, be in 3 years?\n\nAlright, let's break this down. First, I need to find out how old James is now, then find out how old John is now, and then figure out how old John's youngest son will be in 3 years.\n\nLet me write down the information given:\n\n1. John had a son, James, when John was 19. So, James's age is related to John's age at that time.\n2. James is now twice as old as his sister Dora. So, James's current age is 2 times Dora's current age.\n3. Dora will turn 12 in 3 years. So, in 3 years, Dora will be 12. That means Dora is currently 12 - 3 = 9 years old.\n4. John's youngest son was born when John was 32. So, the son's current age is 2024 - 32 - (the year he was born). Hmm, actually, maybe I can figure this out differently.\n\nWait, actually, let's think in terms of variables. Maybe that will make it clearer.\n\nLet me assign variables:\n\nLet me denote:\n\n- Let J = John's current age.\n- Let Jm = James's current age.\n- Let D = Dora's current age.\n- Let S = John's youngest son's current age.\n\nFrom the problem:\n\n1. John had James when he was 19. So, James's age is J - 19. Because when John was 19, he had James, so James's age now is John's current age minus 19. So, Jm = J - 19.\n\n2. James is now twice as old as his sister Dora. So, Jm = 2 * D.\n\n3. Dora will turn 12 in 3 years. So, in 3 years, Dora will be 12. Therefore, her current age is 12 - 3 = 9. So, D = 9.\n\n4. John's youngest son was born when John was 32. So, the son's birth year is the same as when John was 32. If we assume the current year is, say, 2024, then John was 32 in 2024 - 32 = 1992. So, the son was born in 1992. Therefore, the son's current age is 2024 - 1992 = 32 years old? Wait, that can't be. Because John was 19 when he had James, so John is older now. Wait, let me think again.\n\nWait, maybe I don't need to assume the current year. Maybe I can calculate it.\n\nLet me think. If John was 19 when he had James, and now he has a son who was born when he was 32. So, the son is younger than James. So, the son is the youngest.\n\nSo, the son was born when John was 32, so the son's age now is John's current age minus 32. So, S = J - 32.\n\nSo, let's write down the equations again:\n\n1. Jm = J - 19\n2. Jm = 2 * D\n3. D = 9\n4. S = J - 32\n\nSo, from equation 3, D = 9. Then from equation 2, Jm = 2 * 9 = 18. So, James is 18 years old now.\n\nFrom equation 1, Jm = J - 19. Since Jm is 18, that means 18 = J - 19. Therefore, solving for J, we get J = 18 + 19 = 37. So, John is 37 years old now.\n\nNow, John's youngest son was born when John was 32. So, the son's current age is J - 32 = 37 - 32 = 5. So, the son is 5 years old now.\n\nThe question asks how old the youngest son will be in 3 years. So, in 3 years, he'll be 5 + 3 = 8 years old.\n\nWait, let me double-check this because sometimes these age problems can be tricky with the timing.\n\nSo, let's go step by step again:\n\n- Dora is currently 9 because she'll be 12 in 3 years.\n- James is twice as old as Dora, so James is 18.\n- James is 18 now, and he was born when John was 19. So, John's current age is 18 + 19 = 37.\n- John's youngest son was born when John was 32, so the son's current age is 37 - 32 = 5.\n- In 3 years, the son will be 5 + 3 = 8.\n\nYes, that seems consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can think in terms of timelines.\