{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Kalinda is working on a 360 piece puzzle with her mom. Kalinda can normally add 4 pieces per minute. Her mom can typically place half as many pieces per minute as Kalinda.  How many hours will it take them to complete this puzzle?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "1", "rationale": "Okay, so Kalinda and her mom are working on this 360-piece puzzle. Hmm, let me think about how to approach this. I need to figure out how long it will take them to finish the puzzle together.\n\nFirst, let's see what each of them can do individually. Kalinda can add 4 pieces per minute. That seems straightforward. Her mom, on the other hand, can place half as many pieces per minute as Kalinda. So, if Kalinda does 4 pieces each minute, her mom does half of that. Let me calculate that.\n\nHalf of 4 is 2, so her mom can place 2 pieces per minute. Got that. So, individually, Kalinda is faster than her mom. Now, since they're working together, I should probably add their rates to find out how much they can do together per minute.\n\nWait, no, actually, in these kinds of problems, when two people are working together, you add their rates to find the combined rate. So, if Kalinda does 4 pieces per minute and her mom does 2 pieces per minute, together they can do 4 + 2 = 6 pieces per minute. That makes sense because they can both work on different parts of the puzzle simultaneously.\n\nNow, the puzzle has 360 pieces. So, if they're working together at 6 pieces per minute, how long will it take them to finish all 360 pieces? I think I need to use the formula: time = total work / rate.\n\nSo, plugging in the numbers, time = 360 pieces / 6 pieces per minute. Let me do that division. 360 divided by 6 is 60. So, that gives me 60 minutes.\n\nBut the question asks for how many hours it will take them. Since there are 60 minutes in an hour, I can convert minutes to hours by dividing by 60. So, 60