{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: There are four schools competing at a basketball tournament. Each school has sent a girls\u2019 basketball team and a boys\u2019 basketball team and each team has 5 players each. Each school has also sent a coach for each team. In total, how many people have all of the schools sent?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "48", "rationale": "Okay, so I have this problem here about four schools competing in a basketball tournament. Each school sent a girls' team and a boys' team, each with 5 players. They also sent a coach for each team. I need to figure out the total number of people all the schools sent together.\n\nHmm, let me break this down. First, each school has two teams: girls and boys. Each team has 5 players. So, for one school, that would be 5 girls plus 5 boys, right? That's 10 players in total per school.\n\nBut wait, the problem mentions a coach for each team. So, that means each school also sent a coach for the girls' team and a coach for the boys' team. That adds two more people per school.\n\nSo, if I consider one school, the total number of people they sent would be the players plus the coaches. That's 10 players + 2 coaches = 12 people per school.\n\nNow, since there are four schools competing, I need to find the total number of people across all four schools. So, I can multiply the number of people per school by the number of schools. That would be 12 people/school * 4 schools.\n\nLet me calculate that: 12 times 4 is 48. So, 48 people in total.\n\nWait, let me double-check to make sure I didn't miss anything. Each school has two teams with 5 players each, so 5 + 5 is 10 players. Plus two coaches, one for each team, so 10 + 2 is 12 per school. Four schools would be 12 * 4, which is indeed 48.\n\nIs there another way to approach this? Maybe by calculating the players and coaches separately. Let me try that.\n\nFor players: Each school has 5 girls 