{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Elise is learning to write and decides to keep re-writing the alphabet until she knows it. She writes it in full twice, writes half of it once, then re-writes everything she has already written. How many letters has Elise written in total?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "130", "rationale": "Okay, so I need to figure out how many letters Elise has written in total. Let me read the problem again and break it down step by step.\n\nElise is learning to write the alphabet. She keeps re-writing it until she knows it. The problem describes a specific process:\n\n1. She writes the alphabet in full twice.\n2. Then she writes half of it once.\n3. After that, she re-writes everything she has already written.\n\nI need to calculate the total number of letters she has written across all these steps.\n\nFirst, let's clarify how many letters are in the English alphabet. I know it's 26 letters from A to Z. So, each \"full\" writing of the alphabet is 26 letters.\n\nNow, let's go through each step one by one.\n\n**Step 1: Writes the alphabet in full twice.**\n\nOkay, so if the alphabet has 26 letters, writing it twice means she writes 26 letters the first time and then another 26 letters the second time. So, that's 26 + 26 letters so far.\n\nCalculating that: 26 * 2 = 52 letters.\n\nSo, after step 1, Elise has written 52 letters.\n\n**Step 2: Writes half of it once.**\n\nHmm, half of the alphabet would be 13 letters because half of 26 is 13. So, she writes 13 letters in this step.\n\nAdding that to the previous total: 52 + 13 = 65 letters.\n\nSo, after step 2, she has written 65 letters.\n\n**Step 3: Re-writes everything she has already written.**\n\nThis is a bit trickier. She is re-writing everything she has already written. So, that means she is writing the entire collection of letters she has written up to that point again.\n\nSo, how much is that? She has written 65 letters in total so far, so she is writing another 65 letters.\n\nAdding that: 65 + 65 = 130 letters.\n\nTherefore, after step 3, she has written a total of 130 letters.\n\nWait, let me just double-check to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nStep 1: 26 * 2 = 52 letters.\n\nStep 2: 13 letters added, total 52 + 13 = 65.\n\nStep 3: She re-writes everything she has written, so that's 65 letters again. So, 65 + 65 = 130 letters.\n\nYes, that seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can think of it as:\n\nTotal letters written in step 1: 52.\n\nThen, in step 2, she adds 13, so total is 65.\n\nThen, in step 3, she re-writes all of that, meaning she writes 65 again, so 65 + 65 = 130.\n\nYes, that makes sense.\n\nSo, the total number of letters Elise has written is 130.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe total number of letters Elise has written is \\boxed{130}.\n</think>\n\nElise is learning to write the alphabet and follows a specific process:\n\n1. She writes the alphabet in full twice. Since the alphabet has 26 letters, this is \\(26 \\times 2 = 52\\) letters.\n2. She then writes half of the alphabet once. Half of 26 is 13, so she writes 13 letters. This adds to the total: \\(52 + 13 = 65\\) letters.\n3. She re-writes everything she has already written. This means she writes all 65 letters again. Adding this to the total: \\(65 + 65 = 130\\) letters.\n\nThus, the total number of letters Elise has written is \\(\\boxed{130}\\).", "pred_ans": "130", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Elise is learning to write and decides to keep re-writing the alphabet until she knows it. She writes it in full twice, writes half of it once, then re-writes everything she has already written. How many letters has Elise written in total?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: A shop sells school supplies. One notebook is sold at $1.50 each, a pen at $0.25 each, a calculator at $12 each, and a geometry set at $10. Daniel is an engineering student, and he wants to buy five notebooks, two pens, one calculator, and one geometry set. The shop gives a 10% discount on all the purchased items. How much does Daniel have to spend on all the items he wants to buy?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "27", "rationale": "Daniel wants to buy the following items:\n- 5 notebooks at $1.50 each\n- 2 pens at $0.25 each\n- 1 calculator at $12\n- 1 geometry set at $10\n\nFirst, calculate the total cost before any discounts:\n- Notebooks: 5 \u00d7 $1.50 = $7.50\n- Pens: 2 \u00d7 $0.25 = $0.50\n- Calculator: $12\n- Geometry set: $10\n\nAdding these amounts together gives:\n$7.50 + $0.50 + $12 + $10 = $30\n\nSince the shop offers a 10% discount on all items, calculate the discount amount:\n10% of $30 = 0.10 \u00d7 $30 = $3\n\nSubtract the discount from the total cost to find the final amount Daniel has to pay:\n$30 - $3 = $27\n\nTherefore, Daniel has to spend a total of $27 on all the items he wants to buy.\n</think>\n\nLet's solve the problem step by step.\n\n**Given:**\n- **Notebooks:** \\$1.50 each\n- **Pens:** \\$0.25 each\n- **Calculator:** \\$12\n- **Geometry Set:** \\$10\n- **Discount:** 10% on all items\n- **Items to buy:**\n  - 5 notebooks\n  - 2 pens\n  - 1 calculator\n  - 1 geometry set\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the cost of each item before discount.**\n\n1. **Notebooks:**\n   \\[\n   5 \\text{ notebooks} \\times \\$1.50/\\text{notebook} = 5 \\times 1.50 = \\$7.50\n   \\]\n\n2. **Pens:**\n   \\[\n   2 \\text{ pens} \\times \\$0.25/\\text{pen} = 2 \\times 0.25 = \\$0.50\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculator:**\n   \\[\n   1 \\text{ calculator} \\times \\$12/\\text{calculator} = \\$12\n   \\]\n\n4. **Geometry Set:**\n   \\[\n   1 \\text{ geometry set} \\times \\$10/\\text{geometry set} = \\$10\n   \\]\n\n**Step 2: Sum the costs before discount.**\n\\[\n\\$7.50 + \\$0.50 + \\$12 + \\$10 = \\$30\n\\]\n\n**Step 3: Calculate the discount amount.**\n\\[\n10\\% \\text{ of } \\$30 = 0.10 \\times 30 = \\$3\n\\]\n\n**Step 4: Subtract the discount to find the final amount to pay.**\n\\[\n\\$30 - \\$3 = \\$27\n\\]\n\n**Final Answer:**\nDaniel has to spend a total of \\(\\boxed{27}\\) dollars on all the items he wants to buy.", "pred_ans": "27", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: A shop sells school supplies. One notebook is sold at $1.50 each, a pen at $0.25 each, a calculator at $12 each, and a geometry set at $10. Daniel is an engineering student, and he wants to buy five notebooks, two pens, one calculator, and one geometry set. The shop gives a 10% discount on all the purchased items. How much does Daniel have to spend on all the items he wants to buy?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
