{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Colby wants to buy some gumballs that cost a nickel each. If he has 8 quarters, 6 dimes, 14 nickels, and 15 pennies, how many can he buy?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "69", "rationale": "Okay, so Colby wants to buy some gumballs that cost a nickel each. Hmm, nickels are 5 cents, right? So each gumball costs 5 cents. Colby has different coins: 8 quarters, 6 dimes, 14 nickels, and 15 pennies. I need to figure out how many gumballs he can buy with all those coins.\n\nFirst, I should probably figure out how much money Colby has in total. That way, I can see how much he can spend on gumballs. To do that, I need to convert all his coins into cents because the gumballs cost 5 cents each.\n\nStarting with quarters. Each quarter is worth 25 cents, and he has 8 of them. So, 8 quarters would be 8 times 25 cents. Let me calculate that: 8 * 25 = 200 cents. That's $2, but since everything is in cents, I can just keep it as 200 cents.\n\nNext, dimes. Each dime is 10 cents, and he has 6 of them. So, 6 dimes would be 6 * 10 = 60 cents.\n\nThen, nickels. Each nickel is 5 cents, and he has 14. So, 14 nickels would be 14 * 5. Let me compute that: 14 * 5 = 70 cents.\n\nLastly, pennies. He has 15 pennies, each worth 1 cent. So, 15 * 1 = 15 cents.\n\nNow, adding up all these amounts: quarters (200) + dimes (60) + nickels (70) + pennies (15). Let's see: 200 + 60 is 260. Then, 260 + 70 is 330. Finally, 330 + 15 is 345 cents. So, Colby has a total of 345 cents.\n\nSince each gumball is 5 cents, I need to figure out how many 5-cent pieces are in 345 cents. To do that, I can divide the total cents by 5. So, 345 \u00f7 5. Let me compute that: 345 \u00f7 5.\n\nWell, 5 goes into 34 six times (5*6=30), subtract 30 from 34, we get 4. Bring down the 5, making it 45. 5 goes into 45 nine times. So, 69. So, 69 gumballs.\n\nWait a second, let me double-check that to make sure I didn't make a