{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Eliza's rate per hour for the first 40 hours she works each week is $10. She also receives an overtime pay of 1.2 times her regular hourly rate. If Eliza worked for 45 hours this week, how much are her earnings for this week?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "460", "rationale": "Okay, so I need to figure out how much Eliza earned this week. Let me take it step by step. \n\nFirst, the problem says that Eliza's rate per hour for the first 40 hours she works each week is $10. Hmm, so her regular hourly rate is $10. That seems straightforward. \n\nNow, she also gets overtime pay, which is 1.2 times her regular hourly rate. Hmm, so the overtime rate isn't double or anything; it's 1.2 times her regular pay. That means for every hour she works overtime, she gets 1.2 times $10, right? Let me calculate that. \n\n1.2 times $10 is... let me do that multiplication. 1 times 10 is 10, and 0.2 times 10 is 2, so together that's $12 per hour for overtime. Got it. So her regular pay is $10, and overtime is $12 per hour.\n\nNow, the question is, how many hours did she work this week? It says she worked 45 hours. So I need to figure out how much of that is regular hours and how much is overtime.\n\nSince she works 40 hours at regular pay and the rest would be overtime, right? So 45 hours minus 40 hours gives me 5 hours of overtime. Let me write that down:\n\nRegular hours = 40 hours  \nOvertime hours = 45 - 40 = 5 hours\n\nOkay, so for her regular pay, she earns $10 per hour for 40 hours. Let me calculate that. 40 times 10 is 400. So that part is $400.\n\nNow, for the overtime pay, she gets $12 per hour for 5 hours. So I need to multiply 12 by 5. 12 times 5 is 60. So her overtime pay is $60.\n\nTo find the total earnings, I just add the regular pay and the overtime pay together. So $400 plus $60 is $460. \n\nWait, let me double-check to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Regular pay: 40 hours at $10 is indeed $400. Overtime: 5 hours at $12 is $60. Adding them gives $460. Yeah, that seems right.\n\nI was a bit confused at first because sometimes overtime can be calculated differently, but in this case, it's clearly stated as 1.2 times her regular rate. So I think I did it correctly.\n\nSo, summarizing:\n- Regular pay: 40 hours * $10 = $400\n- Overtime pay: 5 hours * 1.2 * $10 = 5 * $12 = $60\n- Total earnings: $400 + $60 = $460\n\nI think that's all there is to it. I can't see any errors in my calculations, so I feel confident that $460 is the correct amount she earned this week.\n\n**Final Answer**\nEliza's total earnings for the week are \\boxed{460} dollars.\n</think>\n\nEliza's regular hourly rate is $10. She works 40 regular hours and 5 overtime hours. \n\n1. Regular pay:\n   \\[\n   40 \\text{ hours} \\times 10 \\text{ dollars/hour} = 400 \\text{ dollars}\n   \\]\n\n2. Overtime pay:\n   \\[\n   5 \\text{ hours} \\times 1.2 \\times 10 \\text{ dollars/hour} = 5 \\times 12 \\text{ dollars/hour} = 60 \\text{ dollars}\n   \\]\n\n3. Total earnings:\n   \\[\n   400 \\text{ dollars} + 60 \\text{ dollars} = 460 \\text{ dollars}\n   \\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{460}\n\\]", "pred_ans": "460", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Eliza's rate per hour for the first 40 hours she works each week is $10. She also receives an overtime pay of 1.2 times her regular hourly rate. If Eliza worked for 45 hours this week, how much are her earnings for this week?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Doctor Jones is scheduling his time for Monday. He is spending nine hours at the clinic where he works that day. He has to do rounds to check on inpatients staying at the clinic, which takes twenty minutes per inpatient, and he has ten appointments, which take thirty minutes each. How many hours will Doctor Jones have left to update his records if he has 9 inpatients at the clinic?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "1", "rationale": "Alright, so I need to solve this problem about Doctor Jones's schedule. Let me read it again and break it down step by step.\n\nDoctor Jones is at the clinic on Monday for nine hours. He has two main tasks: doing rounds for inpatients and seeing appointments. \n\nFirst, the rounds. Each inpatient check takes twenty minutes, and he has ten inpatients. Hmm, so I need to calculate the total time he spends on rounds. Since each inpatient takes 20 minutes, for ten inpatients, that would be 10 times 20 minutes. Let me write that down: 10 * 20 = 200 minutes. \n\nOkay, now the appointments. He has ten appointments, each taking thirty minutes. So, that's 10 * 30 minutes. Let me compute that: 10 * 30 = 300 minutes. \n\nSo, the total time he spends on rounds and appointments is 200 minutes + 300 minutes. That adds up to 500 minutes. \n\nBut wait, his total time at the clinic is nine hours. I need to make sure all times are in the same unit, so let me convert hours to minutes since the other tasks are in minutes. One hour is 60 minutes, so nine hours would be 9 * 60 = 540 minutes. \n\nAlright, so he has 540 minutes in total, and he spends 500 minutes on rounds and appointments. Therefore, the time left for updating his records would be 540 - 500 = 40 minutes. \n\nBut the question asks for how many hours he will have left. Since 40 minutes is less than an hour, I don't need to convert it. Wait, 40 minutes is actually 2/3 of an hour because 40 divided by 60 is 2/3. So, 2/3 of an hour is the time he has left. \n\nLet me just recap to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Ten inpatients at 20 minutes each: 10*20=200. Ten appointments at 30 minutes each: 10*30=300. Total time on these tasks: 200+300=500 minutes. Total time available: 9 hours = 540 minutes. Time left: 540-500=40 minutes, which is 2/3 of an hour. \n\nIs there another way to approach this? Maybe by converting everything to hours from the beginning. Let me try that to cross-verify.\n\nRounds: 10 inpatients at 20 minutes each. 20 minutes is 1/3 of an hour. So, 10*(1/3) = 10/3 hours \u2248 3.333 hours.\n\nAppointments: 10 appointments at 30 minutes each. 30 minutes is 0.5 hours. So, 10*0.5 = 5 hours.\n\nTotal time on these tasks: 10/3 + 5 = 10/3 + 15/3 = 25/3 hours \u2248 8.333 hours.\n\nTotal time available: 9 hours.\n\nTime left: 9 - 25/3 = (27/3 - 25/3) = 2/3 hours.\n\nHmm, same result. So, 2/3 of an hour is 40 minutes, which matches my earlier calculation. So, I think that's correct.\n\nI guess another way to think about it is to calculate the total minutes spent and subtract from the total minutes available. Both methods lead to the same conclusion, so I feel confident that 2/3 of an hour is the right answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nDoctor Jones will have \\boxed{\\dfrac{2}{3}} of an hour left to update his records.\n</think>\n\nDoctor Jones is spending nine hours at the clinic. He needs to do rounds for ten inpatients, each taking twenty minutes, and has ten appointments, each taking thirty minutes.\n\nFirst, calculate the total time spent on rounds:\n\\[ 10 \\text{ inpatients} \\times 20 \\text{ minutes per inpatient} = 200 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nNext, calculate the total time spent on appointments:\n\\[ 10 \\text{ appointments} \\times 30 \\text{ minutes per appointment} = 300 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nAdd the times together:\n\\[ 200 \\text{ minutes} + 300 \\text{ minutes} = 500 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nConvert nine hours to minutes:\n\\[ 9 \\text{ hours} \\times 60 \\text{ minutes per hour} = 540 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nSubtract the time spent on rounds and appointments from the total time available:\n\\[ 540 \\text{ minutes} - 500 \\text{ minutes} = 40 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nConvert 40 minutes back to hours:\n\\[ 40 \\text{ minutes} = \\frac{40}{60} \\text { hours} = \\frac{2}{3} \\text{ hours} \\]\n\nThus, Doctor Jones will have \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{2}{3}}\\) of an hour left to update his records.", "pred_ans": "3", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Doctor Jones is scheduling his time for Monday. He is spending nine hours at the clinic where he works that day. He has to do rounds to check on inpatients staying at the clinic, which takes twenty minutes per inpatient, and he has ten appointments, which take thirty minutes each. How many hours will Doctor Jones have left to update his records if he has 9 inpatients at the clinic?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Mariah\u2019s grandma was teaching her to knit. Mariah used 1/4 of a skein of yarn. Her grandma used 1/2 of a skein of yarn. There are 364 yards in a skein of yarn. How many yards of yarn did they use altogether?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "273", "rationale": "Okay, so Mariah and her grandma are knitting together, right? Mariah used 1/4 of a skein, and her grandma used 1/2 of a skein. Each skein has 364 yards of yarn. I need to figure out how many yards they used in total. Hmm, let me break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, I think I should find out how many yards each of them used individually and then add them together. That makes sense because together they used both their portions. So, let me start with Mariah. She used 1/4 of a skein. Since one skein is 364 yards, 1/4 of that would be... uh, how do I calculate that? Well, 1 divided by 4 is 0.25, right? So, 0.25 times 364 yards. Let me do that multiplication. 364 times 0.25. Hmm, 364 divided by 4 is the same as 364 times 0.25. 4 goes into 36 nine times, with a remainder of 0, and 4 goes into 4 once. So, 9 times 100 is 900, 1 times 100 is 100, and 9 times 1 is 9. Wait, no, that's not right. Maybe I should think of it as 364 divided by 4. Let's see, 4 into 36 is 9, which makes 90. 4 into 4 is 1, so that's 91. So, 364 divided by 4 is 91. So, 91 yards. Okay, so Mariah used 91 yards.\n\nNow, her grandma used 1/2 of a skein. So, half of 364 yards. Well, 364 divided by 2 is 182. So, her grandma used 182 yards.\n\nNow, to find out the total yards they used together, I just need to add Mariah's 91 yards and her grandma's 182 yards. Let's do that: 91 plus 182. Hmm, 90 plus 180 is 270, and 1 plus 2 is 3, so total is 273. So, together, they used 273 yards of yarn.\n\nWait, let me double-check my calculations to make sure I didn't make any mistakes. So, Mariah used 1/4 of 364. 364 divided by 4 is 91. Yep, that'