erefore, to find out how many stickers Leo has now, I need to add the stickers from last year and this year to the number he had two years ago.\n\nSo, two years ago, he had 100. Last year, he added 50. This year, he adds another 100. So, total stickers now would be 100 + 50 + 100.\n\nWait, is that correct? Let me make sure. So, two years ago, he had 100. Then, last year, he collected 50, so he must have had 100 + 50 = 150 stickers at the end of last year. Then, this year, he collected twice as many as last year, which was 50, so he collected 100 this year. So, adding that to his collection, he now has 150 + 100 = 250 stickers.\n\nHmm, okay, so that seems right. But let me see if there's another way to represent this with equations to make sure.\n\nLet me assign variables to each of these time points. Let \\( x \\) represent the number of stickers Leo has this year. Then, last year, he had \\( x - y \\) stickers, but wait, maybe that complicates things.\n\nAlternatively, I can think in terms of years. Let's say two years ago, he had 100 stickers. Last year, which is one year ago, he had 100 + 50 = 150 stickers. This year, he has 150 + 100 = 250 stickers.\n\nWait, that's essentially the same as before, but just phrased differently. So, maybe that's a more straightforward way to think about it.\n\nAlternatively, I can use algebra. Let me define:\n\nLet \\( t \\) be the number of stickers Leo has this year.\n\nThen, last year, he had \\( t - \\) the number he collected this year? Wait, no, because he collects stickers each year.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to model the progression step by step.\n\nTwo years ago, Leo had 100 stickers.\n\nLast year, he added 50 stickers, so he had 100 + 50 = 150 stickers.\n\nThis year, he added twice the number he added last year, which was 50. So, he added 2 * 50 = 100 stickers this year.\n\nTherefore, his current collection is 150 + 100 = 250 stickers.\n\nSo, putting it all together:\n\n- Two years ago: 100 stickers\n- Last year: +50 stickers = 150 stickers\n- This year: +100 stickers = 250 stickers\n\nTherefore, Leo has 250 stickers in his collection now.\n\nWait, let me make sure I didn't skip any steps or make a mistake in the timeline.\n\nTwo years ago: 100 stickers.\n\nEach subsequent year, he adds some number of stickers.\n\nSo, last year, he added 50, so he had 150.\n\nThis year, he added twice as many as last year, so 100, bringing the total to 250.\n\nYes, that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, I can represent this with equations.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet \\( C \\) be the current number of stickers Leo has.\n\nTwo years ago, he had 100 stickers. So, two years ago, \\( C - 2 \\times \\text{annual addition} = 100 \\).\n\nLast year, he had \\( C - 1 \\times \\text{annual addition} \\). And last year, he collected 50 stickers, so \\( C - 1 \\times \\text{annual addition} + 50 = C - \\text{annual addition} \\).\n\nWait, this might be getting a bit confusing.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should think about the number of stickers added each year.\n\nLet me define \\( a \\) as the number of stickers Leo collected last year. Then, this year, he collected \\( 2a \\).\n\nGiven that two years ago, he had 100 stickers. Last year, he had 100 + a stickers. Last year, he collected a stickers, so at the end of last year, he had 100 + a.\n\nThen, this year, he collected 2a stickers, so now he has 100 + a + 2a = 100 + 3a.\n\nBut according to the problem, last year, he collected 50 stickers. So, \\( a = 50 \\).\n\nTherefore, this year, he has 100 + 3*50 = 100 + 150 = 250 stickers.\n\nYes, that matches my earlier result.\n\nSo, both methods give me 250 stickers. \n\nWait, just to double-check, another way. Let me think about it as a timeline.\n\nAt time t = -2: 100 stickers.\n\nFrom t = -2 to t = -1: he collects 50 stickers, so at t = -1, he has 150.\n\nFrom t = -1 to t = 0: he collects twice as much as last year, which was 50, so he collects 100 this year, ending up at 250.\n\nYes, that's consistent.\n\nSo, I think 250 is the correct answer.\n\nJust to make sure, let me try plugging in numbers another way.\n\nSuppose Leo's collection increases by a certain amount each year. But in this case, the problem says last year he collected 50, and this year he collected twice that. So, the number he collected each year isn't a fixed annual addition, but rather dependent on the previous year's collection.\n\nSo, the only way I can think of is to model the problem step by step as I did before.\n\nTherefore, Leo has 250 stickers now.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{250}\n</think>\n\nTwo years ago, Leo had 100 stickers. Last year, he collected 50 stickers. This year, he collected twice the number of stickers as last year.\n\n1. Two years ago, Leo had 100 stickers.\n2. Last year, he collected 50 stickers, so he had 100 + 50 = 150 stickers at the end of last year.\n3. This year, he collected twice the number of stickers as last year, which is 2 * 50 = 100 stickers.\n4. Adding this year's collection to last year's total, Leo now has 150 + 100 = 250 stickers.\n\nThus, the number of stickers Leo has in his collection is \\(\\boxed{250}\\).", "pred_ans": "250", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Leo collects stickers. Two years ago, he had 100 stickers in his collection. Last year, Leo collected 50 stickers. This year, he collected twice the number of stickers as the previous year. How many stickers does Leo have in his collection?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
