 = 38\n\nTotal: 6 + 12 + 38 = 56.\n\nYes, that seems correct.\n\nI don't think I missed anything here. The problem is straightforward once you parse the numbers correctly. It's important to note that the volleyball players are twice the number of tennis players, so that's 12. Then, the soccer players are 16 boys and 22 girls, so that's 38. Adding them all gives the total number of students.\n\nSo, I think I'm confident with 56 as the answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe total number of students is \\boxed{56}.\n</think>\n\nFirst, we determine the number of students playing each sport. There are 6 students playing tennis, so twice that number means 12 students are playing volleyball. \n\nNext, we know there are 16 boys and 22 girls playing soccer. Adding these together gives the total number of soccer players:\n\\[ 16 + 22 = 38 \\]\n\nWe then add the number of students from each sport together to find the total number of students:\n\\[ 6 \\text{ (tennis)} + 12 \\text{ (volleyball)} + 38 \\text{ (soccer)} = 56 \\]\n\nThus, the total number of students is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{56}\n\\]", "pred_ans": "56", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: There are 6 students playing tennis and twice that number playing volleyball. There are 16 boys and 22 girls playing soccer. If each student only participates in one group, how many students are there in total?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: A salesman bought a case of 48 sneakers for $576. He sold 17 of them for $20 at a flash sale on Monday, and the rest were sold to a department store for $25 each throughout the rest of the week. How much money, in dollars, was the salesman's profit?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "539", "rationale": "To determine the salesman's profit, I'll start by calculating the total cost he incurred for the 48 sneakers. The cost per sneaker is $12, so multiplying that by 48 gives the total cost.\n\nNext, I'll calculate the revenue from selling 17 sneakers at the flash sale. Each sneaker was sold for $20, so multiplying 17 by $20 will give the total revenue from the sale.\n\nThen, I'll calculate the revenue from selling the remaining 31 sneakers to the department store. Each of these was sold for $25, so multiplying 31 by $25 will provide the total revenue from those sales.\n\nFinally, to find the profit, I'll subtract the total cost from the total revenue. This will give the amount of money the salesman made after covering all his expenses.\n</think>\n\nTo determine the salesman's **profit**, we'll follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the Total Cost:**\n   \n   - **Cost per sneaker:** \\$12\n   - **Total sneakers bought:** 48\n   - **Total Cost = Cost per sneaker \u00d7 Number of sneakers**\n   \n   \\[\n   \\text{Total Cost} = 12 \\times 48 = \\$576\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the Revenue from the Flash Sale:**\n   \n   - **Number of sneakers sold on Monday:** 17\n   - **Price per sneaker on Monday:** \\$20\n   - **Revenue from Monday = Number of sneakers sold \u00d7 Price per sneaker**\n   \n   \\[\n   \\text{Revenue from Monday} = 17 \\times 20 = \\$340\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the Revenue from Sales to the Department Store:**\n   \n   - **Number of sneakers sold to the department store:** \\(48 - 17 = 31\\)\n   - **Price per sneaker to the department store:** \\$25\n   - **Revenue from Department Store = Number of sneakers sold \u00d7 Price per sneaker**\n   \n   \\[\n   \\text{Revenue from Department Store} = 31 \\times 25 = \\$775\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate Total Revenue:**\n   \n   \\[\n   \\text{Total Revenue} = \\text{Revenue from Flash Sale} + \\text{Revenue from Department Store} = \\$340 + \\$775 = \\$1,\\!115\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate Profit:**\n   \n   \\[\n   \\text{Profit} = \\text{Total Revenue} - \\text{Total Cost} = \\$1,\\!115 - \\$576 = \\$539\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\nThe salesman's profit is \\boxed{539} dollars.", "pred_ans": "539", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: A salesman bought a case of 48 sneakers for $576. He sold 17 of them for $20 at a flash sale on Monday, and the rest were sold to a department store for $25 each throughout the rest of the week. How much money, in dollars, was the salesman's profit?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Derrick has a bakery that makes 10 dozen doughnuts every day, selling at $2 per doughnut. How much money does Derrick make in June if he sells all the doughnuts?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "7200", "rationale": "To determine how much money Derrick makes in June, I'll start by calculating the total number of doughnuts he produces each day. He bakes 10 dozen doughnuts, and since one dozen equals 12, that means he makes 10 \u00d7 12 = 120 doughnuts per day.\n\nNext, I'll find out the revenue from selling these doughnuts. Each doughnut is sold for \\$2, so the revenue from one day is 120 doughnuts \u00d7 \\$2 per doughnut = \\$240 per day.\n\nNow, to find the total revenue for June, I'll multiply the daily revenue by the number of days in June. There are 30 days in June, so the total revenue is \\$240/day \u00d7 30 days = \\$7200.\n\nTherefore, Derrick makes a total of \\$7200 in June.\n</think>\n\n**Solution:**\n\n1. **Calculate the number of doughnuts Derrick bakes each day:**\n   \n   \\[\n   10 \\text{ dozen doughnuts/day} \\times 12 \\text{ doughnuts/dozen} = 120 \\text{ doughnuts/day}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the revenue from selling one dozen doughnuts:**\n   \n   \\[\n   120 \\text{ doughnuts} \\times \\$2 \\text{ per doughnut} = \\$240 \\text{ per day}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the total revenue for June:**\n   \n   \\[\n   \\$240 \\text{ per day} \\times 30 \\text{ days} = \\$7,\\!200\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\$7,\\!200}\n\\]", "pred_ans": "7,200", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Derrick has a bakery that makes 10 dozen doughnuts every day, selling at $2 per doughnut. How much money does Derrick make in June if he sells all the doughnuts?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
