ong as he is tall, so we calculate the length of Carl's cane as follows:\n\\[ \\text{Cane's length} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3 \\text{ feet} \\]\n\nThe length of Carl's cane is \\(\\boxed{3}\\) feet.", "pred_ans": "3", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Carl has a cane that is half as long as he is tall.  Carl is one foot taller than his brother, Ned.  And Ned is two feet shorter than his cousin, Isabel.  If Isabel is 7 feet tall, how long is Carl's cane, in feet?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Sara wants to buy herself a new jacket and 2 pairs of shoes. The jacket she wants costs $30 and each pair of shoes cost $20. Sara babysits the neighbor's kids 4 times, earning $5 each time she babysits them. Her parents pay her $4 each time she mows the lawn. If Sara already had $10 saved before she started babysitting, how many times must she mow the lawn before she can afford the jacket and shoes?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "10", "rationale": "Alright, let's try to figure out how many times Sara needs to mow the lawn to afford her new jacket and shoes. Okay, so she wants a jacket that costs $30 and two pairs of shoes, each costing $20. Hmm, I should start by calculating the total cost of everything she wants to buy.\n\nFirst, the jacket is $30. Then, each pair of shoes is $20, and she wants two pairs. So, let me add those up. 20 times 2 is 40. So, the shoes will cost her $40. Now, if I add that to the jacket, 30 plus 40 is 70. So, altogether, she needs $70.\n\nWait, let me double-check that. Jacket is $30, two pairs of shoes at $20 each. 20 times 2 is 40. 30 plus 40 is indeed 70. Okay, so she needs $70 in total.\n\nNow, she earns money by babysitting her neighbors' kids. She does this 4 times, and each time she earns $5. So, let me figure out how much she makes from babysitting. Each time she gets $5, and she does it 4 times. So, 5 times 4 is 20. She earns $20 from babysitting.\n\nShe also gets paid for mowing the lawn. Her parents pay her $4 each time she mows the lawn. So, that's another way she can earn money.\n\nBefore she started all this, Sara already had $10 saved up. So, she's starting with $10.\n\nOkay, so let's summarize her income and expenses.\n\nFirst, her total expense is $70 for the jacket and shoes.\n\nHer total savings initially: $10.\n\nShe earns from babysitting: $20.\n\nShe can also earn more by mowing the lawn, $4 per lawn.\n\nWe need to find out how many times she needs to mow the lawn so that her total savings (including the $10 she already has) plus her earnings from mowing the lawn will cover the $70 she needs.\n\nLet me represent the number of times she mows the lawn as 'x'. So, each time she mows, she earns $4, so for x times, she'll earn 4x dollars.\n\nTherefore, her total savings will be her initial $10 plus her earnings from babysitting ($20) plus her earnings from mowing the lawn (4x). So, that's 10 + 20 + 4x.\n\nWe want this total to be at least $70 because that's how much she needs to buy the jacket and shoes.\n\nSo, let me write the equation:\n\n10 (initial savings) + 20 (from babysitting) + 4x (from mowing) \u2265 70 (total cost)\n\nLet me compute the left side without 4x first: 10 + 20 is 30.\n\nSo, 30 + 4x \u2265 70.\n\nNow, we can subtract 30 from both sides to solve for x:\n\n4x \u2265 70 - 30\n\n4x \u2265 40\n\nNow, divide both sides by 4:\n\nx \u2265 40 / 4\n\nx \u2265 10\n\nSo, Sara needs to mow the lawn at least 10 times.\n\nWait, let me check if that's correct. If she mows 10 times, she'll earn 10 times $4, which is $40. Then, adding her initial $10 and the $20 from babysitting, that's 10 + 20 + 40, which is 70. That's exactly the amount she needs. So, 10 times is the minimum number needed.\n\nBut let me think again. If she only mows 9 times, she'll earn 9 * 4 = 36 dollars. Then, her total would be 10 + 20 + 36 = 66. 66 is less than 70, so she can't afford the jacket and shoes yet. So, she needs to mow 10 times.\n\nIs there another way to think about this? Maybe by considering each expense and income separately.\n\nShe needs $70. She already has $10. So, she needs an additional $60.\n\nShe earns $20 from babysitting. So, after babysitting, she has 10 + 20 = $30. Then, she still needs 70 - 30 = $40.\n\nEach time she mows, she makes $4. So, how many times does she need to mow to make $40?\n\nThat's 40 divided by 4, which is 10. So again, she needs to mow 10 times.\n\nHm