{"question": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: A bakery produces 60 loaves of bread each day. Two-thirds of the loaves are sold in the morning and half of what is left is sold equally in the afternoon and evening. How many loaves of bread are sold in the afternoon?\n Solution:\n<think>\n", "gold_ans": "10", "rationale": "Okay, so I have this math problem here about a bakery producing loaves of bread. Let me try to figure it out step by step. \n\nFirst, the bakery makes 60 loaves each day. That's straightforward. Now, two-thirds of these are sold in the morning. Hmm, okay, so I need to find out how many loaves are sold in the morning. Let me calculate that.\n\nTwo-thirds of 60... Hmm, how do I do that? Well, I can divide 60 by 3 first, which is 20, and then multiply by 2 because it's two-thirds. So 20 times 2 is 40. So, 40 loaves are sold in the morning. Got that.\n\nNow, the problem says half of what's left is sold equally in the afternoon and evening. Wait, so after selling 40 in the morning, how many are left? Let me subtract 40 from 60. That's 20. So, 20 loaves are left.\n\nNow, half of these 20 are sold in the afternoon and the other half in the evening. So, half of 20 is 10. Which means 10 loaves are sold in the afternoon and 10 in the evening. \n\nSo, the question is asking how many are sold in the afternoon. From my calculation, that's 10. \n\nLet me just recap to make sure I didn't make any mistakes. 60 loaves total. Morning sales: 2/3 of 60 is 40. That leaves 20 for the rest of the day. Then, half of 20 is 10, so 10 in the afternoon and 10 in the evening. Yep, that seems right.\n\nI don't think I made any calculation errors here. It's pretty straightforward once you break it down into parts. So, yeah, 10 loaves sold in the afternoon.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of loaves sold in the afternoon is \\boxed{10}.\n</think>\n\nThe bakery produces 60 loaves of bread each day. Two-thirds of these are sold in the morning:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2}{3} \\times 60 = 40\n\\]\n\nThis leaves 20 loaves for the afternoon and evening. Half of these 20 loaves are sold in the afternoon:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} \\times 20 = 10\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of loaves sold in the afternoon is \\boxed{10}.", "pred_ans": "10", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: A bakery produces 60 loaves of bread each day. Two-thirds of the loaves are sold in the morning and half of what is left is sold equally in the afternoon and evening. How many loaves of bread are sold in the afternoon?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
