398 toys and he needs 2 more, that brings him to 400, which is five times James's 80. 5 \u00d7 80 is indeed 400. So, yes, that seems correct.\n\nI don't think I made any mistakes in my calculations. It seems straightforward. So, the answer should be 398 toys.\n\n**Final Answer**\nJonathan currently has \\boxed{398} toys.\n</think>\n\nJonathan wants to have enough toys to make a play set five times larger than James's play set, which had 80 toys. To find out how many toys Jonathan needs, we calculate five times the number of toys James has:\n\n\\[ 5 \\times 80 = 400 \\]\n\nJonathan is 2 toys short of having 400 toys. Therefore, we can find the number of toys Jonathan currently has by subtracting 2 from 400:\n\n\\[ 400 - 2 = 398 \\]\n\nThus, Jonathan currently has \\boxed{398} toys.", "pred_ans": "398", "wrap_que": "Solve this math problem step by step. You MUST put your final answer in \\boxed{}. Problem: Jonathan was sad to learn he needed 2 more toys than he already had to have enough toys to make a sorted play set five times larger than James's play set, which had 80 toys. How many toys does Jonathan currently have?\n Solution:\n<think>\n"}
